$waves42_48_payload$[
  {
    "wave": 42,
    "plan_id": "90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000242",
    "title": "Thoughts for the Quiet Hour",
    "description": "An editorially reviewed anthology of 346 quiet-hour selections drawn from multiple Christian writers. Historical dates and attributions are preserved where appropriate, while identified theological, pastoral, and source-boundary problems are replaced with clearly labeled Scripture-led reflections. Wave 42 completes the sentence-level release screening and corrects the Genesis 32 reference on day 313. Source: Project Gutenberg ebook 37292.",
    "source_url": "https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/37292",
    "total_days": 346,
    "days": [
      {
        "day_number": 1,
        "title": "Make Space for the Quiet Hour",
        "content": "<p><strong>Read: Exodus 34:2.</strong> Moses was called to meet God in the morning. A quiet hour begins with a deliberate turning of attention: step away from the noise, listen to Scripture, and speak honestly with God. The length of the time matters less than the willingness to return to him day by day.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> When and where can you make room today for prayer and Scripture? What distraction can you set aside for that time?</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Lord, make me attentive to your word and faithful in prayer. Let this time with you shape the way I live today. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Exodus 34:2"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 2,
        "title": "Come up in the morning . . . and present yourself . . . to me on top of the mountain. Ex. 34. 2.",
        "content": "<p>Come up in the morning . . . and present yourself . . . to me in the top of the mount. Ex. 34. 2.</p><p>My Father, I am coming. Nothing on the mean plain shall keep me away from the holy heights. Help me to climb fast, and keep You my foot, lest it fall upon the hard rock! At Your bidding I come, so You will not mock my heart. Bring with You honey from heaven, yes, milk and wine, and oil for my soul's good, and stay the sun in his course, or the time will be too short in which to look upon Your face, and to hear Your gentle voice.</p><p>Morning on the mount! It will make me strong and glad all the rest of the day so well begun.— Joseph Parker.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Exodus 34:2"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 3,
        "title": "My reward is with me. Rev. 22. 12.",
        "content": "<p>My reward is with me. Rev. 22. 12.</p><p><strong>Source and historical context:</strong> Andrew Bonar speculates about rewards for sufferers who do not murmur.</p><p><strong>Devotional focus:</strong> Revelation 22 presents Christ as the coming judge, but it does not rank people by how silently they endure suffering. Lament, protest against injustice, and requests for care appear throughout Scripture. God sees burdens that others overlook. Believers can hope in divine justice without telling a grieving person to suppress tears or stay in harm.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> Have I confused quiet endurance with the only faithful response to pain?</p><p><strong>Practice:</strong> Listen to someone carrying a burden and ask what practical support would help.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Just God, see those who suffer. Give us courage to seek help and to offer steady compassion.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Revelation 22:12"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 4,
        "title": "Where are you? Gen. 3. 9.",
        "content": "<p>Where are you? Gen. 3. 9.</p><p>Are you hiding yourself away from Him who would send you forth to do His own blessed work in His own way? Oh, let me say to you this morning, \"The Lord has need of you.\" It may seem to be only a little thing He has for you to do, but it is an important one. He has \"need of you.\" Turn not your back upon Him; put not yourself out of the way of being employed by Him; do not begin by laying down laws for yourself as to what you will do and what you will not do; but cry out from the very depth of your heart, \"Here am I, send me,\"— W. Hay Aitken.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Genesis 3:9"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 5,
        "title": "Many are the afflictions of the righteous, but the Lord delivereth him out of them all. Psa. 34. 19.",
        "content": "<p>Many are the afflictions of the righteous, but the Lord delivers him out of them all. Psa. 34. 19.</p><p>All the afflictions of the righteous open out into something glorious. The prisoner is not merely delivered, but he finds an angel waiting for him at the door. And with every deliverance comes a specific blessing. One angel is named faith; another, love; another, joy; another, longsuffering; another, gentleness; another, goodness; another, meekness; another, temperance; another, peace. Each of these graces says, \"We have come out of great tribulation.\"— G. Bowen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Psalms 34:19"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 6,
        "title": "The Lord is my . . . song. Psa. 118. 14.",
        "content": "<p>The Lord is my . . . song. Psa. 118. 14.</p><p>Let us think of God Himself becoming our song. This is the fulness and perfection of knowing God: so to know Him that He Himself becomes our delight; so to know Him that praise is sweetest, and fullest, and freshest, and gladdest, when we sing of Him. He who has learned this blessed secret carries the golden key of heaven—no, he has fetched heaven down to earth, and need not envy the angels now.— Mark Guy Pearse.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Psalms 118:14"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 7,
        "title": "Do not be afraid; stand still and see the Lord's salvation. Ex. 14. 13.",
        "content": "<p>Fear you not, stand still, and see the salvation of the Lord. Ex. 14. 13.</p><p>Often God seems to place His children in positions of profound difficulty—leading them into a wedge from which there is no escape; contriving a situation which no human judgment would have permitted, had it been previously consulted. The very cloud conducts them there. You may be thus involved at this very hour. It does seem perplexing and very serious to the last degree; but it is perfectly right. The issue will more than justify Him who has brought you here. It is a platform for the display of His almighty grace and power. He will not only deliver you, but in doing so He will give you a lesson that you will never forget; and to which, in many a psalm and song in after days, you will revert. You will never be able to thank God enough for having done just as He has.— F. B. Meyer.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Exodus 14:13"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 8,
        "title": "Now you are light in the Lord: walk as children of light. Eph. 5. 8.",
        "content": "<p>Now are you light in the Lord: walk as children of light. Eph. 5. 8.</p><p>The influence we exert in the world is created by our relationship to Jesus Christ; and our relationship to Jesus Christ is revealed by our influence.— Selected.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Ephesians 5:8"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 9,
        "title": "Take great care of your souls. Josh. 23. 11. ( Margin. )",
        "content": "<p>Take good heed therefore to your souls. Josh. 23. 11. ( Margin. )</p><p><strong>Source and historical context:</strong> A. J. Gordon compares Christian witness to refining gold into usable currency.</p><p><strong>Devotional focus:</strong> Joshua calls Israel to love God, but human worth is not a measure of usefulness. Scripture also depicts people as made in God’s image before any spiritual achievement. Christ calls people into growing faithfulness, not because they are disposable or worthless, but because they are loved. Spiritual transformation should deepen dignity toward ourselves and our neighbors.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> Where have I measured a person’s worth by usefulness or visible holiness?</p><p><strong>Practice:</strong> Treat one overlooked person with attention and respect, without asking them to prove their value.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Creator God, remind us of every person’s dignity. Shape our hearts to love as Jesus loves.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Joshua 23:11"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 10,
        "title": "He that is faithful in that which is least, is faithful also in much. Luke 16. 10.",
        "content": "<p>He that is faithful in that which is least, is faithful also in much. Luke 16. 10.</p><p>The least action of life can be as surely done from the loftiest motive as the highest and noblest. Faithfulness measures acts as God measures them. True conscientiousness deals with our duties as God deals with them. Duty is duty, conscience is conscience, right is right, and wrong is wrong, whatever sized type they be printed in. \"Large\" and \"small\" are not words for the vocabulary of conscience. It knows only two words—right and wrong.— Alex. McLaren.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Luke 16:10"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 11,
        "title": "My God shall supply all your need according to His riches in glory by Christ Jesus. Phil. 4. 19.",
        "content": "<p>My God shall supply all your need according to His riches in glory by Christ Jesus. Phil. 4. 19.</p><p><strong>Source and historical context:</strong> A writer signing C. H. M. celebrates God’s provision through Philippians 4:19.</p><p><strong>Devotional focus:</strong> Paul writes about provision within a community that has shared resources with him. Trust in God does not make friends, doctors, food assistance, or careful budgeting unnecessary. God’s kindness often reaches us through ordinary generosity and responsible choices. Receiving help is not proof of spiritual weakness, and this verse does not promise wealth on demand.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> What need should I bring both to prayer and to a trustworthy person?</p><p><strong>Practice:</strong> Name one practical resource or relationship that could help meet a genuine need.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Generous God, teach me to give and receive help with humility and gratitude.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Philippians 4:19"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 12,
        "title": "Count it all joy when you face various trials. James 1. 2.",
        "content": "<p>Count it all joy when you fall into divers temptations. James 1. 2.</p><p><strong>Source and historical context:</strong> The unattributed meditation links James 1:2 with happiness during trials.</p><p><strong>Devotional focus:</strong> James invites believers to count endurance as meaningful, but it does not command cheerful feelings in every crisis. Trials are painful, and Scripture makes room for tears, uncertainty, and shared burdens. Faith does not require pretending that harm is good. We can ask God for wisdom while seeking safety, healing, and help from others.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> What honest feeling have I believed I must hide to appear faithful?</p><p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read James 1:2–5 and describe one concern truthfully to God or a trusted person.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> God of wisdom, receive our grief and questions. Strengthen us without demanding a cheerful mask.</p>",
        "references": [
          "James 1:2"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 13,
        "title": "Blessed are they that have not seen, and yet have believed. John 20. 29.",
        "content": "<p>Blessed are they that have not seen, and yet have believed. John 20. 29.</p><p>The seen are shadows: the substance is found in the unseen. . . . No doubt, in Christ, the foundation of our faith is unseen; but so is that of yonder tower that lifts its tall erect form among the waves over which it throws a saving light. It appears to rest on the rolling billows; but, beneath these, invisible and immovable, lies the solid rock on which it stands secure; and when the hurricane roars above, and breakers roar below, I could go calmly to sleep in that lone sea tower. Founded on a rock, and safer than the proudest palace that stands on the sandy, surf-beaten shore, it cannot be moved. Still less the Rock of Ages! Who trusts in that is fit for death, prepared for judgment, ready for the last day's sounding trumpet, since, \"The Lord redeems the soul of His servants, and none of them that trust in Him shall be desolate.\"— Guthrie.</p>",
        "references": [
          "John 20:29"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 14,
        "title": "My Father is glorified when you bear much fruit. John 15. 8.",
        "content": "<p>In this is my Father glorified that you bear much fruit. John 15. 8.</p><p>What a possibility, what an inspiration, that we can enhance the glory of \"our Father\"! Our hearts leap at the thought.</p><p>How can this be done? By bearing \"leaves,\"—a profession of love for Him? No. By bearing some fruit? No. \"That you bear much fruit.\" In the abundance of the yield is the joy, the glory of the husbandman. We should, therefore, aim to be extraordinary, \"hundred-fold\" Christians, satisfied with none but the largest yield. Our lives should be packed with good deeds. Then at harvest time we can say, \"Father, I have glorified You on the earth!\"— W. Jennings.</p>",
        "references": [
          "John 15:8"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 15,
        "title": "Every day I will bless you and praise your name forever. Psa. 145. 2.",
        "content": "<p>Every day will I bless You; and I will praise Your name forever and ever. Psa. 145. 2.</p><p>There is a very beautiful device by which the Japanese are accustomed to express their wishes for their friends. It is the figure of a drum in which the birds have built their nest. The story told of it is that once there lived a good king, so anxiously concerned for the welfare of his people that at the palace gate he set a drum, and whoever had any wrong to be redressed or any want, should beat the drum, and at once, by day or night, the king would grant the suppliant an audience and relief. But throughout the land there reigned such prosperity and contentment that none needed to appeal for anything, and the birds built their nests within it and filled it with the music of their song.</p><p>Such gracious access is granted to us even by the King of Heaven, and day and night His ready hearing and His help are within the reach of all that come to Him; but of all men most blessed are they who have found on earth a blessedness in which all want is forgotten, and trust rests so assured of safety in the Father's care that prayer gives place to ceaseless praise. They rejoice in the Lord always .— Mark Guy Pearse.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Psalms 145:2"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 16,
        "title": "They shall mount up with wings as eagles; they shall run, and not be weary; they shall walk, and not faint.—Isa. 40. 31.",
        "content": "<p>They shall mount up with wings as eagles; they shall run, and not be weary; they shall walk, and not faint.—Isa. 40. 31.</p><p>This, my soul, is the triumph of your being—to be able to walk with God! Flight belongs to the young soul; it is the romance of religion. To run without weariness belongs to the lofty soul; it is the beauty of religion. But to walk and not faint belongs to the perfect soul; it is the power of religion.</p><p>Can you walk in white through the stained thoroughfares of men? Can you touch the vile and polluted ones of earth and retain your garments pure? Can you meet in contact with the sinful and be yourself undefiled? Then you have surpassed the flight of the eagle!— George Matheson.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Isaiah 40:31"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 17,
        "title": "And Moses was in the mount forty days and forty nights. Ex. 24. 18.",
        "content": "<p>And Moses was in the mount forty days and forty nights. Ex. 24. 18.</p><p>The life of fellowship with God cannot be built up in a day. It begins with the habitual reference of all to Him, hour by hour, as Moses did in Egypt. But it moves on to more and longer periods of communion; and it finds its consummation and bliss in days and nights of intercession and waiting and holy intercourse.— F. B. Meyer.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Exodus 24:18"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 18,
        "title": "Elisha said, Lord, please open his eyes so that he may see. 2 Kings 6. 17.",
        "content": "<p>Elisha said, Lord, I ask you, open his eyes that he may see. 2 Kings 6. 17.</p><p><strong>Source and historical context:</strong> A historical writer turns Elisha’s vision of protective horses into a metaphor for every adversity.</p><p><strong>Devotional focus:</strong> In 2 Kings 6, Elisha’s servant is afraid, and the prophet asks that his eyes be opened. The story encourages confidence in God but does not say harm becomes beneficial merely because a sufferer thinks positively. Danger, grief, and illness can require intervention and careful planning. Prayer can accompany practical care without requiring a person to reinterpret trauma as a chariot of blessing.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> When have I made someone responsible for turning harm into a spiritual benefit?</p><p><strong>Practice:</strong> Offer one grounded form of support instead of asking a sufferer to change their attitude.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> God of mercy, make us attentive to fear and generous with protection and care.</p>",
        "references": [
          "2 Kings 6:17"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 19,
        "title": "All things work together for good to them that love God. Rom. 8. 28.",
        "content": "<p>All things work together for good to them that love God. Rom. 8. 28.</p><p>In one thousand trials it is not five hundred of them that work for the believer's good, but nine hundred and ninety-nine of them, and one beside .— George Müller.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Romans 8:28"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 20,
        "title": "Make holy garments for Aaron. Ex. 28. 2.",
        "content": "<p>You will make holy garments for Aaron. Ex. 28. 2.</p><p>Have we no garments of blue, and purple, and beautiful suggestiveness? We have garments of praise; we are clothed with the Lord Jesus. And have we no ornaments? The ornament of a meek and quiet spirit is, in the sight of God, of great price. And have we no golden bells? We have the golden bells of holy actions. Our words are bells, our actions are bells, our purposes are bells. Whenever we move, our motion is thus understood to be a motion towards holy places, holy deeds, holy character.— Joseph Parker.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Exodus 28:2"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 21,
        "title": "Lord, you will hear my voice in the morning; I will pray to you and look up. Psa 5. 3.",
        "content": "<p>My voice will you hear in the morning, O Lord; in the morning will I direct my prayer to You, and will look up. Psa 5. 3.</p><p>The morning is the gate of the day, and should be well guarded with prayer. It is one end of the thread on which the day's actions are strung, and should be well knotted with devotion. If we felt more the majesty of life we should be more careful of its mornings. He who rushes from his bed to his business and waits not to worship is as foolish as though he had not put on his clothes, or cleansed his face, and as unwise as though he dashed into battle without arms or armor. Be it ours to bathe in the softly flowing river of communion with God, before the heat of the wilderness and the burden of the way begin to oppress us.— Spurgeon.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Psalms 5:3"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 22,
        "title": "Show me your ways, O Lord; teach me your paths. Psa. 25. 4.",
        "content": "<p>Show me Your ways, O Lord; teach me Your paths. Psa. 25. 4.</p><p>There is a path in which every child of God is to walk, and in which alone God can accompany him.— Denham Smith.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Psalms 25:4"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 23,
        "title": "There remaineth therefore a rest to the people of God. Heb. 4. 9.",
        "content": "<p>There remains therefore a rest to the people of God. Heb. 4. 9.</p><p>How sweet the music of this first heavenly chime floating across the waters of death from the towers of the New Jerusalem. Pilgrim, faint under your long and arduous pilgrimage, hear it! It is rest . Soldier, carrying still upon you blood and dust of battle, hear it! It is rest . Voyager, tossed on the waves of sin and sorrow, driven here and there on the world's heaving ocean of vicissitude, hear it! The haven is in sight; the very waves that are breaking on you seem to murmur—\" So He gives His beloved rest .\" It is the long-drawn sigh of existence at last answered. The toil and travail of earth's protracted week is at an end. The calm of its unbroken Sabbath is begun. Man, weary man, has found at last the long-sought-for rest in the bosom of his God!— Macduff.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Hebrews 4:9"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 24,
        "title": "Under His shadow. Song of Sol. 2. 3.",
        "content": "<p>Under His shadow. Song of Sol. 2. 3.</p><p>Frances Ridley Havergal says: I seem to see four pictures suggested by that: under the shadow of a rock in a weary plain; under the shadow of a tree; closer still, under the shadow of His wing; nearest and closest, in the shadow of His hand. Surely that hand must be the piercèd hand, that may oftentimes press us sorely, and yet forever encircling, upholding and shadowing!</p>",
        "references": [
          "Song of Solomon 2:3"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 25,
        "title": "He made as though He would have gone further. Luke 24. 28.",
        "content": "<p>He made as though He would have gone further. Luke 24. 28.</p><p><strong>Source and historical context:</strong> F. Whitfield interprets the Emmaus encounter as a warning about ignoring God’s voice.</p><p><strong>Devotional focus:</strong> In Luke 24, Jesus accompanies confused travelers, listens to them, and is welcomed to stay. The story emphasizes recognition and hospitality more than a formula for losing the Spirit’s presence. Spiritual numbness or a difficult season does not prove that God has walked away. Invitation, patience, and a meal shared in love are fitting responses to this passage.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> When do I most need the invitation to welcome Christ rather than the threat of rejection?</p><p><strong>Practice:</strong> Make space for a short Scripture reading or a hospitable meal without judging your feelings.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Risen Jesus, walk with me in uncertainty. Open my understanding through patient love.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Luke 24:28"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 26,
        "title": "My God shall be my strength. Isa. 49. 5.",
        "content": "<p>My God shall be my strength. Isa. 49. 5.</p><p>Oh, do not pray for easy lives! Pray to be stronger men! Do not pray for tasks equal to your powers; pray for powers equal to your tasks! Then the doing of your work shall be no miracle. But you shall be a miracle. Every day you shall wonder at yourself, at the richness of life which has come in you by the grace of God.— Phillips Brooks.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Isaiah 49:5"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 27,
        "title": "Despising the shame. Heb. 12. 2.",
        "content": "<p>Despising the shame. Heb. 12. 2.</p><p>And how is that to be done? In two ways. Go up the mountain, and the things in the plain will look very small; the higher you rise the more insignificant they will seem. Hold fellowship with God, and the threatening foes here will seem very, very unformidable. Another way is, pull up the curtain and gaze on what is behind it. The low foot-hills that lie at the base of some Alpine country may look high when seen from the plain, as long as the snowy summits are wrapped in mist; but when a little puff of wind comes and clears away the fog from the lofty peaks, nobody looks at the little green hills in front. So the world's hindrances and the world's difficulties and cares look very lofty till the cloud lifts. But when we see the great white summits, everything lower does not seem so very high after all. Look to Jesus, and that will dwarf the difficulties.— Alex. McLaren.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Hebrews 12:2"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 28,
        "title": "Are there not twelve hours in the day? John 11. 9.",
        "content": "<p>Are there not twelve hours in the day? John 11. 9.</p><p>The very fact of a Christian being here, and not in heaven, is a proof that some work awaits him.— William Arnot.</p>",
        "references": [
          "John 11:9"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 29,
        "title": "Not as I will, but as you will. Matt. 26. 39.",
        "content": "<p>Not as I will, but as You will. Matt. 26. 39.</p><p>There are no disappointments to those whose wills are buried in the will of God.— Faber.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Matthew 26:39"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 30,
        "title": "The living God. Dan. 6. 20.",
        "content": "<p>The living God. Dan. 6. 20.</p><p>How many times we find this expression in the Scriptures, and yet it is just this very thing that we are so prone to lose sight of! We know it is written \" the living God \"; but in our daily life there is scarcely anything we practically so much lose sight of as the fact that God is The Living God ; that He is now whatever He was three or four thousand years since; that He has the same sovereign power, the same saving love towards those who love and serve Him as ever He had, and that He will do for them now what He did for others two, three, four thousand years ago, simply because He is the living God, the unchanging One. Oh, how therefore we should confide in Him, and in our darkest moments never lose sight of the fact that He is still and ever will be The Living God !— George Müller.</p><p>Therefore we are buried with Him by baptism into death; that like as Christ was raised up from the dead by the glory of the Father, even so we also should walk in newness of life. Rom. 6. 4.</p><p>That is the life we are called upon to live, and that is the life it is our privilege to lead; for God never gives us a call without its being a privilege, and He never gives us the privilege to come up higher without stretching out to us His hand to lift us up. Come up higher and higher into the realities and glories of the resurrection life, knowing that your life is hid with Christ in God. Shake yourself loose of every incumbrance, turn your back on every defilement, give yourself over like clay to the hands of the potter, that He may stamp upon you the fulness of His own resurrection glory, that you, beholding as in a mirror the glory of the Lord, may be changed from glory to glory as by the Spirit of the Lord.— W. Hay Aitken.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Daniel 6:20",
          "Romans 6:4"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 31,
        "title": "Christ is all, and in all. Col. 3. 11.",
        "content": "<p>Christ is all, and in all. Col. 3. 11.</p><p>Editorial reflection: The source's meditation names Christ as the service, direction, joy, and hope of a life. Colossians does not ask us to add Christ to an otherwise unchanged day. Ask which ordinary task can become service to him today, and let his presence shape how you do it.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Colossians 3:11"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 32,
        "title": "Continue in prayer. Col. 4. 2.",
        "content": "<p>Continue in prayer. Col. 4. 2.</p><p>Do you want nothing? Then I fear you do not know your poverty. Have you no mercy to ask of God? Then may the Lord's mercy show you your misery. A prayerless soul is a Christless soul. Prayer is the lisping of the believing infant, the shout of the fighting believer, the requiem of the dying saint falling asleep in Jesus.— Spurgeon.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Colossians 4:2"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 33,
        "title": "In Him the whole building, joined together, grows into a holy temple in the Lord. Eph. 2. 21.",
        "content": "<p>In whom all the building, fitly framed together, grows to an holy temple in the Lord. Eph. 2. 21.</p><p>The life-tabernacle is a wondrous building; there is room for workers of all kinds in the uprearing of its mysterious and glorious walls. If we cannot do the greatest work, we may do the least. Our heaven will come out of the realization of the fact that it was God's tabernacle we were building, and under God's blessing that we were working.— Joseph Parker.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Ephesians 2:21"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 34,
        "title": "Love not the world. 1 John 2. 15.",
        "content": "<p>Love not the world. 1 John 2. 15.</p><p>Love it not, and yet love it. Love it with the love of Him who gave His Son to die for it. Love it with the love of Him who shed His blood for it. Love it with the love of angels, who rejoice in its conversion. Love it to do it good, giving your tears to its sufferings, your pity to its sorrows, your wealth to its wants, your prayers to its miseries, and to its fields of charity, and philanthropy, and Christian piety, your powers and hours of labor. You cannot live without affecting it, or being affected by it. You will make the world better, or it will make you worse.</p><p>God help you by His grace and Holy Spirit so to live in the world as to live above it, and look beyond it; and so to love it that when you leave it, you may leave it better than you found it.— Guthrie.</p>",
        "references": [
          "1 John 2:15"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 35,
        "title": "You open your hand and satisfy the desire of every living thing. Psa. 145. 16.",
        "content": "<p>You open your hand and satisfy the desire of every living thing. Psa. 145. 16.</p><p>Desire, it is a dainty word! It were much that He should satisfy the need , the want; but He goes far beyond that. Pity is moved to meet our need; duty may sometimes look after our wants; but to satisfy the desire implies a tender watchfulness, a sweet and gracious knowledge of us, an eagerness of blessing. God is never satisfied until He has satisfied our desires.— Mark Guy Pearse.</p><p>You servants of the Lord, which by night stand in the house of the Lord. . . . The Lord that made heaven and earth bless you out of Zion. Psa. 134. 1, 3.</p><p>If I would know the love of my friend, I must see what it can do in the winter. So with the divine love. It is very easy for me to worship in the summer sunshine, when the melodies of life are in the air and the fruits of life are on the tree. But let the song of the bird cease, and the fruit of the tree fall; and will my heart still go on to sing? Will I stand in God's house by night? Will I love Him in His own night? Will I watch with Him even one hour in His Gethsemane? Will I help to bear His cross up the Via Dolorosa? My love has come to Him in His humiliation. My faith has found Him in His lowliness. My heart has recognized His majesty through His mean disguise, and I know at last that I desire not the gift, but the Giver. When I can stand in His house by night, I have accepted Him for Himself alone.— George Matheson.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Psalms 145:16",
          "Psalms 134:1",
          "Psalms 134:3"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 36,
        "title": "Anyone who says they remain in Christ should walk as Christ walked. 1 John 2. 6.",
        "content": "<p>He that says he abides in Him ought himself also so to walk even as He walked. 1 John 2. 6.</p><p>The preaching that this world needs most is the sermons in shoes that are walking with Jesus Christ.— Selected.</p>",
        "references": [
          "1 John 2:6"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 37,
        "title": "Then shall we know, if we follow on to know the Lord. Hosea 6. 3.",
        "content": "<p>Then shall we know, if we follow on to know the Lord. Hosea 6. 3.</p><p>The Lord has brought us into the pathway of the knowledge of Him, and bids us pursue that path through all its strange meanderings until it opens out upon the plain where God's throne is. Our life is a following on to know the Lord. We marvel at some of the experiences through which we are called to pass, but afterwards we see that they afforded us some new knowledge of our Lord. . . . We have not to wait for some brighter opportunity; but by improvement of the present are to build for ourselves a bridge to that future.— G Bowen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Hosea 6:3"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 38,
        "title": "Leave your country, your relatives, and your father's house. Gen. 12. 1.",
        "content": "<p>Get you out of your country, and from your kindred, and from your father's house. Gen. 12. 1.</p><p>Editorial reflection: Abraham's departure began with God's call before he could see its outcome. Faith can require a step away from what is familiar, but it does not require pretending that change is easy. Name one step of obedience you can take today and entrust its result to God.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Genesis 12:1"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 39,
        "title": "Abraham . . . was gathered to his people. Gen. 25. 8.",
        "content": "<p>Abraham . . . was gathered to his people. Gen. 25. 8.</p><p>After all communion we dwell as upon islands, dotted over a great archipelago, each upon his little rock with the sea dashing between us; but the time comes when, if our hearts are set upon that great Lord whose presence makes us one, there shall be no more sea and all the isolated rocks shall be parts of a great continent . . . If we cultivate that sense of detachment from the present and of having our true affinities in the unseen, if we dwell here as strangers because our citizenship is in heaven, then death will not drag us away from our associates nor hunt us into a lonely land, but will bring us where closer bonds shall knit the \"sweet societies\" together, and the sheep shall couch close by one another because all gathered round the one Shepherd. Then many a tie shall be re-woven, and the solitary wanderer meet again the dear ones whom he had \"loved long since and lost awhile.\"— Alex. McLaren.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Genesis 25:8"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 40,
        "title": "Therefore the Lord will wait so that He may be gracious to you. Isa. 30. 18.",
        "content": "<p>Therefore will the Lord wait, that He may be gracious to you. Isa. 30. 18.</p><p>This is God's way. In the darkest hours of the night His tread draws near across the billows. As the day of execution is breaking, the angel comes to Peter's cell. When the scaffold for Mordecai is complete, the royal sleeplessness leads to a reaction in favor of the threatened race.</p><p>Ah, soul, it may have come to the worst with you before you are delivered; but you will be! God may keep you waiting, but He will ever be mindful of His covenant, and will appear to fulfil His inviolable word.— F. B. Meyer.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Isaiah 30:18"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 41,
        "title": "He loves our nation and has built us a synagogue. Luke 7. 5.",
        "content": "<p>He loves our nation and he has built us a synagogue. Luke 7. 5.</p><p>Marble and granite are perishable monuments, and their inscriptions may be seldom read. Carve your names on human hearts; they alone are immortal!— Theodore Cuyler.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Luke 7:5"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 42,
        "title": "As many as I love I . . . chasten. Rev. 3. 19.",
        "content": "<p>As many as I love I . . . chasten. Rev. 3. 19.</p><p><strong>Source and historical context:</strong> The historical meditation applies Jesus’ warning to Laodicea to the experience of personal sorrow.</p><p><strong>Devotional focus:</strong> Revelation 3 addresses a particular Christian community called to repentance; it is not a diagnosis of every painful event. Hardship does not show that a person is guilty or being disciplined by God. Jesus also invites fellowship and renewal. We can receive correction where it is warranted while refusing to blame vulnerable people for illnesses, losses, or abuse.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> Have I mistaken hardship for evidence that a person deserves correction?</p><p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read Revelation 3:14–22 as a message to a church, then ask how its invitation differs from blame.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Patient Christ, give us honest repentance and tenderness toward those who suffer.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Revelation 3:19"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 43,
        "title": "What means these stones? Josh. 4. 21.",
        "content": "<p>What means these stones? Josh. 4. 21.</p><p>Editorial reflection: Joshua asked Israel to tell the story behind the memorial stones. Remembering God's help should lead us to tell the next generation what happened and to live as witnesses now. What act of faithfulness will help someone else recognize God's care through you?</p>",
        "references": [
          "Joshua 4:21"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 44,
        "title": "You also, as living stones. 1 Pet. 2. 5. (R. V.)",
        "content": "<p>You also as living stones. 1 Pet. 2. 5. (R. V.)</p><p>There should be something so remarkable, so peculiar about the life and conversation of a Christian that men should be compelled to ask, \"What does this mean?\". . . . Is there anything in your character, words, and habits of life so different from the world around you that men are involuntarily compelled to ask themselves or others, \"What does this mean?\" Not that there is to be a forced singularity, a peculiarity for the sake of being peculiar; that were merely to copy the pharisaism of ancient days. . . . Oh, that we might realize that this is the purpose for which God sends us into the world, as He sent His only begotten Son!— S. A. Blackwood.</p>",
        "references": [
          "1 Peter 2:5"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 45,
        "title": "All . . . saw his face as it had been the face of an angel Acts 6. 15.",
        "content": "<p>All . . . saw his face as it had been the face of an angel Acts 6. 15.</p><p>The face is made every day by its morning prayer, and by its morning look out of windows which open upon heaven.— Joseph Parker.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Acts 6:15"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 46,
        "title": "At the commandment of the Lord they rested in the tents, and at the commandment of the Lord they journeyed. Num. 9. 23.",
        "content": "<p>At the commandment of the Lord they rested in the tents, and at the commandment of the Lord they journeyed. Num. 9. 23.</p><p><strong>Source and historical context:</strong> A writer signing C. H. M. draws an obedience lesson from Israel following the cloud.</p><p><strong>Devotional focus:</strong> Numbers 9 describes a particular wilderness journey in which Israel learns to follow God’s guidance. It does not provide a rule that every mistaken decision separates a believer from divine care. We can discern responsibly, admit mistakes, and change course. God’s faithfulness is greater than our ability to recognize every turning point perfectly.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> Where am I treating a past decision as proof that God has abandoned me?</p><p><strong>Practice:</strong> Review one decision with wise counsel and identify a constructive next step.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Faithful God, guide me with wisdom. Meet me with mercy when I need to begin again.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Numbers 9:23"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 47,
        "title": "In Him, after you believed, you were sealed with the promised Holy Spirit. Eph. 1. 13.",
        "content": "<p>In whom also, after that you believed, you were sealed with that holy Spirit of promise. Eph. 1. 13.</p><p>The Lord puts a seal upon His own, that everybody may know them. The sealing in your case is the Spirit producing in you likeness to the Lord. The holier you become, the seal is the more distinct and plain, the more evident to every passer-by, for then will men take knowledge of you that you have been with Jesus.— Andrew Bonar.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Ephesians 1:13"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 48,
        "title": "Do not boast about tomorrow. Prov. 27. 1.",
        "content": "<p>Boast not yourself of tomorrow. Prov. 27. 1.</p><p>The only preparation for the morrow is the right use of today. The stone in the hands of the builder must be put in its place and fitted to receive another. The morrow comes for nothing, if today is not heeded. Neglect not the call that comes to you this day, for such neglect is nothing else than boasting yourself of tomorrow.— G. Bowen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Proverbs 27:1"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 49,
        "title": "I will help you, says the Lord. Isa. 41. 14.",
        "content": "<p>I will help you, says the Lord. Isa. 41. 14.</p><p>O my soul, is not this enough? Do you need more strength than the omnipotence of the united Trinity? Do you want more wisdom than exists in the Father, more love than displays itself in the Son, or more power than is manifest in the influences of the Spirit? Bring here your empty pitcher! Surely this well will fill it. Haste, gather up your wants, and bring them here—your emptiness, your woes, your needs. See, this river of God is full for your supply; what can you desire beside? Go forth, my soul, in this your might. The eternal God is yours helper!— Spurgeon.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Isaiah 41:14"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 50,
        "title": "To every man his work. Mark 13. 34.",
        "content": "<p>To every man his work. Mark 13. 34.</p><p>He does the most for God's great world who does the best in his own little world.— Selected.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Mark 13:34"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 51,
        "title": "Bring some of the fish you have just caught. John 21. 10.",
        "content": "<p>Bring of the fish which you have now caught. John 21. 10.</p><p>Why was this? Oh, the Lord wants us to minister to Him as well as to receive from Him, and our service finds its true end when it becomes food for our dear Lord. He was pleased to feed on their fish while they were feeding on His. It was the double banquet of which He speaks in the tender message of revelation, \"I will sup with him, and he with Me.\"— A. B. Simpson.</p>",
        "references": [
          "John 21:10"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 52,
        "title": "By faith Abraham, when he was called to go out into a place which he should after receive for an inheritance, obeyed. Heb. 11. 8.",
        "content": "<p>By faith Abraham, when he was called to go out into a place which he should after receive for an inheritance, obeyed. Heb. 11. 8.</p><p>Where he went, he knew not; it was enough for him to know that he went with God. He leant not so much upon the promises as upon the Promiser. He looked not on the difficulties of his lot, but on the King, eternal, immortal, invisible, the only wise God, who had deigned to appoint his course, and would certainly vindicate Himself. O glorious faith! This is your work, these are your possibilities: contentment to sail with sealed orders, because of unwavering confidence in the love and wisdom of the Lord High Admiral: willinghood to rise up, leave all, and follow Christ, because of the glad assurance that earth's best cannot bear comparison with heaven's least.— F. B. Meyer.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Hebrews 11:8"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 53,
        "title": "The Lord is a God of knowledge, and by Him actions are weighed. 1 Sam. 2. 3.",
        "content": "<p>The Lord is a God of knowledge, and by Him actions are weighed. 1 Sam. 2. 3.</p><p>God does not measure what we bring to Him. He weighs it.— Mark Guy Pearse.</p>",
        "references": [
          "1 Samuel 2:3"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 54,
        "title": "After you were enlightened, you endured a hard struggle with suffering. Heb. 10. 32.",
        "content": "<p><strong>Read:</strong> Hebrews 10:32; John 1:45-46; John 3:8</p><p><strong>Source and historical context:</strong> An anthology day combines several historical speakers and a comparison between wind and the Spirit.</p><p><strong>Devotional focus:</strong> John 3 uses wind as an analogy for the Spirit’s freedom; it is not a lesson in atmospheric physics or a mechanical technique for producing spiritual power. Humility matters, but God’s Spirit is not controlled by a formula. Read the three collected passages separately, allowing each to speak in its own setting. Prayer can be patient without trying to manufacture an extraordinary experience.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> Am I seeking a predictable technique instead of receptive, thoughtful faith?</p><p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read John 3:5–8 slowly and allow a moment of silence without demanding a result.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Holy Spirit, grow humility and truthfulness in me. Help me listen without trying to control you.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Hebrews 10:32",
          "John 1:45-46",
          "John 3:8"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 55,
        "title": "Your gentleness has made me great. 2 Sam. 22. 36.",
        "content": "<p>Your gentleness has made me great. 2 Sam. 22. 36.</p><p>The gentleness of Christ is the comeliest ornament that a Christian can wear.— William Arnot.</p>",
        "references": [
          "2 Samuel 22:36"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 56,
        "title": "Jacob went on his way, and the angels of God met him. Gen. 32. 1.",
        "content": "<p>Jacob went on his way, and the angels of God met him. Gen. 32. 1.</p><p>It is in the path where God has bade us walk that we shall find the angels around us. We may meet them, indeed, on paths of our own choosing, but it will be the sort of angel that Balaam met, with a sword in his hand, mighty and beautiful, but wrathful too; and we had better not front him! But the friendly helpers, the emissaries of God's love, the apostles of His grace, do not haunt the roads that we make for ourselves.— Alex. McLaren.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Genesis 32:1"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 57,
        "title": "I am the way, the truth, and the life: no one comes to the Father except through me. John 14. 6.",
        "content": "<p>I am the way, the truth, and the life: no man comes to the Father but by me. John 14. 6.</p><p>Heaven often seems distant and unknown, but if He who made the road there is our guide, we need not fear to lose the way. We do not want to see far ahead—only far enough to discern Him and trace His footsteps. . . . They who follow Christ, even through darkness, will surely reach the Father.— Henry Van Dyke.</p>",
        "references": [
          "John 14:6"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 58,
        "title": "Forgetting those things which are behind . . . I press toward the mark for the prize of the high calling of God in Christ Jesus. Phil. 3. 13, 14.",
        "content": "<p>Forgetting those things which are behind . . . I press toward the mark for the prize of the high calling of God in Christ Jesus. Phil. 3. 13, 14.</p><p>It is not by regretting what is irreparable that true work is to be done, but by making the best of what we are. It is not by complaining that we have not the right tools, but by using well the tools we have. What we are, and where we are, is God's providential arrangement—God's doing, though it may be man's misdoing. Life is a series of mistakes, and he is not the best Christian who makes the fewest false steps. He is the best who wins the most splendid victories by the retrieval of mistakes.— F. W. Robertson.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Philippians 3:13-14"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 59,
        "title": "Come up in the morning . . . and present yourself to me on top of the mountain. Ex. 34. 2.",
        "content": "<p>Come up in the morning . . . and present yourself to me in the top of the mount. Ex. 34. 2.</p><p>The morning is the time fixed for my meeting the Lord. This very word morning is as a cluster of rich grapes. Let me crush them, and drink the sacred wine.</p><p>In the morning! Then God means me to be at my best in strength and hope. I have not to climb in my weakness. In the night I have buried yesterday's fatigue, and in the morning I take a new lease of energy.</p><p>Sweet morning! There is hope in its music. Blessed is the day whose morning is sanctified! Successful is the day whose first victory was won in prayer! Holy is the day whose dawn finds you on the top of the mount! Health is established in the morning. Wealth is won in the morning. The light is brightest in the morning. \"Wake, psaltery and harp; I myself will awake early.\"— Joseph Parker.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Exodus 34:2"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 60,
        "title": "Whatsoever a man soweth, that shall he also reap. Gal. 6. 7.",
        "content": "<p>Whatever a man sows, that shall he also reap. Gal. 6. 7.</p><p>The most common actions of life, its every day and hour, are invested with the highest grandeur, when we think how they extend their issues into eternity. Our hands are now sowing seeds for that great harvest. We shall meet again all we are doing and have done. The graves shall give up their dead, and from the tombs of oblivion the past shall give up all that it holds in keeping, to bear true witness for or against us.— Guthrie.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Galatians 6:7"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 61,
        "title": "It is an eleven-day journey from Horeb by way of Mount Seir to Kadesh-barnea. Deut. 1. 2.",
        "content": "<p>There are eleven days' journey from Horeb, by the way of mount Seir, to Kadesh-barnea. Deut. 1. 2.</p><p><strong>Source and historical context:</strong> A writer signing C. H. M. turns the forty-year wilderness journey into a metaphor for spiritual delay.</p><p><strong>Devotional focus:</strong> Deuteronomy recalls Israel’s complex history, including failure and divine care. We cannot conclude that every prolonged difficulty is God holding someone back until they improve. Illness, poverty, disability, and interrupted plans have many causes. Patient learning can be valuable, but its value does not justify blaming a person for circumstances beyond their control.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> Have I interpreted another person’s delay as a moral deficiency?</p><p><strong>Practice:</strong> Ask what practical barrier you can help remove rather than guessing why progress is slow.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> God of the wilderness, accompany us through delay and give us compassionate wisdom.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Deuteronomy 1:2"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 62,
        "title": "If we confess our sins, He is faithful and just to forgive us our sins, and to cleanse us from all unrighteousness. 1 John 1. 9.",
        "content": "<p>If we confess our sins, He is faithful and just to forgive us our sins, and to cleanse us from all unrighteousness. 1 John 1. 9.</p><p>The same moment which brings the consciousness of sin ought to bring also the confession of it and the consciousness of forgiveness.— Smith.</p>",
        "references": [
          "1 John 1:9"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 63,
        "title": "As captain of the host of the Lord am I now come. Josh. 5. 14.",
        "content": "<p>As captain of the host of the Lord am I now come. Josh. 5. 14.</p><p>Surely Israel might now face the foe with unwavering confidence, and sing of victory even before the battle was gained. And so may the Christian. It is to no conflict of uncertain issue that he advances; the result of the battle is not doubtful. The struggle may be severe, the warfare long; he may sometimes, like the pilgrim, be beaten to the ground, and well-near lose his sword; but \"though cast down\" he is \"not destroyed.\" The Captain of salvation is on his side, and in the midst of sharpest conflict he can say, \"Thanks be to God, who gives us the victory through our Lord Jesus Christ.\"— S. A. Blackwood.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Joshua 5:14"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 64,
        "title": "To me to live is Christ, and to die is gain. Phil. 1. 21.",
        "content": "<p>To me to live is Christ, and to die is gain. Phil. 1. 21.</p><p>Live in Christ, and you are in the suburbs of heaven. There is but a thin wall between you and the land of praises. You are within one hour's sailing of the shore of the new Canaan.— William Rutherford.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Philippians 1:21"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 65,
        "title": "The one who sent me is with me; the Father has not left me alone, for I always do what pleases Him. John 8. 29.",
        "content": "<p>He that sent me is with me; the Father has not left me alone, for I do always those things that please Him. John 8. 29.</p><p>He who holds nearest communion with heaven can best discharge the duties of everyday life.— Selected.</p>",
        "references": [
          "John 8:29"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 66,
        "title": "Quench not the Spirit. 1 Thess. 5. 19.",
        "content": "<p>Quench not the Spirit. 1 Thess. 5. 19.</p><p>In order that you may not quench the Spirit, you must make it a constant study to know what is the mind of the Spirit. You must discriminate with the utmost care between His suggestions and the suggestions of your own deceitful heart. You will keep in constant recollection what are the offices of the Spirit as described by Christ in the Gospel of John. You will be on your guard against impulsive movements, inconsiderate acts, rash words. You will abide in prayer. Search the Word. Confess Christ on all possible occasions. Seek the society of His people. Shrink from conformity to the world, its vain fashions, unmeaning etiquette. Be scrupulous in your reading. \"What I say to you, I say to all, watch!\" \"Have oil in your lamps.\" \"Quench not the Spirit.\"— Bowen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "1 Thessalonians 5:19"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 67,
        "title": "When He comes into the world, He says, . . . You have prepared a body for me. Heb. 10. 5.",
        "content": "<p>When He comes into the world, He says,. . . A body Have you prepared me. Heb. 10. 5.</p><p>This word of Christ must be adopted by each of His followers. Nothing will help us to live in this world and keep ourselves unspotted but the Spirit that was in Christ, that looked upon His body as prepared by God for His service; that looks upon our body as prepared by Him too, that we might offer it to Him. Like Christ, we too have a body in which the Holy Spirit dwells. Like Christ, we too must yield our body, with every member, every power, every action, to fulfil His will, to be offered up to Him, to glorify Him. Like Christ, we must prove in our body that we are holy to the Lord.— Andrew Murray.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Hebrews 10:5"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 68,
        "title": "Full of [satisfied with] years. Gen. 25. 8.",
        "content": "<p>Full of [satisfied with] years. Gen. 25. 8.</p><p>Scaffoldings are for buildings, and the moments and days and years of our earthly lives are scaffolding. What are you building inside it? What kind of a structure will be disclosed when the scaffolding is knocked away? Days and years are ours, that they may give us what eternity cannot take away—a character built upon the love of God in Christ, and moulded into His likeness.</p><p>Has your life helped you to do that? If so, you have got the best out of it, and your life is completed, whatever may be the number of its days. Quality, not quantity, is the thing that determines the perfectness of a life. Has your life this completeness?— Alex. McLaren.</p><p>Keep yourselves in the love of God. Jude 21.</p><p>Fruit ripened in the sun is sweetest.— Selected.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Genesis 25:8"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 69,
        "title": "You will receive power when the Holy Spirit comes upon you, and you will be my witnesses. Acts 1. 8.",
        "content": "<p>You shall receive power after that the Holy Spirit is come upon you, and you shall be witnesses to Me. Acts 1. 8.</p><p>Look at it! Think of it! A hundred and twenty men and women having no patronage, no promise of any earthly favor, no endowment, no wealth—a company of men and women having to get their living by common daily toil, and busied with all the household duties of daily life—and yet they are to begin the conquests of Christianity! To them is entrusted a work which is to turn the world upside down. None so exalted but the influence of this lowly company shall reach to them, until the throne of the Cæsars is claimed for Christ. None so far off but the power of this little band gathered in an upper room shall extend to them until the whole world is knit into a brotherhood! Not a force is there on the earth, either of men or devils, but they shall overcome it, until every knee shall bow to their Master, and every tongue shall confess that He is Lord.</p><p>A thing impossible, absurd, look at it as you will, until you admit this— they are to be filled with the Holy Spirit . Then difficulties melt into the empty air. Then there is no limit to their hopes, for there is no limit to their power. Their strength is not only \"as the strength of ten,\" it is as the strength of the Almighty.</p><p>This is Christ's idea of Christianity; the idea not of man—it is infinitely too sublime—the idea of God!— Mark Guy Pearse.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Acts 1:8"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 70,
        "title": "Those who remain in Me, and I in them, bear much fruit; apart from Me you can do nothing. John 15. 5.",
        "content": "<p>He that abides in Me, and I in him, the same brings forth much fruit: for without Me you can do nothing. John 15. 5.</p><p>Too much taken up with our work, we may forget our Master; it is possible to have the hand full, and the heart empty. Taken up with our Master we cannot forget our work; if the heart is filled with His love, how can the hands not be active in His service?— Adolphe Monod.</p>",
        "references": [
          "John 15:5"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 71,
        "title": "He that eateth Me, even he shall live by Me. John 6. 57.",
        "content": "<p>He that eats Me, even he shall live by Me. John 6. 57.</p><p>To feed on Christ is to get His strength into us to be our strength. You feed on the corn field, and the strength of the corn field comes into you, and is your strength. You feed on Christ, and then go and live your life; and it is Christ in you that lives your life, that helps the poor, that tells the truth, that fights the battles, and that wins the crown.— Phillips Brooks.</p>",
        "references": [
          "John 6:57"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 72,
        "title": "I sought Him, but I found Him not. Song of Sol. 3. 1.",
        "content": "<p>I sought Him, but I found Him not. Song of Sol. 3. 1.</p><p>Tell me where you lost the company of Christ, and I will tell you the most likely place to find Him. Have you lost Christ in the closet by restraining prayer? Then it is there you must seek and find Him. Did you lose Christ by sin? You will find Him in no other way than by the giving up of the sin, and seeking by the Holy Spirit to mortify the member in which the lust does dwell. Did you lose Christ by neglecting the Scriptures? You must find Him in the Scriptures. It is a true proverb, \"Look for a thing where you dropped it; it is there.\" So look for Christ where you lost Him, for He has not gone away.— Spurgeon.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Song of Solomon 3:1"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 73,
        "title": "Come behind in no gift. 1 Cor. 1. 7.",
        "content": "<p>Come behind in no gift. 1 Cor. 1. 7.</p><p>The Scripture gives four names to Christians, taken from the four cardinal graces so essential to man's salvation: Saints for their holiness, believers for their faith, brethren for their love, disciples for their knowledge.— Thomas Fuller.</p>",
        "references": [
          "1 Corinthians 1:7"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 74,
        "title": "They rest not day and night. Rev. 4. 8.",
        "content": "<p>They rest not day and night. Rev. 4. 8.</p><p>O blessed rest! When we rest not day and night, saying, \"Holy, holy, holy, Lord God Almighty!\"—when we shall rest from sin, but not from worship; from suffering and sorrow, but not from joy! O blessed day, when I shall rest with God; when I shall rest in knowing, loving, rejoicing, and praising; when my perfect soul and body shall together perfectly enjoy the most perfect God; when God, who is love itself, shall perfectly love me, and rest in His love to me, and I shall rest in my love to Him; when He shall rejoice over me with joy, and joy over me with singing, and I shall rejoice in Him!— Baxter.</p><p>They that wait upon the Lord shall renew their strength; they shall mount up with wings as eagles; they shall run, and not be weary, and they shall walk, and not faint. Isa. 40. 31.</p><p>The eagle that soars in the upper air does not worry itself as to how it is to cross rivers.— Selected.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Revelation 4:8",
          "Isaiah 40:31"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 75,
        "title": "Their eyes were holden. Luke 24. 16.",
        "content": "<p>Their eyes were holden. Luke 24. 16.</p><p>Editorial reflection: The travelers to Emmaus did not recognize Jesus while he walked beside them. His presence was real before they understood it. When a day feels uncertain, return to Scripture and prayer; ask the risen Lord to open your eyes to his companionship and direction.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Luke 24:16"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 76,
        "title": "Their eyes were opened. Luke 24. 31.",
        "content": "<p>Their eyes were opened. Luke 24. 31.</p><p>There is much precious significance in this. The Lord is often present in our lives in things that we do not dream possess any significance. We are asking God about something which needs His mighty working, and the very instrument by which He is to work is by our side, perhaps for weeks and months and years all unrecognized, until suddenly, some day it grows luminous and glorious with the very presence of the Lord, and becomes the mighty instrument of His victorious working. He loves to show His hand through the unexpected. Often he keeps us from seeing His way until just before He opens it, and then, immediately that it is unfolded, we find that He was walking by our side in the very thing, long before we even suspected its meaning.— A. B. Simpson.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Luke 24:31"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 77,
        "title": "All things work together for good to them that love God. Rom. 8. 28.",
        "content": "<p>All things work together for good to them that love God. Rom. 8. 28.</p><p>If our circumstances find us in God, we shall find God in all our circumstances.— Selected.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Romans 8:28"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 78,
        "title": "He leadeth me in the paths of righteousness for His name's sake. Ps. 23. 3.",
        "content": "<p>He leads me in the paths of righteousness for His name's sake. Ps. 23. 3.</p><p>He always has a purpose in His leading. He knows where the bits of green pasture are, and He would lead His flock to these. The way may be rough, but it is the right way to the pasture. \"Paths of righteousness\" may not be straight paths; but they are paths that lead somewhere—to the right place. Many desert paths are illusive. They start out clear and plain, but soon they are lost in the sands. They go nowhere. But the paths of righteousness have a goal to which they unerringly lead.— J. R. Miller.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Psalms 23:3"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 79,
        "title": "And he said, O my Lord, please send someone else. Ex. 4. 13.",
        "content": "<p>And he said, O my Lord, send, I ask you, by the hand of him whom you will send. Ex. 4. 13.</p><p>It was a very grudging assent. It was as much as to say, \"Since You are determined to send me and I must undertake the mission, then let it be so; but I would that it might have been another, and I go because I am compelled.\" So often do we shrink back from the sacrifice or obligation to which God calls us, that we think we are going to our doom. We seek every reason for evading the divine will, little realizing that He is forcing us out from our quiet homes into a career which includes, among other things, the song of victory on the banks of the Red Sea; the two lonely sojourns for forty days in converse with God; the shining face; the vision of glory; the burial by the hand of Michael; and the supreme honor of standing beside the Lord on the Transfiguration mount.— F. B. Meyer.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Exodus 4:13"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 80,
        "title": "Be careful how you live. Eph. 5. 15.",
        "content": "<p>See then that you walk circumspectly. Eph. 5. 15.</p><p>There is no such thing as negative influence. We are all positive in the place we occupy, making the world better or making it worse.— T. DeWitt Talmage.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Ephesians 5:15"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 81,
        "title": "She took for him an ark of bulrushes . . . and she laid it in the flags by the river's brink. Ex. 2. 3.",
        "content": "<p>She took for him an ark of bulrushes . . . and she laid it in the flags by the river's brink. Ex. 2. 3.</p><p>The mother of Moses laid the ark in the flags by the river's brink. Ay, but before doing so, she laid it on the heart of God! She could not have laid it so courageously upon the Nile, if she had not first devoutly laid it upon the care and love of God.</p><p>We are often surprised at the outward calmness of men who are called upon to do unpleasant and most trying deeds; but could we have seen them in secret, we should have known the moral preparation which they underwent before coming out to be seen by men. Be right in the sanctuary, if you would be right in the market-place. Be steadfast in prayer, if you would be calm in affliction. Start your race from the throne of God itself, if you would run well, and win the prize.— Joseph Parker.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Exodus 2:3"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 82,
        "title": "Carry one another's burdens, and so fulfill the law of Christ. Gal. 6. 2.",
        "content": "<p>Bear you one another's burdens, and so fulfil the law of Christ. Gal. 6. 2.</p><p>By lifting the burdens of others we lose our own.— Selected.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Galatians 6:2"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 83,
        "title": "I have finished the work you gave Me to do. John 17. 4.",
        "content": "<p>I have finished the work which You gave Me to do. John 17. 4.</p><p>Was the work of the Master indeed done? Was not its heaviest task yet to come? He had not yet met the dread hour of death. Why did He say that His work was done? It was because He knew that, when the will is given, the battle is ended. He was only in the shadows of the garden; but to conquer these shadows was already to conquer all. He who has willed to die has already triumphed over death. All that remains to Him is but the outer husk, the shell.</p><p>The cup which our Father gives us to drink is a cup for the will. It is easy for the lips to drain it when once the heart has accepted it. Not on the heights of Calvary, but in the shadows of Gethsemane is the cup presented; the act is easy after the choice. The real battle-field is in the silence of the spirit. Conquer there, and you are crowned.— George Matheson.</p>",
        "references": [
          "John 17:4"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 84,
        "title": "A great multitude . . . stood before the throne. Rev. 7. 9.",
        "content": "<p>A great multitude . . . stood before the throne. Rev. 7. 9.</p><p>A station on the feet in front of the throne in heaven is the effect of being often on the knees before the throne on earth .— Selected.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Revelation 7:9"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 85,
        "title": "God saw the light, that it was good: and God divided the light from the darkness. Gen. 1. 4.",
        "content": "<p>God saw the light, that it was good: and God divided the light from the darkness. Gen. 1. 4.</p><p><strong>Source and historical context:</strong> Spurgeon applies Genesis 1’s light imagery to Christian moral boundaries.</p><p><strong>Devotional focus:</strong> Genesis describes God bringing order to creation; it does not sort human beings into disposable camps. New Testament light imagery calls disciples to truth, mercy, and integrity. Moral discernment must not become contempt for people with different beliefs or backgrounds. Christians are called to witness through good works and neighbor love, not through dehumanization.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> Where does a useful moral boundary risk turning into judgment of another person’s worth?</p><p><strong>Practice:</strong> Practice one clear act of integrity alongside respectful engagement with someone different from you.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> God of light, guide my choices and keep my heart free from contempt.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Genesis 1:4"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 86,
        "title": "The path of the righteous is like the rising light that shines brighter and brighter until full daylight. Prov. 4. 18.",
        "content": "<p>The path of the just is as the shining light, that shines more and more to the perfect day. Prov. 4. 18.</p><p>Have I begun this path of heavenly love and knowledge now? Am I progressing in it? Do I feel some dawnings of the heavenly light, earnests and antepasts of the full day of glory? Let all God's dealings serve to quicken me in my way. Let every affection it may please Him to send, be as the moving pillar-cloud of old, beckoning me to move my tent onward, saying, \"Arise you and depart, for this is not your rest.\" Let me be often standing now on faith's lofty eminences, looking for \"the day of God\"—the rising sun which is to set no more in weeping clouds. Wondrous progression! How will all earth's learning, its boasted acquirements and eagle-eyed philosophy sink into the lispings of very infancy in comparison with this manhood of knowledge! Heaven will be the true \" Excelsior ,\" its song, \" a song of degrees ,\" Jesus leading His people from height to height of glory, and saying, as He said to Nathaniel, \" You will see greater things than these! \"— Macduff.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Proverbs 4:18"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 87,
        "title": "Take us the foxes, the little foxes, that spoil the vineyards; for our vineyards are in blossom. Song of Sol. 2. 15. (R. V.)",
        "content": "<p>Take us the foxes, the little foxes, that spoil the vineyards; for our vineyards are in blossom. Song of Sol. 2. 15. (R. V.)</p><p>How numerous the little foxes are! Little compromises with the world; disobedience to the still, small voice in little things; little indulgences of the flesh to the neglect of duty; little strokes of policy; doing evil in little things that good may come; and the beauty, and the fruitfulness of the vine are sacrificed!— J. Hudson Taylor.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Song of Solomon 2:15"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 88,
        "title": "The children of your Father which is in heaven. Matt. 5. 45.",
        "content": "<p>The children of your Father which is in heaven. Matt. 5. 45.</p><p>The best name by which we can think of God is Father. It is a loving, deep, sweet, heart-touching name, for the name of father is in its nature full of inborn sweetness and comfort. Therefore, also, we must confess ourselves children of God, for by this name we deeply touch our God, since there is not a sweeter sound to the father than the voice of the child.— Martin Luther.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Matthew 5:45"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 89,
        "title": "In the morning the word of the Lord came to me. Ezek. 12. 8.",
        "content": "<p>In the morning came the word of the Lord to me. Ezek. 12. 8.</p><p>A quiet hour spent alone with God at the beginning of the day is the best beginning for the toils and cares of active business. A brief season of prayer, looking above for wisdom and grace and strength, and seeking for an outpouring of the Holy Spirit, helps us to carry our religion into the business of the day. It brings joy and peace within the heart. And as we place all our concerns in the care and keeping of the Lord, faithfully striving to do His will, we have a joyful trust that however dark or discouraging events may appear, our Father's hand is guiding everything, and will give the wisest direction to all our toils.— Selected.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Ezekiel 12:8"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 90,
        "title": "The Lord God formed man out of the dust of the ground, and breathed into his nostrils the breath of life; and man became a living soul. Gen. 2. 7.",
        "content": "<p>The Lord God formed man out of the dust of the ground, and breathed into his nostrils the breath of life; and man became a living soul. Gen. 2. 7.</p><p>And so this soul of mine is a compound of two worlds—dust and deity! It touches the boundary line of two hemispheres. It is allied on one side to the divine; on the other, to the beast of the field. Its beginning is from beneath, but its culmination is from above; it is started from the dust of the ground, but it is finished in the breath of God.</p><p>My soul, are you living up to your twofold origin? Are you remembering your double parentage, and therefore your double duty? You have a duty to your God, for His breath is in you; you have a duty to the earth, for out of it were you taken.— George Matheson.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Genesis 2:7"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 91,
        "title": "Always rejoicing. 2 Cor. 6. 10.",
        "content": "<p>Always rejoicing. 2 Cor. 6. 10.</p><p>No Christian can ever know what is meant by those two little words, \"always rejoicing,\" but the Christian who takes up his cross and follows Jesus.— W. Hay Aitken.</p>",
        "references": [
          "2 Corinthians 6:10"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 92,
        "title": "All the land you see I will give to you and your descendants forever. Gen. 13. 15.",
        "content": "<p>All the land which you see, to you will I give it, and to your seed forever. Gen. 13. 15.</p><p>God's promises are ever on the ascending scale. One leads up to another, fuller and more blessed than itself. In Mesopotamia God said, \"I will show you the land.\" At Bethel, \"This is the land.\" Here, \"I will give you all the land, and children innumerable as the grains of sand.\" And we shall find even these eclipsed.</p><p>It is thus that God allures us to saintliness. Not giving anything till we have dared to act—that He may test us. Not giving everything at first—that He may not overwhelm us. And always keeping in hand an infinite reserve of blessing. Oh, the unexplored remainders of God! Whoever saw His last star?— F. B. Meyer.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Genesis 13:15"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 93,
        "title": "That night they caught nothing. John 21. 3.",
        "content": "<p>That night they caught nothing. John 21. 3.</p><p>God may let the sinful world succeed in their forbidden schemes, but, blessed be His name, He does not allow His chosen ones to prosper in the path which leads them out of His holy will! He has a storm to send after every Jonah, and an empty net for every unbelieving and inconsistent Simon.— A. B. Simpson.</p>",
        "references": [
          "John 21:3"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 94,
        "title": "They made me keeper of the vineyards; but mine own vineyard have I not kept. Song of Sol. i. 6.",
        "content": "<p>They made me keeper of the vineyards; but my own vineyard have I not kept. Song of Sol. i. 6.</p><p>Our attention is here drawn to a danger which is preeminently one of this day: the intense activity of our times may lead to zeal in service to the neglect of personal communion; but such neglect will not only lessen the value of the service, but tend to incapacitate us for the highest service.— J. Hudson Taylor.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Song of Solomon 1:6"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 95,
        "title": "We came to the land where you sent us . . . we saw the descendants of Anak there. Num. 13. 27, 28.",
        "content": "<p>We came to the land where you sent us . . . we saw the children of Anak there. Num. 13. 27, 28.</p><p>It is when we are in the way of duty that we find giants . It was when Israel was going forward that the giants appeared. When they turned back into the wilderness they found none.— Selected.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Numbers 13:27-28"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 96,
        "title": "Each one resembled the children of a king. Judg. 8. 18.",
        "content": "<p>Each one resembled the children of a king. Judg. 8. 18.</p><p>Frances Ridley Havergal says: \"If the King is indeed near of kin to us, the royal likeness will be recognizable.\"</p>",
        "references": [
          "Judges 8:18"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 97,
        "title": "He maketh me to lie down in green pastures: He leadeth me beside the still waters. Psa. 23. 2.",
        "content": "<p>He makes me to lie down in green pastures: He leads me beside the still waters. Psa. 23. 2.</p><p>This suggests the rest into which our Good Shepherd leads His flock. Life is not all toil. God gives us many quiet resting-places in our pilgrim way.</p><p>Night is one of these, when, after the day's toil, struggle, and exhaustion, we are led aside, and the curtains are drawn to shut out the noise, and He gives His beloved sleep, in sleep giving the wonderful blessings of renewal. The Sabbath is another of these quiet resting-places. God would have us drop our worldly tasks, and have a day for the refreshing of both body and soul. . . . Friendship's trysts are also quiet resting-places, where heart may commune with heart, where Jesus comes, too, unseen, and gives His blessing. All ordinances of Christian worship—seasons of prayer and devotion, hours of communion with God—are quiet resting-places.</p><p>Far more than we are apt to realize do we need these silent times in our busy life, needing them all the more the busier the life may be.— J. R. Miller.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Psalms 23:2"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 98,
        "title": "A daily rate for every day. 2 Kings 25. 30.",
        "content": "<p>A daily rate for every day. 2 Kings 25. 30.</p><p>One staff aids a traveler, but a bundle of staves is a heavy burden.— Spurgeon.</p>",
        "references": [
          "2 Kings 25:30"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 99,
        "title": "Carry one another's burdens, and so fulfill the law of Christ. Gal. 6. 2.",
        "content": "<p>Bear you one another's burdens, and so fulfil the law of Christ. Gal. 6. 2.</p><p>However perplexed you may at any hour become about some question of truth, one refuge and resource is always at hand: you can do something for some one beside yourself. At the times when you cannot see God, there is still open to you this sacred possibility, to show God: for it is the love and kindness of human hearts through which the divine reality comes home to men, whether they name it or not. Let this thought, then, stay with you: there may be times when you cannot find help, but there is no time when you cannot give help.— George Merriam.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Galatians 6:2"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 100,
        "title": "Work out your own salvation with fear and trembling: for it is God which worketh in you both to will and to do of His good pleasure. Phil. 2. 12, 13.",
        "content": "<p>Work out your own salvation with fear and trembling: for it is God which works in you both to will and to do of His good pleasure. Phil. 2. 12, 13.</p><p>It is not your business and mine to study whether we shall get to heaven, or even to study whether we shall be good men; it is our business to study how we shall come into the midst of the purposes of God and have the unspeakable privilege in these few years of doing something of His work.— Phillips Brooks.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Philippians 2:12-13"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 101,
        "title": "God . . . has shone in our hearts, giving us the light of the knowledge of God's glory in the face of Jesus Christ. 2 Cor. 4. 6.",
        "content": "<p>God . . . has shined in our hearts, to give the light of the knowledge of the glory of God in the face of Jesus Christ. 2 Cor. 4. 6.</p><p>Christian! rest not until you know the full, the unbroken shining of God in your heart. To this end, yield to every stirring of it that shows you some unconquered and perhaps unconquerable evil. Just bring it to the light; let the light shine upon it, and shine it out. Wait upon the Lord more than watchers for the morning, for \"the path of the just is as the shining light, shining more and more to the perfect day.\" Count upon it that God wants to fill you with the light of His glory: wait on Him more than watchers for the morning. \"Wait, I say, on the Lord.\"— Andrew Murray.</p>",
        "references": [
          "2 Corinthians 4:6"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 102,
        "title": "My soul, wait only for God. Psa. 62. 5.",
        "content": "<p>My soul, wait you only upon God. Psa. 62. 5.</p><p><strong>Source and historical context:</strong> An unattributed meditation urges listening as well as speaking in prayer.</p><p><strong>Devotional focus:</strong> Psalm 62 speaks of waiting for God, not of diagnosing why a prayer has gone unanswered. Silence can be restful or difficult. We should not tell people that distress, disability, grief, or depression means they failed to listen. Prayer may include speaking, waiting, seeking counsel, and taking the practical steps available. God’s care is not measured by a particular sensation.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> Can I approach a season of unanswered prayer without accusing myself or someone else?</p><p><strong>Practice:</strong> Spend a short quiet moment with Psalm 62:5 and write down a real concern and one possible next step.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Patient God, receive both my words and my silence. Help me persevere with honesty.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Psalms 62:5"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 103,
        "title": "Could you not stay awake with me for one hour? Matt. 26. 40.",
        "content": "<p>Could you not watch with me one hour? Matt. 26. 40.</p><p>Editorial reflection: Jesus asked his friends to stay awake with him in Gethsemane. Prayer is a way to remain present to God and to people under pressure. Give this hour your attention rather than a hurried promise: pause, listen, and pray for someone who needs courage.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Matthew 26:40"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 104,
        "title": "My meat is to do the will of Him that sent me. John 4. 34.",
        "content": "<p>My food is to do the will of Him that sent me. John 4. 34.</p><p>Seek your life's nourishment in your life's work.— Phillips Brooks.</p>",
        "references": [
          "John 4:34"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 105,
        "title": "It is God which worketh in you, both to will and to do of His good pleasure. Phil. 2. 13.",
        "content": "<p>It is God which works in you, both to will and to do of His good pleasure. Phil. 2. 13.</p><p>Full salvation is to realize that everything we see in Christ, our Example, may be ours, not by imitation, but by reproduction.— Selected.</p><p>Look, I am with you all the days. Matt, 28. 20. (R. V., margin.)</p><p>\" All the days \"—in winter days, when joys are fled; in sunless days, when the clouds return again and again after rain; in days of sickness and pain; in days of temptation and perplexity, as much as in days when the heart is as full of joy as the woodlands in spring are full of song. That day never comes when the Lord Jesus is not at the side of His saints. Lover and friend may stand afar, but He walks with them through the fires; He fords with them the rivers; He stands by them when face to face with the lion. We can never be alone. We must always add His resources to our own when making our calculations.— F. B. Meyer.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Philippians 2:13"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 106,
        "title": "Having . . . boldness to enter into the holiest by the blood of Jesus . . . let us draw near with a true heart. Heb. 10. 19, 22.",
        "content": "<p><strong>Read:</strong> Hebrews 10:19; Hebrews 10:22; Acts 27:23-25</p><p><strong>Source and historical context:</strong> The anthology contrasts the tabernacle’s inner sanctuary with access to God through Christ.</p><p><strong>Devotional focus:</strong> Hebrews interprets Christ’s priestly work through Israel’s sanctuary imagery. The Hebrew Bible itself also witnesses to God’s gracious nearness to Israel, prayer, covenant, and mercy. Christian confidence in Christ need not erase Jewish worship or misrepresent the Torah as only exclusion. Read Hebrews alongside the Psalms to appreciate both continuity and theological differences without disparagement.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> How can I describe my Christian convictions accurately while honoring Judaism’s own sacred tradition?</p><p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read Psalm 145 with Hebrews 10:19–22 and note how both speak of approaching God.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Holy God, teach me reverence, gratitude, and truthful respect for our neighbors.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Hebrews 10:19",
          "Hebrews 10:22",
          "Acts 27:23-25"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 107,
        "title": "They that wait upon the Lord shall . . . mount up with wings as eagles. Isa. 40. 31.",
        "content": "<p>They that wait upon the Lord shall . . . mount up with wings as eagles. Isa. 40. 31.</p><p><strong>Source and historical context:</strong> The historical writer likens trust in God to a bird flying above a trap.</p><p><strong>Devotional focus:</strong> Isaiah 40 promises strength for weary people and speaks honestly of exhaustion. Faith does not make us invulnerable or able to escape every harmful situation alone. God can strengthen us through friends, safe shelter, wise boundaries, and necessary professional help. The text invites hope during weakness, not blame when a person cannot soar above difficulty.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> What support would make endurance more possible for someone who feels trapped?</p><p><strong>Practice:</strong> Identify a safe and practical source of help rather than expecting willpower to solve everything.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Strengthening God, meet us in weariness and guide us toward safety and care.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Isaiah 40:31"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 108,
        "title": "Perfect love casteth out fear. 1 John 4. 18.",
        "content": "<p>Perfect love casts out fear. 1 John 4. 18.</p><p>Fear and love rise up in antagonism to each other as motives in life, like those two mountains from which respectively the blessings and curses of the old law were pronounced—the Mount of Cursing all barren, stony, without verdure and without water; the Mount of Blessing green and bright with many a flower, and blessed with many a trickling rill. Fear is barren. Love is fruitful. The one is a slave, and its work is little worth. The other is free, and its deeds are great and precious. From the blasted summit of the mountain which produces to bondage may be heard the words of the law; but the power to keep all these laws must be sought on the sunny hill where liberty dwells in love and gives energy to obedience. Therefore, if you would use in your own life the highest power that God has given us for our growth in grace, draw your arguments, not from fear, but from love.— Alex. McLaren.</p>",
        "references": [
          "1 John 4:18"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 109,
        "title": "The love of Christ constraineth us. 2 Cor. 5. 14.",
        "content": "<p>The love of Christ constrains us. 2 Cor. 5. 14.</p><p>The love of Christ is too large for any heart to hold it. It will overflow into others' hearts: it will give itself out, give itself away, for the enriching of other lives. The heart of Christ is a costly thing for any one to have. It will lead those who have it where it led Him. If it cost Him the cross, it will cost them no less.— J. M. Campbell.</p>",
        "references": [
          "2 Corinthians 5:14"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 110,
        "title": "I, the Lord your God, will hold your right hand, saying to you, Do not fear; I will help you. Isa. 41. 13.",
        "content": "<p>I the Lord your God will hold your right hand, saying to you, Fear not, I will help you. Isa. 41. 13.</p><p>Don't try to hold God's hand; let Him hold yours. Let Him do the holding , and you do the trusting .— H. W. Webb Peploe.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Isaiah 41:13"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 111,
        "title": "Consider the great things He has done for you. 1 Sam. 12. 24.",
        "content": "<p>Consider how great things He has done for you. 1 Sam. 12. 24.</p><p><strong>Source and historical context:</strong> The historical selection encourages remembrance of God’s goodness in Samuel’s appeal to Israel.</p><p><strong>Devotional focus:</strong> In 1 Samuel 12, the people are called to remember God’s acts and respond faithfully. Gratitude does not require relabeling injustice, bereavement, or loss as disguised gifts. Sometimes we cannot explain what happened, and remembering can include both good moments and painful truths. Faithful memory gives thanks without denying grief.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> Where can I give thanks without pretending that every past event was good?</p><p><strong>Practice:</strong> List one real kindness received and one loss you can honestly lament.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> God of memory, help me name gifts truthfully while honoring wounds that still need care.</p>",
        "references": [
          "1 Samuel 12:24",
          "Matthew 25:24-26"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 112,
        "title": "To Him be glory both now and forever. 2 Pet. 3. 18.",
        "content": "<p>To Him be glory both now and forever. 2 Pet. 3. 18.</p><p>Believer, you are anticipating the time when you shall join the saints above in ascribing all glory to Jesus; but are you glorifying Him now? The apostle's words are, \"To Him be glory both now and forever.\"— C. H. Spurgeon.</p>",
        "references": [
          "2 Peter 3:18"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 113,
        "title": "You will know that I am the Lord, for those who wait for me will not be ashamed. Isa. 49. 23.",
        "content": "<p>You shall know that I am the Lord: for they shall not be ashamed that wait for me. Isa. 49. 23.</p><p>J. Hudson Taylor says: \"Quiet waiting before God could save from many a mistake and from many a sorrow.\"</p>",
        "references": [
          "Isaiah 49:23"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 114,
        "title": "Let it be for you as you wish. Matt. 15. 28.",
        "content": "<p>Be it to you even as you will. Matt. 15. 28.</p><p>Oh, the victories of prayer! They are the mountain-tops of the Bible. They take us back to the plains of Mamre, to the fords of Peniel, to the prison of Joseph, to the triumphs of Moses, to the transcendent victories of Joshua, to the deliverances of David, to the miracles of Elijah and Elisha, to the whole story of the Master's life, to the secret of Pentecost, to the key-note of Paul's unparalleled ministry, to the lives of saints and the deaths of martyrs, to all that is most sacred and sweet in the history of the Church and the experience of the children of God. And when, for us, the last conflict shall have passed, and the footstool of prayer shall have given place to the harp of praise, the spots of time that shall be gilded with the most celestial and eternal radiance, shall be those, often linked with deepest sorrow and darkest night, over which we have the inscription, \"Jehovah-Shammah: The Lord was there!\"— A. B. Simpson.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Matthew 15:28"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 115,
        "title": "You are my God; I will seek you early. Psa. 63. 1.",
        "content": "<p>You are my God: early will I seek You. Psa. 63. 1.</p><p>In a world where there is so much to ruffle the spirit's plumes, how needful that entering into the secret of God's pavilion, which will alone bring it back to composure and peace! In a world where there is so much to sadden and depress, how blessed the communion with Him in whom is the one true source and fountain of all true gladness and abiding joy! In a world where so much is ever seeking to unhallow our spirits, to render them common and profane, how high the privilege of consecrating them anew in prayer to holiness and to God.— Archbishop Trench.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Psalms 63:1"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 116,
        "title": "In Him was life; and the life was the light of men. John 1. 4. You are the light of the world. Matt. 5. 14.",
        "content": "<p>In Him was life; and the life was the light of men. John 1. 4. You are the light of the world. Matt. 5. 14.</p><p>In the light we can walk and work. We walk in the light and become entirely children of light. We let our light, the light of God, shine, so that men may see our good works, and glorify our Father in heaven. Gently, silently, lovingly, unceasingly, we give ourselves to transmit the light and the love God so unceasingly shines into us. Our one work is to wait, and admit, and then transmit the light of God in Christ.— Andrew Murray.</p>",
        "references": [
          "John 1:4",
          "Matthew 5:14"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 117,
        "title": "Be steadfast, immovable, always doing the Lord's work abundantly. 1 Cor. 15. 58.",
        "content": "<p>Be you steadfast, unmovable, always abounding in the work of the Lord. 1 Cor. 15. 58.</p><p>Activity in doing good is one recipe for being cheerful Christians; it is like exercise to the body, and it keeps the soul in health.— Bishop Ryle.</p>",
        "references": [
          "1 Corinthians 15:58"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 118,
        "title": "Looking up to heaven He sighed. Mark 7. 34.",
        "content": "<p>Looking up to heaven He sighed. Mark 7. 34.</p><p>Too often we sigh and look within; Jesus sighed and looked without. We sigh, and look down; Jesus sighed, and looked up. We sigh, and look to earth; Jesus sighed, and looked to heaven. We sigh, and look to man; Jesus sighed, and looked to God.— Stork.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Mark 7:34"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 119,
        "title": "We glory in tribulations. Rom. 5. 3.",
        "content": "<p>We glory in tribulations. Rom. 5. 3.</p><p>Have you ever thought that some day you will never have anything to try you or anybody to vex you again?— A. B. Simpson.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Romans 5:3"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 120,
        "title": "Set your affection on things above, not on things on the earth. Col. 3. 2.",
        "content": "<p>Set your affection on things above, not on things on the earth. Col. 3. 2.</p><p>He who has his affections set on things above is like one who hangs on by the skies; and, having a secure hold of these, could say, though he saw the world roll away from beneath his feet, \"My heart is fixed; my heart is fixed; O Lord, I will sing and give praise!\"— Guthrie.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Colossians 3:2"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 121,
        "title": "The Lord has indeed risen and has appeared to Simon. Luke 24. 34.",
        "content": "<p>The Lord is risen indeed, and has appeared to Simon. Luke 24. 34.</p><p>Editorial reflection: The risen Jesus met Simon personally before Peter spoke publicly at Pentecost. Witness grows from an encounter with Christ, not merely from finding the right words. Spend time with the Lord before trying to speak for him, and ask for a truthful and gentle voice.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Luke 24:34"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 122,
        "title": "They . . . gladly received Peter's word; about three thousand people joined them that day. Acts 2. 41.",
        "content": "<p>They . . . gladly received (Peter's) word; and the same day there were added to them about three thousand souls. Acts 2. 41.</p><p>Before the Lord can use us in His service we must have close individual dealing with Himself. He always will have to do in secret with that soul that He intends to use in blessing others.</p><p>Do you want to speak for Jesus to those around you? Then you must go to Jesus Himself for your message. What you say for Jesus must be got from Jesus.</p><p>Oh, how much breath falls powerless on every side because it has not been inhaled in the sanctuary! We want more secret dealing with the living God. We run without being sent: we speak before God has spoken to us: no wonder we so often fail. Oh, what secret prayer and what heart-searching discipline the heart needs before God can use it!— F. Whitfield.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Acts 2:41"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 123,
        "title": "The righteous eateth to the satisfying of his soul. Prov. 13. 25.",
        "content": "<p>The righteous eats to the satisfying of his soul. Prov. 13. 25.</p><p><strong>Source and historical context:</strong> Frances Ridley Havergal spiritualizes Proverbs 13:25 into a judgment about anyone who feels unsatisfied.</p><p><strong>Devotional focus:</strong> Proverbs 13 concerns the realities of provision and consequences, not a test proving dissatisfaction is a person’s fault. Hunger, loneliness, spiritual dryness, and depression require attentive care, not blame. Jesus’ invitation to come to him is generous, and the community is called to share actual bread as well as words of hope. Honest need is not spiritual failure.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> What need have I dismissed because I assumed it proved weak faith?</p><p><strong>Practice:</strong> Offer or request one concrete form of care, and read the proverb in its wider chapter.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Compassionate God, teach us to notice real needs and respond without judgment.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Proverbs 13:25"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 124,
        "title": "The Lord disciplines those He loves. Heb. 12. 6.",
        "content": "<p>Whom the Lord loves He chastens. Heb. 12. 6.</p><p><strong>Source and historical context:</strong> A historical meditation applies Hebrews 12:6 to illness and hardship using the image of a chariot.</p><p><strong>Devotional focus:</strong> Hebrews speaks of belonging, perseverance, and loving formation. It should not be used to declare that abuse, disease, or disaster was sent by God to improve someone. Some suffering is preventable, and people deserve protection and treatment. Spiritual growth may happen during hardship, but God’s love is not measured by the amount of pain a person experiences.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> Have I explained another person’s suffering too quickly instead of offering help?</p><p><strong>Practice:</strong> Ask a hurting neighbor what would make today safer or easier.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> God of compassion, form us in love without making harm a lesson we must accept.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Hebrews 12:6"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 125,
        "title": "The blood of Jesus Christ, His Son, cleanseth us from all sin. 1 John 1. 7.",
        "content": "<p>The blood of Jesus Christ, His Son, cleanses us from all sin. 1 John 1. 7.</p><p>Learn a lesson from the eye of the miner, who all day long is working amid the flying coal dust. When he emerges in the light of day his face may be grimy enough; but his eyes are clear and lustrous, because the fountain of tears in the lachrymal gland is ever pouring its gentle tides over the eye, cleansing away each speck of dust as soon as it alights.</p><p>Is not this the miracle of cleansing which our spirits need in such a world as this? And this is what our blessed Lord is prepared to do for us by His cleansing blood, if only we will trust Him.— F. B. Meyer.</p>",
        "references": [
          "1 John 1:7"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 126,
        "title": "Do whatever He tells you. John 2. 5.",
        "content": "<p>Whatever He says to you, do it. John 2. 5.</p><p>Florence Nightingale said: \"If I could give you information of my life, it would be to show how a woman of very ordinary ability has been led by God in strange and unaccustomed paths to do in His service what He has done in her. And if I could tell you all, you would see how God has done all, and I nothing. I have worked hard, very hard, that is all; and I have never refused God anything.\"</p>",
        "references": [
          "John 2:5"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 127,
        "title": "I know how to abound. Phil. 4. 12.",
        "content": "<p>I know how to abound. Phil. 4. 12.</p><p>It is a dangerous thing to be prosperous. The crucible of adversity is a less severe trial to the Christian than the refining-pot of prosperity. It needs more than human skill to carry the brimming cup of mortal joy with a steady hand; yet Paul had learned that skill, for he declares, \"In all things I am instructed both to be full and to be hungry.\" When we have much of God's providential mercies it often happens that we have but little of God's grace; satisfied with earth, we are content to do without heaven. Rest assured, it is harder to know how to be full than it is to know how to be hungry, so desperate is the tendency of human nature to pride and forgetfulness of God. Take care that you ask in your prayers that God would teach you \"how to be full.\"— Spurgeon.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Philippians 4:12"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 128,
        "title": "Whosoever exalteth himself shall be abased; and he that humbleth himself shall be exalted. Luke 14. 11.",
        "content": "<p>Whoever exalts himself shall be abased; and he that humbles himself shall be exalted. Luke 14. 11.</p><p>. . . If you ask the way to the crown—'tis by the cross; to the mountain—'tis by the valley; to exaltation 'tis he that humbles himself.— J. H. Evans.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Luke 14:11"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 129,
        "title": "For their sakes I sanctify myself, that they also might be sanctified through the truth. John 17. 19.",
        "content": "<p>For their sakes I sanctify myself, that they also might be sanctified through the truth. John 17. 19.</p><p>Do you remember, when Jesus was sitting with His disciples at the last supper, how He lifted up His voice and prayed, and in the midst of His prayer there came these wondrous words: \"For their sakes I sanctify myself, that they also might be sanctified\"? Is there anything in all the teachings that man has had from the lips of God that is nobler, that is more far-reaching than that—to be my best not simply for my own sake, but for the sake of the world? You can help your fellow-men—you must help your fellow-men; but the only way you can help them is by being the noblest and the best man that it is possible for you to be.— Phillips Brooks.</p>",
        "references": [
          "John 17:19"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 130,
        "title": "He that is slow to anger is better than the mighty; and he that ruleth his spirit than he that taketh a city. Prov. 16. 32.",
        "content": "<p>He that is slow to anger is better than the mighty; and he that rules his spirit than he that takes a city. Prov. 16. 32.</p><p>More dear in the sight of God and His angels than any other conquest is the conquest of self, which each man, with the help of heaven, can secure for himself.— Dean Stanley.</p><p>For this child I prayed, and the Lord has given me my petition which I asked of Him: therefore also I have lent him to the Lord; as long as he lives he shall be lent to the Lord. 1 Sam. 1. 27, 28.</p><p>God sometimes bestows gifts just that love may have something to renounce. The things that He puts into our hands are possibly put there that we may have the opportunity of showing what is in our heart. Oh, that there were in us a fervor of love that would lead us to examine everything that belongs to us, to ascertain how it might be made a means of showing our affection to Christ!— George Bowen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Proverbs 16:32",
          "1 Samuel 1:27-28"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 131,
        "title": "Seek God's kingdom and His righteousness first, and all these things will be given to you. Matt. 6. 33.",
        "content": "<p>Seek you first the kingdom of God and His righteousness, and all these things shall be added to you. Matt. 6. 33.</p><p>We need have only one care, that we put the first thing first—faithfulness to God. Then all else we need for both worlds will be supplied. God will never fail us; but we forget, sometimes, in our rejoicing over such an assurance, that we must fulfil our part if we would claim the divine promise.</p><p>It will not always be easy. Tomorrow it may mean a distasteful task, a disagreeable duty, a costly sacrifice for one who does not seem worthy. Life is full of sore testings of our willingness to follow the Good Shepherd. We have not the slightest right to claim this assurance unless we have taken Christ as the guide of our life.— J. R. Miller.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Matthew 6:33"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 132,
        "title": "His praise shall continually be in my mouth. Psa. 34. 1.",
        "content": "<p>His praise shall continually be in my mouth. Psa. 34. 1.</p><p>Let not your praises be transient—a fit of music, and then the instrument hung by the wall till another gaudy day of some remarkable providence makes you take it down. God comes not guestwise to His saints' house, but to dwell with them. David took this up for a life work: \"As long as I live, I will praise you.\"— Gurnall.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Psalms 34:1"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 133,
        "title": "I am not able to bear all this people alone, because it is too heavy for me. Num. 11. 14.",
        "content": "<p><strong>Read:</strong> Numbers 11:14; Isaiah 6:5</p><p><strong>Source and historical context:</strong> A writer signing C. H. M. comments on Moses’ admission that responsibility is too heavy.</p><p><strong>Devotional focus:</strong> Numbers 11 records Moses reaching the limit of what he can bear; God answers by sharing leadership. This story does not shame burnout or guarantee that every task will succeed. A faithful person may need rest, colleagues, changed expectations, or professional support. Asking for help is often an act of honest leadership, not evidence of disobedience.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> Which responsibility has become too heavy to carry alone?</p><p><strong>Practice:</strong> Name one task to delegate or one trustworthy person to ask for assistance.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Wise God, teach me to serve responsibly and welcome the help I need.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Numbers 11:14",
          "Isaiah 6:5"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 134,
        "title": "While I was musing the fire burned. Psa. 39. 3.",
        "content": "<p>While I was musing the fire burned. Psa. 39. 3.</p><p>My soul, if you would muse more, the fire would burn more. Why do you not retire oftener with yourself? You would be better fitted for the world if you were less worldly. If you had more heavenly fire you would have more earthly power.</p><p>Is there no secret pavilion into which you can go and warm yourself? Is there no holy of holies where you can catch a glow of impulse that will make you strong? Is it not written of the Son of Man that \"as He prayed the fashion of His countenance was altered\"? Yes; it was from His prayer that His transfigured glory came. It was from the glow of His heart that there issued the glow of His countenance. It was when He was musing that the fire kindled.</p><p>O my soul, would you have your life glorified, beautified, transfigured to the eyes of men? Get you up into the secret place of God's pavilion, where the fires of love are burning. Your life shall shine gloriously to the dwellers on the plain. Your prayers shall be luminous; they shall light your face like the face of Moses when he knew not that it shone. Your words shall be burning; they will kindle many a heart journeying on the road to Emmaus. Your path shall be lambent; when you have prayed in Elijah's solitude you will have Elijah's chariot of fire.— George Matheson.</p><p>Whoever shall give to drink to one of these little ones a cup of cold water only in the name of a disciple, truly I say to you, he shall in nowise lose his reward. Matt. 10. 42.</p><p>We are in danger of looking too far for opportunities of doing good and communicating. In reaching for rhododendrons we trample down the daisies.— Selected.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Psalms 39:3",
          "Matthew 10:42"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 135,
        "title": "Hide yourself by the brook. 1 Kings 17. 3.",
        "content": "<p>Hide yourself by the brook. 1 Kings 17. 3.</p><p><strong>Source and historical context:</strong> F. Whitfield reflects on Elijah receiving water from a brook that later dries up.</p><p><strong>Devotional focus:</strong> Elijah’s story includes both unexpected provision and real scarcity. It does not explain every family’s poverty or lost opportunity as God’s strategy to prevent idolatry. Material hardship can call for solidarity and practical action. Gifts can be received with gratitude, and lack can be named honestly without accusing the person who suffers.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> Where have I explained scarcity instead of helping address it?</p><p><strong>Practice:</strong> Share a resource or help someone find local support where an actual need exists.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Provider God, keep our gratitude humble and our care for others practical.</p>",
        "references": [
          "1 Kings 17:3"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 136,
        "title": "His kingdom ruleth over all. Psa. 103. 19.",
        "content": "<p>His kingdom rules over all. Psa. 103. 19.</p><p><strong>Source and historical context:</strong> Mark Guy Pearse celebrates divine sovereignty in Psalm 103.</p><p><strong>Devotional focus:</strong> Psalm 103 praises God’s justice and compassion, especially toward people who are oppressed. Divine sovereignty should not be used to praise cruelty or excuse preventable harm. Scripture makes room for lament and calls people to resist wrongdoing. We can trust God amid events we do not understand while naming injustice plainly and helping people find protection.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> Have I used a statement about providence to minimize someone’s pain?</p><p><strong>Practice:</strong> Pray for a specific injustice and take one realistic step to support those affected.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Righteous God, teach me to praise your mercy and to stand against harm.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Psalms 103:19"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 137,
        "title": "If we suffer we shall also reign with Him. 2 Tim. 2. 12.",
        "content": "<p>If we suffer we shall also reign with Him. 2 Tim. 2. 12.</p><p>The highest bidder for the crown of glory is the lowliest wearer of the cross of self-denial.— A. J. Gordon.</p>",
        "references": [
          "2 Timothy 2:12"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 138,
        "title": "Guard your heart carefully, for life flows from it. Prov. 4. 23.",
        "content": "<p>Keep your heart with all diligence: for out of it are the issues of life. Prov. 4. 23.</p><p>He who would keep his heart pure and holy, must plant a sentinel at every avenue by which sin may find access there, guarding against none more than the \"little\" sins, as they are called.</p><p>The man of God has his eyes to keep, and so Job said, \"I have made a covenant with my eyes\"—his tongue , and hence the exhortation, \"Keep your tongue from evil, and your lips from speaking guile\"—his ears , and hence the warning, \"Cease, my son, to hear the instruction that causes to err\"—his feet , and hence David says, \"I have refrained my feet from every evil way, that I might keep Your word.\" And since there is no gate of the five senses by which the enemy may not come in like a flood, unless the Spirit lift up a standard against him, we have need to guard every port, and write over every portal, \"Here there enters nothing to hurt or to defile.\"— Guthrie.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Proverbs 4:23"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 139,
        "title": "Whatever you do, . . . do everything in the name of the Lord Jesus. Col. 3. 17.",
        "content": "<p>Whatever you do, . . . do all in the name of the Lord Jesus. Col. 3. 17.</p><p>Do little things as if they were great, because of the majesty of the Lord Jesus Christ, who dwells in you; and do great things as if they were little and easy, because of His omnipotence.— Pascal.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Colossians 3:17"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 140,
        "title": "Him they compelled to bear His cross. Matt. 27. 32.",
        "content": "<p>Him they compelled to bear His cross. Matt. 27. 32.</p><p><strong>Source and historical context:</strong> W. Hay Aitken interprets Simon of Cyrene carrying Jesus’ cross as a lesson about willing suffering.</p><p><strong>Devotional focus:</strong> Matthew tells how Simon was compelled to carry Jesus’ cross during the crucifixion. This scene does not teach that people must remain in unsafe relationships or that fleeing danger makes God punish them with more pain. Christian discipleship includes courageous love and can include setting boundaries or escaping violence. We should not turn another person’s suffering into a test of obedience.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> What distinction should I make between costly love and preventable harm?</p><p><strong>Practice:</strong> If a situation is unsafe, prioritize help and safety rather than treating exposure to harm as a duty.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Jesus, give us courage to love wisely and defend the vulnerable.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Matthew 27:32"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 141,
        "title": "Our Lord Jesus Christ . . . gave Himself for our sins that He might deliver us from this present evil world. Gal. 1. 4.",
        "content": "<p>Our Lord Jesus Christ . . . gave Himself for our sins that He might deliver us from this present evil world. Gal. 1. 4.</p><p>Attachment to Christ is the only secret of detachment from the world.— A. J. Gordon.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Galatians 1:4"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 142,
        "title": "You are the light of the world. A city that is set on an hill cannot be hid. Matt. 5. 14.",
        "content": "<p>You are the light of the world. A city that is set on an hill cannot be hid. Matt. 5. 14.</p><p>Lamps do not talk, but they do shine. A lighthouse sounds no drum, it beats no gong; and yet far over the waters its friendly spark is seen by the mariner. So let your actions shine out your religion. Let the main sermon of your life be illustrated by all your conduct.— Spurgeon.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Matthew 5:14"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 143,
        "title": "Without me you can do nothing. John 15. 5.",
        "content": "<p>Without me you can do nothing. John 15. 5.</p><p>Editorial reflection: Jesus calls his disciples to abide in him, as branches depend on a vine. This is a promise of living dependence, not a demand to manufacture strength alone. Before today's work, ask Christ for the patience and love you cannot produce by willpower alone.</p>",
        "references": [
          "John 15:5"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 144,
        "title": "I can do all things, through Christ which strengtheneth me. Phil. 4. 13.",
        "content": "<p>I can do all things, through Christ which strengthens me. Phil. 4. 13.</p><p><strong>Source and historical context:</strong> F. B. Meyer promises unlimited ability to believers abiding in Christ.</p><p><strong>Devotional focus:</strong> Paul writes Philippians 4:13 while describing both plenty and want. His claim is about strength to face changing circumstances, not unlimited achievement or an obligation to bear abuse. We may still have limits, need help, and encounter failure. Christ’s sustaining presence enables perseverance and humility; it does not make planning, boundaries, or rest unnecessary.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> Where have I treated a verse about contentment as a demand for limitless capacity?</p><p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read Philippians 4:10–13 and name one realistic limit you should honor.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Christ our strength, sustain me in plenty and need, and teach me to accept wise limits.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Philippians 4:13"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 145,
        "title": "As my Father has sent me, so I send you. John 20. 21.",
        "content": "<p>As my Father has sent me, even so send I you. John 20. 21.</p><p>We should never leave our room until we have seen the face of our dear Master, Christ, and have realized that we are being sent forth by Him to do His will, and to finish the work which He has given us to do. He who said to His immediate followers, \"As my Father has sent me, even so send I you,\" says as much to each one of us, as the dawn summons us to live another day. We should realize that we are as much sent forth by Him as the angels who \"do His commandments, hearkening to the voice of His word.\" There is some plan for each day's work, which He will unfold to us, if only we will look up to Him to do so; some mission to fulfil; some ministry to perform; some lesson patiently to learn, that we may be able to \"reach others also.\" As to our plans we need not be anxious; because He who sends us forth is responsible to make the plan, according to His infinite wisdom; and to reveal it to us, however dull and stupid our faculties may be. And as to our sufficiency, we are secure of having all needful grace; because He never sends us forth, except He first breathes on us and says, \"Receive you the Holy Spirit.\" There is always a special endowment for special power.— F. B. Meyer.</p>",
        "references": [
          "John 20:21"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 146,
        "title": "A fountain . . . for sin and for uncleanness. Zech. 13. 1.",
        "content": "<p>A fountain . . . for sin and for uncleanness. Zech. 13. 1.</p><p>You that have faith in the Fountain, frequent it . Beware of two errors which are very natural and very disastrous. Beware of thinking any sin too great for it; beware of thinking any sin too small. There is not a sin so little, but it may be the germ of everlasting perdition; there is not a sin so enormous, but a drop of atoning blood will wash it away as utterly as if it were drowned in the depths of the sea.— James Hamilton.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Zechariah 13:1"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 147,
        "title": "I am black . . . as the tents of Kedar. Song of Sol. i. 5.",
        "content": "<p>I am black . . . as the tents of Kedar. Song of Sol. i. 5.</p><p>Editorial reflection: Song of Songs gives voice to a beloved person who is seen and loved. The historical selection pairs this image with the assurance that the Lord knows us fully and still loves us. Bring what you would rather hide into honest prayer and receive his mercy without ranking anyone's worth by appearance.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Song of Solomon 1:5"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 148,
        "title": "I am my beloved's, and his desire is toward me. Song of Sol. 7. 10.",
        "content": "<p>I am my beloved's, and his desire is toward me. Song of Sol. 7. 10.</p><p>Nothing humbles the soul like sacred and intimate communion with the Lord; yet there is a sweet joy in feeling that He knows all , and, notwithstanding, loves us still.— J. Hudson Taylor.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Song of Solomon 7:10"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 149,
        "title": "David enquired of the Lord. 2 Sam. 5. 19.",
        "content": "<p>David enquired of the Lord. 2 Sam. 5. 19.</p><p>Christian, if you would know the path of duty, take God for your compass; if you would steer your ship through the dark billows, put the tiller into the hand of the Almighty. Many a rock might be escaped if we would let our Father take the helm; many a shoal or quicksand we might well avoid if we would leave it to His sovereign will to choose and to command. The Puritan said, \"As sure as ever a Christian carves for himself he'll cut his own fingers.\" \"I will instruct you and teach you in the way which you will go,\" is God's promise to His people. Let us, then, take all our perplexities to Him and say, \"Lord, what will you have me to do?\" Leave not your chamber this morning without enquiring of the Lord .— Spurgeon.</p><p>A certain man . . . who never had walked . . . heard Paul speak: who . . . perceiving that he had faith to be healed, said . . . Stand upright on your feet. And he leaped and walked. Acts 14. 8, 9, 10.</p><p>Where true faith is, it will induce obedience and where it does induce obedience, it will always, in one form or another, bring a blessing.— W. Hay Aitken.</p><p>Then said Martha to Jesus, Lord . . . I know that . . . whatever you will ask of God, God will give it you. Jesus says to her, Your brother shall rise again. Martha says to Him, I know that he shall rise again in the resurrection at the last day. John 11. 21, 22, 23, 24.</p><p>Beware, in your prayer, above everything, of limiting God, not only by unbelief, but by fancying that you know what He can do. Expect unexpected things, above all that we ask or think. Each time you intercede, be quiet first and worship God in His glory. Think of what He can do, of how He delights to hear Christ, of your place in Christ; and expect great things.— Andrew Murray.</p>",
        "references": [
          "2 Samuel 5:19",
          "Acts 14:8-10",
          "John 11:21-24"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 150,
        "title": "As many of you as have been baptized into Christ have put on Christ. Gal. 3. 27.",
        "content": "<p>As many of you as have been baptized into Christ have put on Christ. Gal. 3. 27.</p><p>Not simply the righteousness of our Savior, not simply the beauty of His holiness or the graces of His character, are we to put on as a garment. The Lord Himself is our clothing. Every Christian is not only a Christ-bearer but a Christ-wearer. We are so to enter into Him by communion, to be so endued with His presence, and embued with His Spirit that men shall see Him when they see us, as they see our garments when they look upon our bodies.— A. J. Gordon.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Galatians 3:27"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 151,
        "title": "You will never wash my feet. John 13. 8.",
        "content": "<p>You will never wash my feet. John 13. 8.</p><p>Whatever hinders us from receiving a blessing that God is willing to bestow upon us is not humility, but the mockery of it. A genuine humility will ever feel the need of the largest measures of grace, and will be perfected just in the degree in which that grace is bestowed. The truly humble man will seek to be filled with all the fulness of God, knowing that when so filled there is not the slightest place for pride or for self.— George Bowen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "John 13:8"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 152,
        "title": "Cast your burden on the Lord, and He will sustain you. Psa. 55. 22.",
        "content": "<p>Cast your burden upon the Lord, and He shall sustain you. Psa. 55. 22.</p><p>He that takes his own cares upon himself loads himself with an uneasy burden. The fear of what may come, expectation of what will come, desire of what will not come, and the inability to redress all these, must bring him continual torment. I will cast my cares upon God: He has bidden me. They cannot hurt Him: He can redress them.— Hall.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Psalms 55:22"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 153,
        "title": "Well done, good and faithful servant. . . . You wicked and lazy servant. Matt. 25. 21, 26.",
        "content": "<p>Well done, good and faithful servant. . . . You wicked and slothful servant. Matt. 25. 21, 26.</p><p>God holds us responsible not for what we have , but for what we might have; not for what we are , but for what we might be.— Mark Guy Pearse.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Matthew 25:21",
          "Matthew 25:26"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 154,
        "title": "Jesus constrained His disciples to get into a ship. Matt. 14. 22.",
        "content": "<p>Jesus constrained His disciples to get into a ship. Matt. 14. 22.</p><p><strong>Source and historical context:</strong> George Matheson parallels a disappointing venture with Jesus’ disciples at sea.</p><p><strong>Devotional focus:</strong> Matthew’s Gospel says Jesus sent the disciples across the lake and met them amid a storm. It does not authorize us to identify God as the cause of every personal disaster or failed project. Sometimes wise choices produce hard outcomes and sometimes course corrections are needed. Christ’s compassion invites courage and discernment without requiring us to call a dangerous storm a gift.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> What disappointment needs a careful response instead of a quick explanation?</p><p><strong>Practice:</strong> Discuss one setback with a trusted person and consider what safety or learning it requires.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Lord Jesus, meet us in storms and help us make wise decisions when plans change.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Matthew 14:22"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 155,
        "title": "Work to show yourself approved by God, a worker who need not be ashamed and who handles the word of truth rightly. 2 Tim. 2. 15.",
        "content": "<p>Study to show yourself approved to God, a workman that needs not to be ashamed, rightly dividing the word of truth. 2 Tim. 2. 15.</p><p>Have your tools ready; God will find you work.— Charles Kingsley.</p>",
        "references": [
          "2 Timothy 2:15"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 156,
        "title": "Come out from among them and be separate. 2 Cor. 6. 17.",
        "content": "<p>Come out from among them, and be you separate. 2 Cor. 6. 17.</p><p>With all the world in his choice, God placed His ancient people in a very remarkable situation. On the north they were walled in by the snowy ranges of Lebanon; a barren desert formed their eastern boundary; far to the south stretched a sterile region, called the howling wilderness; while the sea—not then, as now, the highway of the nations, facilitating rather than impeding intercourse—lay on their west, breaking on a shore that had few harbors and no navigable rivers to invite the steps of commerce.</p><p>May we not find a great truth in the very position in which God placed His chosen people? It certainly teaches us that to be holy, or sanctified, we must be a separate people—living in the world, but not of it—as oil, that may be mixed, but cannot be combined with water.— Guthrie.</p>",
        "references": [
          "2 Corinthians 6:17"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 157,
        "title": "I am with you and will keep you wherever you go, and I will bring you back to this land. Gen. 28. 15.",
        "content": "<p>I am with you, and will keep you in all places where you go, and will bring you again into this land. Gen. 28. 15.</p><p>\"With you,\" companionship; \"Keep you,\" guardianship; \"Bring you,\" guidance.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Genesis 28:15"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 158,
        "title": "I have appointed you . . . to bring salvation to the ends of the earth. Acts 13. 47.",
        "content": "<p>I have set you . . . that you should be for salvation to the ends of the earth. Acts 13. 47.</p><p>Editorial reflection: The mission in Acts reaches beyond one place or people. A faithful witness begins wherever we are and points toward God's welcome for the nations. Pray for someone outside your usual circle, then consider one concrete act of hospitality or service.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Acts 13:47"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 159,
        "title": "You will be my witnesses . . . to the ends of the earth. Acts 1. 8.",
        "content": "<p>You shall be witnesses to me . . . to the uttermost parts of the earth. Acts 1. 8.</p><p>Men are questioning now, as they never have questioned before, whether Christianity is, indeed, the true religion which is to be the salvation of the world. Christian men, it is for us to give our bit of answer to that question. It is for us, in whom the Christian church is at this moment partially embodied, to declare that Christianity, that the Christian faith, the Christian manhood can do that for the world which the world needs.</p><p>You ask, \"What can I do?\"</p><p>You can furnish one Christian life. You can furnish a life so faithful to every duty, so ready for every service, so determined not to commit every sin, that the great Christian church shall be the stronger for your living in it, and the problem of the world be answered, and a certain great peace come into this poor, perplexed, phase of our humanity as it sees that new revelation of what Christianity is.— Phillips Brooks.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Acts 1:8"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 160,
        "title": "I know whom I have believed. 2 Tim. 1. 12.",
        "content": "<p>I know whom I have believed. 2 Tim. 1. 12.</p><p>Personal acquaintance with Christ is a living thing. Like a tree that uses every hour for growth, it thrives in sunshine, it is refreshed by rain—even the storm drives it to fasten its grip more firmly in the earth for its support. So, troubled heart, in all experience, say, \"This comes that I may make closer acquaintance with my Lord.\"— Selected.</p>",
        "references": [
          "2 Timothy 1:12"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 161,
        "title": "Wait for the promise of the Father. Acts 1. 4.",
        "content": "<p>Wait for the promise of the Father. Acts 1. 4.</p><p>When the day of Pentecost was fully come, they were all with one accord in one place . . . and they were all filled with the Holy Spirit. Acts 2. 1, 4.</p><p>Obedience to a divine prompting transforms it into a permanent acquisition.— F. B. Meyer.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Acts 1:4",
          "Acts 2:1",
          "Acts 2:4"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 162,
        "title": "We have come to know and believe the love God has for us. 1 John 4. 16.",
        "content": "<p>We have known and believed the love that God has to us. 1 John 4. 16.</p><p>The secret of walking closely with Christ, and working successfully for Him, is to fully realize that we are His beloved. Let us but feel that He has set His heart upon us, that He is watching us from those heavens with tender interest, that He is working out the mystery of our lives with solicitude and fondness, that He is following us day by day as a mother follows her babe in his first attempt to walk alone, that He has set His love upon us, and, in spite of ourselves, is working out for us His highest will and blessing, as far as we will let Him, and then nothing can discourage us. Our hearts will glow with responsive love. Our faith will spring to meet His mighty promises, and our sacrifices shall become the very luxuries of love for one so dear. This was the secret of John's spirit. \"We have known and believed the love that God has to us.\" And the heart that has fully learned this has found the secret of unbounded faith and enthusiastic service.— A. B. Simpson.</p>",
        "references": [
          "1 John 4:16"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 163,
        "title": "Endure . . . as a good soldier of Jesus Christ. 2 Tim. 2. 3.",
        "content": "<p>Endure . . . as a good soldier of Jesus Christ. 2 Tim. 2. 3.</p><p>Life is not victory, but battle. Be patient a little longer. By and by, each in his turn, we shall hear the sunset gun.— Selected.</p>",
        "references": [
          "2 Timothy 2:3"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 164,
        "title": "Whoever does not carry their cross and follow me cannot be my disciple. Luke 14. 27.",
        "content": "<p>Whoever does not bear his cross and come after me, cannot be my disciple. Luke 14. 27.</p><p>There is always the shadow of the cross resting upon the Christian's path. Is that a reason why you should avoid or not undertake the duty? Have you made up your mind that you will follow your Master everywhere else, save when he ascends the path that leads to the cross? Is that your religion? The sooner you change it, the better. The religion of the Lord Jesus Christ is the religion of the cross, and unless we take up our cross, we can never follow Him.— W. Hay Aitken.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Luke 14:27"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 165,
        "title": "These . . . have turned the world upside down. Acts 17. 6.",
        "content": "<p>These . . . have turned the world upside down. Acts 17. 6.</p><p>The serene beauty of a holy life is the most powerful influence in the world next to the might of God.— Pascal.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Acts 17:6"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 166,
        "title": "You do not understand what I am doing now, but you will understand later. John 13. 7.",
        "content": "<p>What I do you know not now; but you will know hereafter. John 13. 7.</p><p>God keeps a school for His children here on earth and one of His best teachers is Disappointment. My friend, when you and I reach our Father's house, we shall look back and see that the sharp-voiced, rough; visaged teacher, Disappointment, was one of the best guides to train us for it. He gave us hard lessons; he often used the rod; he often led us into thorny paths; he sometimes stripped off a load of luxuries; but that only made us travel the freer and the faster on our heavenward way. He sometimes led us down into the valley of the death-shadow; but never did the promises read so sweetly as when spelled out by the eye of faith in that very valley. Nowhere did he lead us so often, or teach us such sacred lessons, as at the cross of Christ. Dear, old, rough-handed teacher! We will build a monument to you yet, and crown it with garlands, and inscribe on it: Blessed be the memory of Disappointment! — Theodore Cuyler.</p>",
        "references": [
          "John 13:7"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 167,
        "title": "Your strength will match your days. Deut. 33. 25.",
        "content": "<p>As your days, so shall your strength be. Deut. 33. 25.</p><p>Editorial reflection: God's promise of strength for each day invites trust for the present burden. It does not mean that weakness or hardship will vanish. Ask Christ for enough grace to do the next faithful thing, and allow another person to help when you need it.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Deuteronomy 33:25"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 168,
        "title": "I can do all things through Christ which strengtheneth me. Phil. 4. 13.",
        "content": "<p>I can do all things through Christ which strengthens me. Phil. 4. 13.</p><p>He will not impose upon you one needless burden. He will not exact more than He knows your strength will bear. He will ask no Peter to come to Him on the water, unless He impart at the same time strength and support on the unstable waves. He will not ask you to draw water if the well is too deep, or to withdraw the stone if too heavy. But neither at the same time will He admit as an impossibility that which, as a free and responsible agent, it is in your power to avert. He will not regard as your misfortune what is your crime.— Macduff.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Philippians 4:13"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 169,
        "title": "Your heart is not right in God's sight. Acts 8. 21.",
        "content": "<p>Your heart is not right in the sight of God. Acts 8. 21.</p><p>The worst of all mockeries is a religion that leaves the heart unchanged: a religion that has everything but the love of Christ enshrined in the soul.— F. Whitfield.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Acts 8:21"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 170,
        "title": "The Holy Ghost said, Separate me Barnabas and Saul for the work whereunto I have called them. Acts 13. 2.",
        "content": "<p>The Holy Spirit said, Separate me Barnabas and Saul for the work to which I have called them. Acts 13. 2.</p><p>We have such a nice little quiet, shady corner in the vineyard, down among the tender grapes, with such easy little weedings and waterings to attend to. And then the Master comes and draws us out into the thick of the work, and puts us in a part of the field where we never should have thought of going, and puts larger tools into our hands, that we may do more at a stroke. And we know we are not sufficient for these things, and the very tools seem too heavy for us, and the glare too dazzling and the vines too tall. Ah! but would we dally, go back? He would not be in the shady corner with us now; for when He put us forth He went before us, and it is only by closer following that we can abide with Him.— Frances Ridley Havergal.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Acts 13:2"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 171,
        "title": "Small things. Zech. 4. 10.",
        "content": "<p>Small things. Zech. 4. 10.</p><p>It is the little words you speak, the little thoughts you think, the little things you do or leave undone, the little moments you waste or use wisely, the little temptations which you yield to or overcome—the little things of every day that are making or marring your future life.— Selected.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Zechariah 4:10"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 172,
        "title": "Be perfect, be of good comfort. 2 Cor. 13. 11.",
        "content": "<p>Be perfect, be of good comfort. 2 Cor. 13. 11.</p><p>A glance at the words is enough to make us feel how contradictory they are. Be perfect —that is a word that strikes us with despair; at once we feel how far away we are from our own poor ideal, and alas! how much further from God's ideal concerning us. Be of good comfort —ah, that is very different! That seems to say, \"Do not fret; do not fear. If you are not what you would be, you must be thankful for what you are.\"</p><p>Now the question is this—How can these two be reconciled?</p><p>It is only the religion of Jesus Christ that reconciles them. He stands in our midst, and with the right hand of His righteousness He points us upward, and says, \"Be perfect.\" There is no resting-place short of that. Yet with the left hand of His love He does encompass us, as He says, \"Soul, be of good comfort; for that is what I came to do for you.\"— Mark Guy Pearse.</p>",
        "references": [
          "2 Corinthians 13:11"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 173,
        "title": "Therefore, be perfect, as your Father in heaven is perfect. Matt. 5. 48.",
        "content": "<p>Be you therefore perfect, even as your Father which is in heaven is perfect. Matt. 5. 48.</p><p>Seeking the aid of the Holy Spirit, let us aim at perfection. Let every day see some sin crucified, some battle fought, some good done, some victory won; let every fall be followed by a rise, and every step gained become, not a resting-place, but a new starting-point for further and higher progress.— Guthrie.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Matthew 5:48"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 174,
        "title": "Sleep on now, and take your rest. Mark 14. 41.",
        "content": "<p>Sleep on now, and take your rest. Mark 14. 41.</p><p>Never did that sacred opportunity to watch with Christ return to His disciples. Lost then, it was lost forever. And now when Jesus is still beholding the travail of His soul in the redemption of the world, if you fail to be with Him watching for souls as they that must give account, remember that the opportunity will never return. \"Watch, therefore,\" says your Lord, \"lest coming suddenly, He may find you sleeping.\"— A. J. Gordon.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Mark 14:41"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 175,
        "title": "Let us not sleep, as do others. 1 Thess. 5. 6.",
        "content": "<p>Let us not sleep, as do others. 1 Thess. 5. 6.</p><p>There are many ways of promoting Christian wakefulness. Among the rest, let me strongly advise Christians to converse together concerning the ways of the Lord. Christian and Hopeful, as they journeyed towards the Celestial City, said to themselves:</p><p>\"To prevent drowsiness in this place, let us fall into good discourse.\"</p><p>Christians who isolate themselves and walk alone are very liable to grow drowsy. Hold Christian company, and you will be kept wakeful by it, and refreshed and encouraged to make quicker progress in the road to heaven.— Spurgeon.</p>",
        "references": [
          "1 Thessalonians 5:6"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 176,
        "title": "He placed His right hand on me and said, Do not be afraid. Rev. 1. 17.",
        "content": "<p>He laid His right hand upon me, saying to me, Fear not. Rev. 1. 17.</p><p>One of Wellington's officers, when commanded to go on some perilous duty, lingered a moment as if afraid, and then said:</p><p>\"Let me have one clasp of your all-conquering hand before I go; and then I can do it.\"</p><p>Seek the clasp of Christ's hand before every bit of work, every hard task, every battle, every good deed. Bend your head in the dewy freshness of every morning, before you go forth to meet the day's duties and perils, and wait for the benediction of Christ, as He lays His hands upon you. They are hands of blessing. Their touch will inspire you for courage and strength and all beautiful and noble living.— J. R. Miller.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Revelation 1:17"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 177,
        "title": "Being seen of them forty days, and speaking of the things pertaining to the kingdom of God. Acts 1. 3.",
        "content": "<p>Being seen of them forty days, and speaking of the things pertaining to the kingdom of God. Acts 1. 3.</p><p>This lingering for forty days is the crowning proof of Christ's tender regard for His little flock. He who had laid down His life for them is loath to leave them. Though they had forsaken Him, and doubted Him, they had not wearied, much less had they worn out, His love. He stays to look again, and yet again, and yet again, upon them, as if turning back and lingering to bless them. It is all of a piece with His life of love. Everywhere He meets them without a touch of upbraiding, without recalling a single memory of all His bitter suffering, revealing Himself to the disciples with a tenderness and blessedness indescribably beautiful.</p><p>How can He go till He has healed the Magdalene's broken heart? He must linger till poor Peter can venture near to have his forgiveness assured. He must stay to strengthen Thomas' faith. He must wait with them till He has made them feel that He is just the same friendly, brotherly Jesus that He has ever been, caring for them in their work, watching them with a yearning pity, stooping to kindle a fire for their warmth, and to cook the fish for their meal, and then to bid them come and dine.— Mark Guy Pearse.</p><p>Jesus, . . . being wearied with His journey, sat thus on the well. . . . (For His disciples were gone away to the city to buy food.) . . . And many of the Samaritans of that city believed on Him for the saying of the woman, which testified, He told me all that ever I did. John 4. 6, 8, 39.</p><p>The bits of wayside work are very sweet. Perhaps the odd bits, when all is done, will really come to more than the seemingly greater pieces!. . . It is nice to know that the King's servants are always really on duty, even while some can only stand and wait.— Frances Ridley Havergal.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Acts 1:3",
          "John 4:6",
          "John 4:8",
          "John 4:39"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 178,
        "title": "I leave you peace; I give you my peace . . . do not let your hearts be troubled or afraid. John 14. 27.",
        "content": "<p>Peace I leave with you, my peace I give to you . . . let not your heart be troubled, neither let it be afraid. John 14. 27.</p><p>Dark hours come to us all; and if we have no clew to a peace that can pass unbroken through their murky gloom, we shall be in a state of continual dread. Any stone flung by a chance passer-by may break the crystal clearness of the Lake of Peace and send disturbing ripples across it, unless we have learnt to trust in the perpetual presence of Him who can make and keep a \"great calm\" within the soul. Only let nothing come to you which you shall not instantly hand over to Him—all petty worries, all crushing difficulties, all inability to believe.— F. B. Meyer.</p>",
        "references": [
          "John 14:27"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 179,
        "title": "Isaac dwelt by the well Lahai-roi. Gen. 25. 11.",
        "content": "<p>Isaac dwelt by the well Lahai-roi. Gen. 25. 11.</p><p>Isaac dwelt there, and made the well of the living and all-seeing God his constant source of supply. The usual tenor of a man's life, the dwelling of his soul, is the true test of his state. Let us learn to live in the presence of the living God. Let us pray the Holy Spirit that this day, and every other day, we may feel, \"You God see me.\" May the Lord Jehovah be as a well to us, delightful, comforting, unfailing, springing up to eternal life. The bottle of the creature cracks and dries up, but the well of the Creator never fails. Happy is he who dwells at the well, and so has abundant and constant supplies near at hand! Glorious Lord, constrain us that we may never leave You, but dwell by the well of the living God!— Spurgeon.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Genesis 25:11"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 180,
        "title": "Judas Iscariot . . . was a thief, and had the bag, and bore what was put therein. John 12. 4, 6.",
        "content": "<p>Judas Iscariot . . . was a thief, and had the bag, and bore what was put in it. John 12. 4, 6.</p><p>Editorial reflection: John's account warns that religious language can conceal self-interest. The money in Judas's care was meant for service, not private gain. Examine how you handle what has been entrusted to you and choose one practical way to care for a neighbor in need.</p>",
        "references": [
          "John 12:4",
          "John 12:6"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 181,
        "title": "You have received freely; give freely. Matt. 10. 8.",
        "content": "<p>Freely you have received, freely give. Matt. 10. 8.</p><p>Ah, but if we should go thoroughly into this matter, should we not probably find that many of us are guilty, in some modified and yet sufficiently alarming sense, of treachery to the poor? Are we not, some of us, sent to them with benefactions which never reach them, and are only unconscious of guilt because so long accustomed to look upon the goods as bestowed on us, whereas the light of God's word would plainly reveal upon those goods the names of the poor and needy?— George Bowen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Matthew 10:8"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 182,
        "title": "Let every man take heed how he buildeth. 1 Cor. 3. 10.",
        "content": "<p>Let every man take heed how he builds. 1 Cor. 3. 10.</p><p>Our business is not to build quickly, but to build upon a right foundation, and in a right spirit. Life is more than a mere competition as between man and man; it is not who can be done first, but who can work best; it is not who can rise highest in the shortest time, but who is working most patiently and lovingly in accordance with the designs of God.— Joseph Parker.</p>",
        "references": [
          "1 Corinthians 3:10"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 183,
        "title": "Your strength will match your days. Deut. 33. 25.",
        "content": "<p>As your days, so shall your strength be. Deut. 33. 25.</p><p>No day without its duty; no duty without strength to perform it.— Selected.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Deuteronomy 33:25"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 184,
        "title": "Surely the Lord is in this place, and I knew it not. Gen. 28. 16.",
        "content": "<p>Surely the Lord is in this place, and I knew it not. Gen. 28. 16.</p><p><strong>Source and historical context:</strong> George Matheson reads Jacob’s night at Bethel as a model for interpreting bereavement.</p><p><strong>Devotional focus:</strong> Genesis 28 describes Jacob’s encounter with God during an uncertain journey. It does not require believers to label every painful experience a gift or to discover a hidden blessing behind every tragedy. God can be near in grief without explaining it away. Spiritual honesty includes lament, listening, and giving a person time to mourn rather than demanding a triumphant account.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> Where have I expected someone to find a lesson before their grief could be heard?</p><p><strong>Practice:</strong> Give someone space to name a loss without trying to improve the story.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> God who met Jacob, be near to those who grieve and keep us from easy answers.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Genesis 28:16"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 185,
        "title": "My meat is to do the will of Him that sent me. John 4. 34.",
        "content": "<p>My food is to do the will of Him that sent me. John 4. 34.</p><p>The real secret of an unsatisfied life lies too often in an unsurrendered will.— J. Hudson Taylor.</p>",
        "references": [
          "John 4:34"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 186,
        "title": "Giving all diligence, add to your faith virtue. 2 Pet. 1. 5.",
        "content": "<p>Giving all diligence, add to your faith virtue. 2 Pet. 1. 5.</p><p>You will find it less easy to unroot faults than to choke them by gaining virtues. Do not think of your faults, still less of others' faults; in every person who comes near you look for what is good and strong; honor that; rejoice in it, and, as you can, try to imitate it; and your faults will drop off, like dead leaves, when their time comes.— John Ruskin.</p>",
        "references": [
          "2 Peter 1:5"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 187,
        "title": "Awake, north wind; come, south wind; blow upon my garden so its spices may flow out. Song of Sol. 4. 16.",
        "content": "<p>Awake, O north wind; and come, you south; blow upon my garden, that the spices of it may flow out. Song of Sol. 4. 16.</p><p>Sometimes God sends severe blasts of trial upon His children to develop their graces. Just as torches burn most brightly when swung violently to and fro; just as the juniper plant smells sweetest when flung into the flames; so the richest qualities of a Christian often come out under the north wind of suffering and adversity. Bruised hearts often emit the fragrance that God loves to smell. Almost every true believer's experience contains the record of trials which were sent for the purpose of shaking the spice tree.— Theodore Cuyler.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Song of Solomon 4:16"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 188,
        "title": "Awake, north wind; come, south wind; blow upon my garden so its spices may flow out. Song of Sol. 4. 16.",
        "content": "<p>Awake, O north wind; and come, you south; blow upon my garden, that the spices of it may flow out. Song of Sol. 4. 16.</p><p><strong>Source and historical context:</strong> Theodore Cuyler continues the same garden-wind analogy begun in the previous day.</p><p><strong>Devotional focus:</strong> Song of Songs 4 celebrates love in poetic language. Its winds are not instructions to accept pain as something God must send to make us holy. Christian growth can come through learning, friendship, repentance, joyful worship, and gentle care. Those facing hardship should not be told to welcome harm; they can seek safety and relief while asking God for sustaining grace.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> Can I look for spiritual growth without treating suffering as a requirement?</p><p><strong>Practice:</strong> Nurture one hopeful practice, such as encouraging a friend or resting with a psalm.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> God of love, cultivate compassion and joy in our lives without glorifying pain.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Song of Solomon 4:16"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 189,
        "title": "Look at the man! John 19. 5.",
        "content": "<p>See the man! John 19. 5.</p><p>\"See the man!\" was Pilate's jeer. That is what all the ages have been doing since, and the vision has grown more and more glorious. As they have looked, the crown of thorns has become a crown of golden radiance, and the cast-off robe has glistened like the garments He wore on the night of the transfiguration. Martyrs have smiled in the flames at that vision. Sinners have turned at it to a new life. Little children have seen it, and have had awakened by it dim recollections of their heaven-home. Toward it the souls of men yearn ever.— Robert E. Speer.</p>",
        "references": [
          "John 19:5"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 190,
        "title": "John said, Look, the Lamb of God! The two disciples heard him and followed Jesus. John 1. 36, 37.",
        "content": "<p>He (John) says, See the Lamb of God! And the two disciples heard him speak, and they followed Jesus. John 1. 36, 37.</p><p>To be a Christian means to know the presence of a true personal Christ among us, and to follow.— Phillips Brooks.</p>",
        "references": [
          "John 1:36-37"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 191,
        "title": "You must not eat from it. Gen. 3. 3.",
        "content": "<p>You shall not eat of it. Gen. 3. 3.</p><p>The Sin of Paradise was eating the tree of knowledge before the tree of life. Life must ever be first. Knowing and not being, hearing and not doing, admiring and not possessing, all are light without life.— Selected.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Genesis 3:3"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 192,
        "title": "Let patience finish its work so that you may be complete and lack nothing. James 1. 4.",
        "content": "<p>Let patience have her perfect work, that you may be perfect and entire, wanting nothing. James 1. 4.</p><p><strong>Source and historical context:</strong> Mark Guy Pearse connects James 1:4 to patient endurance.</p><p><strong>Devotional focus:</strong> James encourages steadfastness but does not forbid lament, safety-seeking, or honest protest. We cannot know that every painful circumstance was arranged by God for a lesson. Christian maturity can include speaking against injustice and asking for help. Patience means remaining grounded in hope while acting wisely, not submitting to mistreatment in silence.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> Is there an injustice or heavy burden I have been afraid to name?</p><p><strong>Practice:</strong> Speak truthfully about the problem to a safe and trustworthy person.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> God of patience, give me courage to seek what is right while I wait in hope.</p>",
        "references": [
          "James 1:4"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 193,
        "title": "Sow beside all waters. Isa. 32. 20.",
        "content": "<p>Sow beside all waters. Isa. 32. 20.</p><p>Never mind whereabouts your work is. Never mind whether it be visible or not. Never mind whether your name is associated with it. You may never see the issues of your toils. You are working for eternity. If you cannot see results here in the hot working day, the cool evening hours are drawing near, when you may rest from your labors and then they will follow you. So do your duty, and trust God to give the seed you sow \"a body as it has pleased Him,\"— Alex. McLaren.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Isaiah 32:20"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 194,
        "title": "Hold me up, and I will be safe. Psa. 119. 117.",
        "content": "<p>Hold you me up, and I shall be safe. Psa. 119. 117.</p><p>Do not spoil the chime of this morning's bells by ringing one half a peal! Do not say, \"Hold you me up,\" and stop there, or add, \"But all the same I shall stumble and fall!\" Finish the peal with God's own music, the bright words of faith that He puts into your mouth: \"Hold you me up, and I shall be safe! \"— Frances Ridley Havergal.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Psalms 119:117"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 195,
        "title": "Lord, my servant lieth at home sick of the palsy. Matt. 8. 6.",
        "content": "<p>Lord, my servant lies at home sick of the palsy. Matt. 8. 6.</p><p>We, in this age of the church, are in the position of that sick servant at Capernaum. To the eye of sense we are separated from the Savior. We see Him not—we can touch Him not—the hand cannot steal amid the crowd to catch His garment hem—we cannot hear His loved footsteps as of old on our threshold; but faith penetrates the invisible; the messenger—prayer—meets Him in the streets of the New Jerusalem; and faith and prayer together, the twin delegates from His church below, He has never yet sent empty away.— Macduff.</p><p>Work out your own salvation with fear and trembling: for it is God which works in you, both to will and to do of His good pleasure. Phil. 2. 12, 13.</p><p>What a staggering weight of thought is excited by these words! Stay, my soul, and wonder that the Eternal God should stoop to work within your narrow limits. Is it not a marvel indeed, that He, whom the heavens cannot contain, and in whose sight they are not clean, should trouble Himself to work on such material, so unpromising, and amidst circumstances so uncongenial?</p><p>How careful should we be to make Him welcome, and to throw no hindrance in His way! How eager to garner up all the least movements of His gracious operation, as the machinist conserves the force of his engine; and as the goldsmith, with miserly care, collects every flake of gold leaf! Surely we shall be sensible of the fear of holy reverence and the trembling of eager anxiety; as we \"work out,\" into daily act and life, all that God our Father is \"working in.\"— F. B. Meyer.</p><p>. . . Sinners of whom I am chief. . . . Now to the King, eternal, immortal, invisible, the only wise God, be honor and glory forever and ever. Amen. 1 Tim. 1. 15, 17.</p><p>Only those who have struck the deepest note of penitence can reach the highest note of praise.— A. J. Gordon.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Matthew 8:6",
          "Philippians 2:12-13",
          "1 Timothy 1:15",
          "1 Timothy 1:17"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 196,
        "title": "Blessed is the man . . . that keepeth the Sabbath. Isa. 56. 2.",
        "content": "<p>Blessed is the man . . . that keeps the Sabbath. Isa. 56. 2.</p><p>The Sabbath is the savings-bank of human life, into which we deposit one day in seven to be repaid in the autumn of life with compound interest.— Selected.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Isaiah 56:2"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 197,
        "title": "Cleanse me from hidden faults. Psa. 19. 12.",
        "content": "<p>Cleanse you me from secret faults. Psa. 19. 12.</p><p>The world wants men who are saved from secret faults. The world can put on an outside goodness and go very far in uprightness and morality, and it expects that a Christian shall go beyond it, and be free from secret faults. A little crack will spoil the ring of the coin. . . . The world expects, and rightly, that the Christian should be more gentle, and patient, and generous, than he who does not profess to be a disciple of the Lord Jesus. For the sake of those who take their notion of religion from our lives, we need to put up this prayer earnestly, \"Cleanse you me from secret faults.\"— Mark Guy Pearse.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Psalms 19:12"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 198,
        "title": "Do what is good. 2 Kings 10. 5.",
        "content": "<p>Do you that which is good. 2 Kings 10. 5.</p><p>Keep as few good intentions hovering about as possible. They are like ghosts haunting a dwelling. The way to lay them is to find bodies for them. When they are embodied in substantial deeds they are no longer dangerous.— William Arnot.</p>",
        "references": [
          "2 Kings 10:5"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 199,
        "title": "Grow in grace, and in the knowledge of our Lord and Savior, Jesus Christ. 2 Pet. 3. 18.",
        "content": "<p>Grow in grace, and in the knowledge of our Lord and Savior, Jesus Christ. 2 Pet. 3. 18.</p><p>Grace has its dawn as well as day; grace has its green blade, and afterwards its ripe corn in the ear; grace has its babes and its men in Christ. With God's work there, as with all His works, \"in all places of His dominion,\" progress is both the prelude and the path to perfection. Therefore we are exhorted to grow in grace, and in the knowledge of our Lord and Savior Jesus Christ, to go on to perfection, saying with Paul, \"I count not myself to have apprehended; but this one thing I do, forgetting those things which are behind, and reaching forth to those things which are before, I press towards the mark for the prize of the high calling of God in Christ Jesus.\"— Guthrie.</p>",
        "references": [
          "2 Peter 3:18"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 200,
        "title": "Sin, taking occasion by the commandment, deceived, and by it slew me. Rom. 7. 11.",
        "content": "<p>Sin, taking occasion by the commandment, deceived, and by it slew me. Rom. 7. 11.</p><p>Christian, beware how you think lightly of sin. Take heed lest you fall by little and little. Sin, a little thing? Is it not a poison? Who knows its deadliness? Sin, a little thing? Do not the little foxes spoil the grapes? Does not the tiny coral insect build a rock which wrecks a navy? Do not little strokes fell lofty oaks? Will not continual droppings wear away stones? Sin, a little thing? It girded the Redeemer's head with thorns, and pierced His heart! It made Him suffer anguish, bitterness and woe. Could you weigh the least sin in the scales of eternity, you would fly from it as from a serpent, and abhor the least appearance of evil . Look upon all sin as that which crucified the Savior, and you will see it to be \"exceeding sinful.\"— Spurgeon.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Romans 7:11"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 201,
        "title": "Your heavenly Father knows. Matt. 6. 32.",
        "content": "<p>Your heavenly Father knows. Matt. 6. 32.</p><p>The Master judges by the result, but our Father judges by the effort. Failure does not always mean fault. He knows how much things cost, and weighs them where others only measure. Your Father! Think how great store His love sets by the poor beginnings of the little ones, clumsy and unmeaning as they may be to others. All this lies in this blessed relationship, and infinitely more. Do not fear to take it all as your own.— Mark Guy Pearse.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Matthew 6:32"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 202,
        "title": "You have died, and your life is hidden with Christ in God. Col. 3. 3.",
        "content": "<p><strong>Read:</strong> Colossians 3:3; John 2:3-5</p><p><strong>Source and historical context:</strong> The anthology compares hidden life in Christ with seeking help from God under strain.</p><p><strong>Devotional focus:</strong> Colossians 3 calls believers to put on compassion, kindness, and patience. We cannot infer from a disappointing outcome that God decided suffering was more useful than relief. Hope in Christ can sustain people, while ordinary support and community responsibility still matter. The question is how to love faithfully today, not how to justify a neighbor’s unmet need.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> When can I help directly rather than speculate about God’s reasons for a shortage?</p><p><strong>Practice:</strong> Offer assistance with a specific need, or ask for help if the shortage is your own.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Christ our life, shape us into a compassionate community that shares its resources.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Colossians 3:3",
          "John 2:3-5"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 203,
        "title": "Let him that thinketh he standeth take heed lest he fall. 1 Cor 10. 12.",
        "content": "<p>Let him that thinks he stands take heed lest he fall. 1 Cor 10. 12.</p><p>Angels fell in heaven, Adam in paradise, Peter in Christ's presence.— Theophilus Polwheile.</p>",
        "references": [
          "1 Corinthians 10:12"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 204,
        "title": "Continue in prayer. Col. 4. 2.",
        "content": "<p>Continue in prayer. Col. 4. 2.</p><p>The greatest and the best talent that God gives to any man or woman in this world is the talent of prayer. And the best usury that any man or woman brings back to God when He comes to reckon with them at the end of this world is a life of prayer. And those servants best put their Lord's money to the exchangers who rise early and sit late, as long as they are in this world, ever finding out, and ever following after better and better methods of prayer, and ever forming more secret, more steadfast, and more spiritually fruitful habits of prayer, till they literally pray without ceasing, and till they continually strike out into new enterprises in prayer, and new achievements, and new enrichments.— Alex. Whyte.</p><p>He entered into one of the ships . . . and . . . sat down. Luke 5. 3.</p><p>When Jesus sits in the ship everything is in its right place. The cargo is in the hold, not in the heart . Cares and gains, fears and losses, yesterday's failure and today's success do not thrust themselves in between us and His presence. The heart cleaves to Him . \"Goodness and mercy shall follow me,\" sang the psalmist. Alas, when the goodness and mercy come before us, and our blessings shut Jesus from view! Here is the blessed order—the Lord ever first, I following Him, His goodness and mercy following me.— Mark Guy Pearse.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Colossians 4:2"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 205,
        "title": "Now you are light in the Lord: walk as children of light. Eph. 5. 8.",
        "content": "<p>Now are you light in the Lord: walk as children of light. Eph. 5. 8.</p><p>We do not realize the importance of the unconscious part of our life ministry. It goes on continually. In every greeting we give to another on the street, in every moment's conversation, in every letter we write, in every contact with other lives, there is a subtle influence that goes from us that often reaches further, and leaves a deep impression than the things themselves that we are doing at the time. It is not so much what we do in this world as what we are , that tells in spiritual results and impressions.— J. R. Miller.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Ephesians 5:8"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 206,
        "title": "Created in Christ Jesus for good works. Eph. 2. 10.",
        "content": "<p>Created in Christ Jesus to good works. Eph. 2. 10.</p><p>Let us ask Him to work in us to will those good works, so that our will , without being impaired in its free operation, may be permeated and moulded by His will, just as light suffuses the atmosphere without displacing it. And let us also expect that He will infuse into us sufficient strength that we may be able to do His will to all pleasing. Thus, day by day, our life will be a manifestation of those holy volitions and lovely deeds which shall attest the indwelling and inworking of God. And men shall see our good works, and glorify our Father which is in heaven.— F. B. Meyer.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Ephesians 2:10"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 207,
        "title": "Go in this strength of yours . . . have I not sent you? Judges 6. 14.",
        "content": "<p>Go in this your might . . . have not I sent you? Judges 6. 14.</p><p>God never leaves His child to fail when in the path of obedience.— Theodore Cuyler.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Judges 6:14"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 208,
        "title": "Set your affection on things above, not on things on the earth. Col. 3. 2.",
        "content": "<p>Set your affection on things above, not on things on the earth. Col. 3. 2.</p><p>Whatever your hand finds to do, do it with your might. Eccles. 9. 10.</p><p>If we are to live separate from the world, how, since men only do well what they do with a will, are we, with affections fixed on things above, to perform aright the secular, ordinary duties of life? If our hearts are engrossed with heavenly things, how are we to obey this other, and equally divine, commandment, \"Whatever your hand finds to do, do it with your might\"?</p><p>The two are perfectly consistent. Man standing between the celestial and terrestrial worlds is related to both; and resembling neither a flower, which, springing from the dust and returning to it, belongs altogether to the earth, nor a star which, shining far remote from its lower sphere, belongs altogether to the heavens, our hearts may be fitly likened to the rainbow that, rising into heaven but resting on earth, is connected both with the clods of the valley and the clouds of the sky.— Guthrie.</p><p>Let us lay aside every weight, and the sin which does so easily beset us, and let us run with patience the race that is set before us, looking to Jesus. Heb. 12. 1, 2.</p><p>Think, as you sit here, of anything that you are doing that is wrong, of any habit of your life, of your self-indulgence, or of that great, pervasive habit of your life which makes you a creature of the present instead of the eternities, a creature of the material earth instead of the glorious skies. Ask yourself of any habit that belongs to your own personal life, and bring it face to face with Jesus Christ.— Phillips Brooks.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Colossians 3:2",
          "Hebrews 12:1-2"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 209,
        "title": "They took knowledge of them, that they had been with Jesus. Acts 4. 13.",
        "content": "<p>They took knowledge of them, that they had been with Jesus. Acts 4. 13.</p><p>If I think of the world, I get the impress of the world; if I think of my trials and sorrows, I get the impress of my trials and sorrows; if I think of my failures, I get the impress of my failures; if I think of Christ, I get the impress of Christ.— Selected.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Acts 4:13"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 210,
        "title": "You call me Teacher and Lord, and you are right, for that is what I am. John 13. 13. (R. V. margin).",
        "content": "<p>You call me Teacher, and Lord: and you say well; for so I am. John 13. 13. (R. V. margin).</p><p>How wonderful a Teacher we have! Sometimes we seek Him in the house, but He is not there. We go forth seeking Him and find Him perhaps in the wilderness or on a mountain praying, or leading some poor blind man by the hand, or eating with publicans or sinners, or asleep in a storm or conversing with a Samaritan woman, or surrounded by wrathful men, or bearing a cross. It is not merely His words that instruct. His place, His occupation, His companions, His environment, His garment, His silence, His submission—all teem with instruction. And they that learn of Him are made like to Him.— George Bowen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "John 13:13"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 211,
        "title": "The Father sent the Son to be the Savior of the world. 1 John 4. 14.",
        "content": "<p>The Father sent the Son to be the Savior of the world. 1 John 4. 14.</p><p>It is a sweet thought that Jesus Christ did not come forth without His Father's permission, authority, consent, and assistance. He was sent of the Father that He might be the Savior of men. . . . Did you ever consider the depth of love in the heart of Jehovah, when God the Father equipped His Son for the great enterprise of mercy? If not, be this your day's meditation. The Father sent Him! Contemplate that subject. Think how Jesus works what the Father wills. In the wounds of the dying Savior see the love of the great I AM. Let every thought of Jesus be also connected with the eternal, ever-blessed God.— Spurgeon.</p>",
        "references": [
          "1 John 4:14"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 212,
        "title": "They that wait upon the Lord shall change their strength. Isa. 40. 31. (R. V.)",
        "content": "<p>They that wait upon the Lord shall change their strength. Isa. 40. 31. (R. V.)</p><p>Editorial reflection: Those who wait for the Lord do not have to pretend they are never weary. Waiting can mean bringing that weariness into his presence and receiving strength for what lies ahead. Set aside a few quiet minutes to pray before you resume your work.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Isaiah 40:31"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 213,
        "title": "As for you, the Lord your God has not allowed you to do this. Deut. 18. 14.",
        "content": "<p>As for you, the Lord your God has not suffered you so to do. Deut. 18. 14.</p><p>What a stepping-stone! We give thanks, often with a tearful, doubtful voice, for our spiritual mercies positive; but what an almost infinite field there is for mercies negative! We cannot even imagine all that God has suffered us not to do, not to be.— Frances Ridley Havergal.</p><p>Jesus went forth, and saw a great multitude, and was moved with compassion toward them, and He healed their sick. . . . And when He had sent the multitudes away, He went up into a mountain apart to pray: and when the evening was come He was there alone. Matt. 14. 14, 23.</p><p>Do we, like Him, combine the two great elements of human character? Are our public duties, the cares, and business, and engrossments of the world, finely tempered and hallowed by a secret walk with God? If the world were to follow us from its busy thoroughfares, would it trace us to our family altars and our closet devotions?</p><p>Action and meditation are the two great components of Christian life, and the perfection of the religious character is to find the two in unison and harmony.— Macduff.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Deuteronomy 18:14",
          "Matthew 14:14",
          "Matthew 14:23"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 214,
        "title": "Leaving you an example for you to follow in His steps. 1 Pet. 2. 21. (R. V.)",
        "content": "<p>Leaving you an example, that you should follow His steps. 1 Pet. 2. 21. (R. V.)</p><p>I have long since ceased to pray, \"Lord Jesus, have compassion on a lost world!\" I remember the day and the hour when I seemed to hear the Lord rebuking me for making such a prayer. He seemed to say to me, \"I have had compassion upon a lost world, and now it is for you to have compassion.\"— A. J. Gordon.</p>",
        "references": [
          "1 Peter 2:21"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 215,
        "title": "You must have no other gods before me. Ex. 20. 3.",
        "content": "<p>You will have no other gods before me. Ex. 20. 3.</p><p>If you find yourself beginning to love any pleasure better than your prayers, any book better than your Bible, any house better than God's, any table better than the Lord's, any person better than your Savior, any one better than your soul, a present indulgence better than the hope of heaven—take alarm!— Guthrie.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Exodus 20:3"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 216,
        "title": "Follow my example, as I follow Christ. 1 Cor. 11. 1.",
        "content": "<p>Be you followers of me, even as I also am of Christ. 1 Cor. 11. 1.</p><p>When in the Mexican war the troops were wavering, a general rose in his stirrups and dashed into the enemy's line, shouting, \"Men, follow!\" They, seeing his courage and disposition, dashed on after him, and gained the victory.</p><p>What men want to rally them for God is an example to lead them. All your commands to others to advance amount to nothing so long as you stay behind. To effect them aright, you need to start for heaven yourself, looking back only to give the stirring cry of \"Men, follow!\"— T. DeWitt Talmage.</p>",
        "references": [
          "1 Corinthians 11:1"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 217,
        "title": "Serving the Lord with all humility of mind. Acts 20. 19.",
        "content": "<p>Serving the Lord with all humility of mind. Acts 20. 19.</p><p>There is a legend of an artist who long sought for a piece of sandalwood, out of which to carve a Madonna. He was about to give up in despair, leaving the vision of his life unrealized, when in a dream he was bidden to carve his Madonna from a block of oak wood, which was destined for the fire. He obeyed, and produced a masterpiece from a log of common fire-wood.</p><p>Many of us lose great opportunities in life by waiting to find sandalwood for our carvings, when they really lie hidden in the common logs that we burn.— Orison Swett Marden.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Acts 20:19"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 218,
        "title": "My grace is enough for you, for my strength is made perfect in weakness. 2 Cor. 12. 9.",
        "content": "<p>My grace is sufficient for you: for my strength is made perfect in weakness. 2 Cor. 12. 9.</p><p><strong>Source and historical context:</strong> F. Whitfield interprets Paul’s unanswered request about his thorn in the flesh.</p><p><strong>Devotional focus:</strong> In 2 Corinthians 12, Paul receives reassurance of grace amid continuing weakness. The passage does not say that relief from illness or danger would lead people away from heaven. God’s answers to prayer cannot be reduced to one pattern. Seeking treatment, protection, or relief can be entirely consistent with faith. Weakness need not become a moral verdict.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> Have I made someone feel guilty for seeking relief from pain?</p><p><strong>Practice:</strong> Encourage an appropriate practical support alongside prayer for a person under strain.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> God whose grace is sufficient, accompany us and open responsible paths toward healing.</p>",
        "references": [
          "2 Corinthians 12:9"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 219,
        "title": "Remain in me, and I in you. Just as a branch cannot bear fruit unless it remains in the vine, neither can you unless you remain in me. John 15. 4.",
        "content": "<p>Abide in me, and I in you. As the branch cannot bear fruit of itself, except it abide in the vine, no more can you, except you abide in me. John 15. 4.</p><p>From moment to moment, and from hour to hour, the inner nature of man is to be continuously sustained with the life of God. Only as I am constantly receiving His fulness into my emptiness am I really living in the true, full, deep sense of the word, that life of eternity, which is my privilege now, and will be my glory hereafter.— W. Hay Aitken.</p>",
        "references": [
          "John 15:4"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 220,
        "title": "By faith Noah . . . prepared an ark to the saving of his house. Heb. 11. 7.",
        "content": "<p>By faith Noah . . . prepared an ark to the saving of his house. Heb. 11. 7.</p><p>What a humble, what a modest sphere for the exercise of faith! One would have said that the purpose was quite disproportionate to the work. The ark was a great undertaking, but what was it undertaken for? To save his own family. Is so narrow a sphere worthy to be the object of faith? Is so commonplace a scene as the life of the family circle fit to be a temple for the service of God? . . . My soul, when you have finished your prayers and ended your meditations, do not say that you have left the house of God. God's house shall to you be everywhere, and your own house shall be a part of it. You will feel that all the duties of this place are consecrated; that it is none other than the house of God and one of the gates to heaven. You will feel that every one of its duties is an act of high communion. Therefore be it your to make your house His house. Be it your to consecrate each word and look and deed in the social life of home. Be it your to build your ark of refuge for the wants of common day; truly, your labor of love shall be called an act of faith.— George Matheson.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Hebrews 11:7"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 221,
        "title": "We are His workmanship, created in Christ Jesus for good works that God prepared beforehand for us to do. Eph. 2. 10.",
        "content": "<p>We are His workmanship, created in Christ Jesus to good works which God has before ordained that we should walk in them. Eph. 2. 10.</p><p>No man is born into the world whose work is not born with him. There is always work, and tools to work withal, for those who will.— J. B. Lowell.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Ephesians 2:10"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 222,
        "title": "He . . . began to wash the disciples' feet. John 13. 5.",
        "content": "<p>He . . . began to wash the disciples' feet. John 13. 5.</p><p>We forget that Jesus Christ is the same today, when He is sitting on the throne, as He was yesterday, when He trod the pathway of our world. And in this forgetfulness how much we miss! What He was, that He is. What He said, that He says. The Gospels are simply specimens of the life that He is ever living; they are leaves torn out of the diary of His unchangeable Being. Today He is engaged in washing the feet of His disciples, soiled with their wilderness journeyings. Yes, that charming incident is having its fulfilment in you, my friend, if only you do not refuse the lowly loving offices of Him whom we call Master and Lord, but who still girds Himself and comes forth to serve. And we must have this incessant cleansing if we would keep right. It is not enough to look back to a certain hour when we first knelt at the feet of the Son of God for pardon; and heard Him say, \"Your sins, which are many, are all forgiven.\" We need daily, hourly cleansing—from daily, hourly sin.— F. B. Meyer.</p>",
        "references": [
          "John 13:5"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 223,
        "title": "I am the Lord, I change not. Mal. 3. 6.",
        "content": "<p>I am the Lord, I change not. Mal. 3. 6.</p><p>Our hope is not hung upon such untwisted thread as \"I imagine so,\" or \"it is likely\"; but the cable, the strong rope of our fastened anchor, is the oath and promise of Him who is eternal verity. Our salvation is fastened with God's own hand and Christ's own strength to the strong stake of God's unchanging nature.— William Rutherford.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Malachi 3:6"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 224,
        "title": "I will cause the shower to come down in his season; there shall be showers of blessing. Ezek. 34. 26.",
        "content": "<p>I will cause the shower to come down in his season; there shall be showers of blessing. Ezek. 34. 26.</p><p><strong>Source and historical context:</strong> Spurgeon pictures Ezekiel’s promised showers as comfort during spiritual drought.</p><p><strong>Devotional focus:</strong> Ezekiel 34 addresses God’s care for a flock harmed by unfaithful shepherds. Its promise cannot be turned into a guarantee that each private difficulty will resolve on demand. The chapter also emphasizes accountability for leaders who exploit the vulnerable. A faithful response to the promise includes practical protection and justice, not simply expecting good feelings to return.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> Who needs concrete support rather than another promise of instant improvement?</p><p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read Ezekiel 34:1–16 and identify one action that protects someone vulnerable.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Shepherd of your people, teach us to protect the neglected and to trust your mercy.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Ezekiel 34:26"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 225,
        "title": "Nevertheless, at your word. Luke 5. 5.",
        "content": "<p>Nevertheless, at your word. Luke 5. 5.</p><p>Oh, what a blessed formula for us! This path of mine is dark, mysterious, perplexing; nevertheless, at Your word I will go forward. This trial of mine is cutting, sore for flesh and blood to bear. It is hard to breathe through a broken heart, Your will be done. But, nevertheless, at Your word I will say, Even so, Father! This besetting habit, or infirmity, or sin of mine, is difficult to crucify. It has become part of myself—a second nature; to be severed from it would be like the cutting off of a right hand, or the plucking out of a right eye; nevertheless, at Your word I will lay aside every weight; this idol I will utterly abolish. This righteousness of mine it is hard to ignore; all these virtues, and amiabilities, and natural graces, it is hard to believe that they dare not in any way be mixed up in the matter of my salvation; and that I am to receive all from first to last as the gift of God, through Jesus Christ my Lord. Nevertheless, at Your word I will count all but loss for the excellency of His knowledge.— Macduff.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Luke 5:5"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 226,
        "title": "If we suffer, we shall also reign with Him. 2 Tim. 2. 12.",
        "content": "<p>If we suffer, we shall also reign with Him. 2 Tim. 2. 12.</p><p><strong>Source and historical context:</strong> A. J. Gordon compares a photographic negative to earthly sorrow and heavenly reward.</p><p><strong>Devotional focus:</strong> Paul’s language about endurance does not give us a formula in which more pain purchases more glory. Christian hope rests in God’s grace and the risen Christ. People may resist violence, seek treatment, and grieve without diminishing their faith. Hope should strengthen care for those suffering now rather than make suffering an achievement to pursue.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> Where have I treated another person’s pain as proof of a special calling?</p><p><strong>Practice:</strong> Offer care today without promising a specific reward for the hardship.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> God of hope, sustain those who suffer and teach us to love without romanticizing pain.</p>",
        "references": [
          "2 Timothy 2:12"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 227,
        "title": "The word of God, which lives and remains forever. 1 Pet. 1. 23.",
        "content": "<p>The word of God, which lives and abides forever. 1 Pet. 1. 23.</p><p>The Word abides. The Jew hated it—but it lived on, while the veil was torn away from the shrine which the Shekinah had forsaken, and while Jerusalem itself was destroyed. The Greek derided it—but it has seen his philosophy effete and his Acropolis in ruins. The Romans threw it into the flames—but it rose from its ashes, and swooped down upon the falling eagle. The reasoner cast it into the furnace, which his own negligence had heated \"seven times hotter than its accustomed\"—but it came out without the smell of fire. The formalist fastened serpents around it to poison it—but it shook them off and felt no harm. The infidel cast it overboard in a tempest of sophistry and sarcasm—but it rode gallantly upon the crest of the proud waters. And it is living still—yet heard in the loudest swelling of the storm—it has been speaking all the while—it is speaking now!— Punshon.</p>",
        "references": [
          "1 Peter 1:23"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 228,
        "title": "Let the peace of God rule in your hearts. Col. 3. 15.",
        "content": "<p>Let the peace of God rule in your hearts. Col. 3. 15.</p><p>Years ago one of our fleets was terribly shattered by a violent gale—but it was found that some of the ships were unaffected by its violence. They were in what mariners call \"the eye of the storm.\" While all around was desolation, they were safe. So it is with him who has the peace of God in his heart.— Pilkington.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Colossians 3:15"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 229,
        "title": "You serve the Lord Christ. Col. 3. 24.",
        "content": "<p>You serve the Lord Christ. Col. 3. 24.</p><p>Our business as Christians is to serve the Lord in every business of life.— Mark Guy Pearse.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Colossians 3:24"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 230,
        "title": "Love not the world, neither the things that are in the world. 1 John 2. 15.",
        "content": "<p>Love not the world, neither the things that are in the world. 1 John 2. 15.</p><p>If you will go to the banks of a little stream, and watch the flies that come to bathe in it, you will notice that, while they plunge their bodies into the water, they keep their wings high out of the water; and, after swimming about a little while, they fly away with their wings unwet through the sunny air. Now, that is the lesson for us. Here we are immersed in the cares and business of the world; but let us keep the wings of our soul, our faith and our love, out of the world, that, with these unclogged, we may be ready to take our flight to heaven.— J. Inglis.</p>",
        "references": [
          "1 John 2:15"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 231,
        "title": "I would have you without carefulness. 1 Cor. 7. 32.",
        "content": "<p>I would have you without carefulness. 1 Cor. 7. 32.</p><p>Do not look forward to the changes and chances of this life in fear. Rather look to them with full hope that, as they arise, God, whose you are, will deliver you out of them. He has kept you until now—do you but hold fast to His dear hand, and He will lead you safely through all things; and when you cannot stand, He will bear you in His arms. Do not look forward to what may happen tomorrow. The same everlasting Father who cares for you today will take care of you tomorrow, and every day. Either He will shield you from suffering, or He will give you unfailing strength to bear it. Be at peace then, and put aside all anxious thoughts and imaginations.— Francis de Sales.</p>",
        "references": [
          "1 Corinthians 7:32"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 232,
        "title": "The Lord God says: I will let the house of Israel ask me to do this for them. Ezek. 36. 37.",
        "content": "<p>Thus says the Lord God, I will yet for this be inquired of by the house of Israel, to do it for them. Ezek. 36. 37.</p><p>Prayer is the forerunner of mercy. Turn to sacred history and you will find that scarcely ever did a great mercy come to this world unheralded by supplication. Prayer is always the preface to blessing. It goes before the blessing as the blessing's shadow . When the sunlight of God's mercies rises upon our necessities it casts the shadow of prayer far down upon the plain. Or, to use another illustration, when God piles up a hill of mercies He Himself shines behind them, and He casts on our spirits the shadow of prayer so that we may rest certain, if we are much in prayer, our pleadings are the shadows of mercy. Prayer is thus connected with the blessing to show us the value of it.— Spurgeon.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Ezekiel 36:37"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 233,
        "title": "Let us not be weary in well doing, for in due season we shall reap if we faint not. Gal. 6. 9.",
        "content": "<p>Let us not be weary in well doing, for in due season we shall reap if we faint not. Gal. 6. 9.</p><p>The hours of this present life are the ages in embryo of the life to come.— A. J. Gordon.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Galatians 6:9"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 234,
        "title": "My presence will go with you. Ex. 33. 14.",
        "content": "<p>My presence shall go with you. Ex. 33. 14.</p><p>We should never leave our prayer closets in the morning without having concentrated our thoughts deeply and intensely on the fact of the actual presence of God there with us, encompassing us, and filling the room as literally as it fills heaven itself. It may not lead to any distinct results at first, but, as we make repeated efforts to realize the presence of God, it will become increasingly real to us. And, as the habit grows upon us, when alone in a room, or when treading the sward of some natural woodland temple, or when pacing the stony street—in the silence of night, or amid the teeming crowds of daylight—we shall often find ourselves whispering the words, \"You are near; you are here, O Lord.\"— F. B. Meyer.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Exodus 33:14"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 235,
        "title": "To the Lord our God belong mercies and forgiveness. Dan. 9. 9.",
        "content": "<p>To the Lord our God belong mercies and forgiveness. Dan. 9. 9.</p><p>As a spring lock closes itself, but cannot be unlocked without a key, so we ourselves may run into sin, but cannot return without the key of God's grace.— Cawdray.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Daniel 9:9"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 236,
        "title": "It is high time to awake out of sleep. Rom. 13. 11.",
        "content": "<p>It is high time to awake out of sleep. Rom. 13. 11.</p><p>I have heard of a painter who loved to work by the morning light. He said that the colors were better understood by the light of the early day, and so he was accustomed to be in his studio waiting for the rising of the sun. Then every moment it grew lighter, and he found he could accomplish things which he could not reach if he waited till the day had advanced.</p><p>Is there not work waiting for us—work that no one else can do—work, too, that the Master has promised to help us perform? Shall He come and find that we still sleep? Or shall the Son of Righteousness, when He appears, find us waiting, as that painter waited, looking and longing for the first gleam of day? Surely those of us who thus wait on the Lord shall renew our strength, and, eagle-like, rise to greet the Sun.— Thomas Champness.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Romans 13:11"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 237,
        "title": "The church of God, which He purchased with His own blood. Acts 20. 28.",
        "content": "<p>The church of God, which He has purchased with His own blood. Acts 20. 28.</p><p>Surely He may do what He will with His own. The price He has paid to make them His own is a sufficient guarantee that He will never make light of anything in which their welfare is at all concerned. We are precious to Him by the virtue of the blood which He has shed for us, and for Him to be found at any time wanting in solicitude for our happiness would be for Him to treat that blood of His as the sinners of this world treat it. The persuasion of Christ's love must be graven in our hearts so deeply that no semblance of indifference on His part will ever make the slightest impression upon us. This is the victory which overcomes the world.— George Bowen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Acts 20:28"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 238,
        "title": "May the God of hope fill you with all joy and peace as you believe, so that you may overflow with hope by the power of the Holy Spirit. Rom. 15. 13.",
        "content": "<p>The God of hope fill you with all joy and peace in believing, that you may abound in hope, through the power of the Holy Spirit. Rom. 15. 13.</p><p>In spiritual as in earthly things there is great strength in hope, and, therefore, God's people are carefully to cultivate that grace. A well-grounded hope that, having been made new creatures in Jesus Christ, we are His; that with our names, though unknown to fame, written in the Book of Life, we have grace in possession and heaven in prospect; that after a few more brief years, pure as the angels that sing before the throne, we shall be brought with gladness into the palace of the King, to be like Christ and with Christ, seeing Him eye to eye and face to face—such hopes are powerful springs of action.— Guthrie.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Romans 15:13"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 239,
        "title": "He asked you for life, and you gave it to him, length of days forever and ever. Psa. 21. 4.",
        "content": "<p>He asked life of You, and You gave it him, even length of days or ever and ever. Psa. 21. 4.</p><p><strong>Source and historical context:</strong> Bishop Reynolds uses a startling biblical comparison when reflecting on Psalm 21:4.</p><p><strong>Devotional focus:</strong> Psalm 21 is a royal thanksgiving and should be read in its literary setting. The violent Jael story is not a rule for kindness, and this psalm does not guarantee every request will receive more than was asked. Prayer can include gratitude, confusion, and lament. God’s faithfulness is not measured by the size of any material benefit.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> What request can I bring honestly without dictating the outcome?</p><p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read Psalm 21 beside a lament psalm and hold thanksgiving and uncertainty together.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Listening God, receive my requests and help me respond with courage whatever comes.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Psalms 21:4"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 240,
        "title": "This beginning of miracles did Jesus. John 2. 11.",
        "content": "<p>This beginning of miracles did Jesus. John 2. 11.</p><p>It was out of the common thing that the precious thing was brought; and it is out of the common things of daily life, presented obediently to Jesus and laid at His feet, that He brings His own glorious gifts, so that our whole lives become one great sacrament.— W. Hay Aitken.</p>",
        "references": [
          "John 2:11"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 241,
        "title": "In the daytime . . . He led them with a cloud, and all the night with a light of fire. Psa. 78. 14.",
        "content": "<p>In the daytime . . . He led them with a cloud, and all the night with a light of fire. Psa. 78. 14.</p><p>My day is my prosperity; it is the time when the sun of fortune is bright above me, and, therefore, it is the time when I need a shade. If my sunshine were not chequered I would forget You, O my God.</p><p>But I have nights to meet as well as days. The night is my adversity; it is the time when the sun of fortune has gone down behind the hills, and I am left alone, and then it is, O my Father, that I need the light of Your fire! My light of fire for the night is the vision of Calvary—the vision of Your love in the Cross. I need the light of Your fire \" all the night.\"— George Matheson.</p><p>Now are we the sons of God: and it does not yet appear what we shall be; but we know that when He shall appear, we shall be like Him, for we shall see Him as He is. 1 John 3. 2.</p><p>\"Now are we the sons of God.\" That is the pier upon one side of the gulf. \"It does not yet appear what we shall be, but when He shall appear we shall be like Him.\" That is the pier on the other. How are the two to be connected? There is only one way by which the present sonship will blossom and fruit into the future perfect likeness, and that is, if we throw across the gulf, by God's help day by day, the bridge of growing likeness to Himself, and purity from it.— Alex. McLaren.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Psalms 78:14",
          "1 John 3:2"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 242,
        "title": "Look, we are going up to Jerusalem. Matt. 20. 18.",
        "content": "<p>See, we go up to Jerusalem. Matt. 20. 18.</p><p><strong>Source and historical context:</strong> J. B. Stratton reflects on Jesus going to Jerusalem despite the danger awaiting him.</p><p><strong>Devotional focus:</strong> Matthew 20 describes Jesus knowingly approaching his suffering and death, a unique part of the Gospel story. It does not turn every tragedy into a necessary triumph or oblige others to endure preventable violence. Following Jesus includes loving courage, truthfulness, and care for people in danger. We may hold resurrection hope without calling another person’s loss spiritually beneficial.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> How can I honor Christ’s courage without interpreting someone else’s pain for them?</p><p><strong>Practice:</strong> Offer concrete support to someone facing difficulty; avoid claiming their suffering has an assigned purpose.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Risen Jesus, give us courage to love and protect life, and hope that does not silence grief.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Matthew 20:18"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 243,
        "title": "These were the potters, and those that dwelt among plants and hedges: there they dwelt with the king for his work. 1 Chron. 4. 23.",
        "content": "<p>These were the potters, and those that dwelt among plants and hedges: there they dwelt with the king for his work. 1 Chron. 4. 23.</p><p>Anywhere and everywhere we may dwell \"with the King for His work.\" We may be in a very unlikely or unfavorable place for this; it may be in a little country life, with little enough to be seen of the \"goings\" of the King around us; it may be among hedges of all sorts, hindrances in all directions; it may be, furthermore, with our hands full of all manner of pottery for our daily task. No matter! The King who placed us \"there\" will come and dwell there with us; the hedges are all right, or He would soon do away with them; and it does not follow that what seems to hinder our way may not be for its very protection; and as for the pottery, why, this is just exactly what He has seen fit to put into our hands, and therefore it is, for the present, \"His work.\"— Frances Ridley Havergal.</p>",
        "references": [
          "1 Chronicles 4:23"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 244,
        "title": "I will instruct you and teach you the way to go; I will guide you while watching over you. Psa. 32. 8.",
        "content": "<p>I will instruct you, and teach you in the way which you will go; I will guide you with mine eye. Psa. 32. 8.</p><p>When God does the directing, our life is useful and full of promise, whatever it is doing; and discipline has its perfecting work.— H. E. Cobb.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Psalms 32:8"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 245,
        "title": "The Son of Man came not to be served but to serve, and to give His life as a ransom for many. Matt. 20. 28.",
        "content": "<p>The Son of man came not to be ministered to, but to minister, and to give His life a ransom for many. Matt. 20. 28.</p><p>We are so to surrender ourselves to Christ that this great purpose of His coming shall claim and possess the whole life. We are to live, like God, to bless others. This is His will, His purpose concerning us. This is what His power waits to do for us. And this too, is the claim of His great love upon us.</p><p>Do not sigh a poor assent to the truth of it, and then pass by neglectfully on the other side. Do not think about it and pray about it without even a passing hope that the prayer will be answered. Do not gather yourself up in great resolutions to be good and useful. Kneel in sight of the Crucified. In the cross of Christ spell out His great purpose and yearning love to men. Let the heart feel all the might of the appeal that comes to us from those torn hands and feet and bleeding brow, from all the dreadful shame and agony of our dear Lord. And, bought and bound by all this, surrender yourself to Him for His great purpose. Take Him as your strength for this life-work.— Mark Guy Pearse.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Matthew 20:28"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 246,
        "title": "Jesus . . . went about doing good. Acts 10. 38.",
        "content": "<p>Jesus . . . went about doing good. Acts 10. 38.</p><p>The finest of all fine arts is the art of doing good; and yet it is the least cultivated.— T. DeWitt Talmage.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Acts 10:38"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 247,
        "title": "The angel of the Lord said to her [Hagar], Return to your mistress and submit to her authority. Gen. 16. 9.",
        "content": "<p>And the angel of the Lord said to her [Hagar], Return to your mistress, and submit yourself under her hands. Gen. 16. 9.</p><p><strong>Source and historical context:</strong> The historical note generalizes an instruction given to Hagar under coercion.</p><p><strong>Devotional focus:</strong> Genesis 16 describes Hagar as an Egyptian enslaved woman who has been mistreated and fled. Her story must never be used to instruct an abused person to return to danger. God sees Hagar and calls her by name, and Genesis 21 shows God caring for her and Ishmael outside Abraham’s household. Responsible Christian reading attends to power, safety, and the vulnerable rather than turning a narrative into a command for everyone.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> Whose circumstances have I reduced to a rule without hearing their story?</p><p><strong>Practice:</strong> When someone discloses abuse, believe them, help identify safety and support, and do not pressure reconciliation.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> God who sees Hagar, defend the vulnerable and teach us to protect their dignity and freedom.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Genesis 16:9"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 248,
        "title": "Then Solomon spoke . . . I have built you a house to dwell in. 1 Kings 8. 12, 13.",
        "content": "<p>Then spoke Solomon . . . I have surely built you an house to dwell in. 1 Kings 8. 12, 13.</p><p>Solomon, the prince of peace, alone could build the temple. If we would be soul-winners and build up the church, which is God's temple, let us note this; not by discussion nor by argument, but by lifting up Christ shall we draw men to Him.— J. Hudson Taylor.</p>",
        "references": [
          "1 Kings 8:12-13"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 249,
        "title": "I have chosen you in the furnace of affliction. Isa. 48. 10.",
        "content": "<p>I have chosen you in the furnace of affliction. Isa. 48. 10.</p><p><strong>Source and historical context:</strong> Spurgeon finds encouragement in Isaiah’s imagery of a furnace and God’s choice.</p><p><strong>Devotional focus:</strong> Isaiah 48 speaks to Israel within a particular prophetic dispute; the furnace image does not guarantee personal protection from disease or economic loss. Faith can give spiritual strength, but seeking medical care, safety, food, and practical support is wise and often necessary. We do not infer someone’s spiritual standing from their health or wealth. Hope grows alongside honest attention to needs.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> Where might a comforting promise need to be accompanied by practical care?</p><p><strong>Practice:</strong> Find one concrete way to support someone experiencing illness or financial difficulty.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> God of Israel and all creation, teach us faithful trust and compassionate action.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Isaiah 48:10"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 250,
        "title": "God has chosen what the world considers lowly and despised. 1 Cor. 1. 28.",
        "content": "<p>Base things of the world and things which are despised has God chosen. 1 Cor. 1. 28.</p><p><strong>Source and historical context:</strong> Bishop Simpson uses pieces of mosaic stone and glass as a comparison for Christian community.</p><p><strong>Devotional focus:</strong> Paul reminds the Corinthians that God’s call does not follow human hierarchies of prestige. The passage does not declare those without status worthless. A mosaic is meaningful because every part contributes; people deserve honor whether or not their contribution is visible. God’s grace challenges contempt and invites communities where overlooked voices are welcomed.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> Whom have I been tempted to overlook because they seem unimportant?</p><p><strong>Practice:</strong> Invite a quieter person’s perspective and listen without measuring their usefulness.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Creator God, teach us to honor every person and build communities of mutual respect.</p>",
        "references": [
          "1 Corinthians 1:28"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 251,
        "title": "Serve the Lord with gladness; come before His presence with singing. Psa. 100. 2.",
        "content": "<p>Serve the Lord with gladness; come before His presence with singing. Psa. 100. 2.</p><p>God wants our life to be a song. He has written the music for us in His Word and in the duties that come to us in our places and relations in life. The things we ought to do are the notes set upon the staff. To make our life beautiful music we must be obedient and submissive. Any disobedience is the singing of a false note, and yields discord.— J. R. Miller.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Psalms 100:2"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 252,
        "title": "When you pray, go into your room, shut the door, and pray to your Father in secret. Matt. 6. 6.",
        "content": "<p>When you pray, enter into your closet, and when you have shut your door, pray to your Father, which is in secret. Matt. 6. 6.</p><p><strong>Source and historical context:</strong> Theodore Cuyler strongly urges the practice of private prayer.</p><p><strong>Devotional focus:</strong> In Matthew 6, Jesus speaks against public religious performance and invites sincere prayer. Exhaustion, illness, grief, and disability can make ordinary prayer habits difficult. Their absence is not evidence of possession or exclusion from God’s care. Prayer may be a word, a silence, or a request spoken with a friend. God welcomes the weary rather than turning devotion into a threat.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> What gentle way of praying would serve me in my present circumstances?</p><p><strong>Practice:</strong> Choose a modest prayer practice you can sustain without fear or shame.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Jesus, receive our honest prayers and help us return to you with freedom.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Matthew 6:6"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 253,
        "title": "Be holy, for I am holy. 1 Pet. 1. 16.",
        "content": "<p>Be you holy; for I am holy. 1 Pet. 1. 16.</p><p>The highway of holiness is along the commonest road of life—along your very way. In wind and rain, no matter how it beats—it is only going hand in hand with Him.— Mark Guy Pearse.</p>",
        "references": [
          "1 Peter 1:16"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 254,
        "title": "And the Lord said, Shall I hide from Abraham that thing which I do? Gen. 18. 17.",
        "content": "<p>And the Lord said, Shall I hide from Abraham that thing which I do? Gen. 18. 17.</p><p>Abraham, in communion with God, knew long before Lot, in Sodom, of the destruction of that city. Oh for more communion!— Selected.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Genesis 18:17"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 255,
        "title": "The life which I now live in the flesh. Gal. 2. 20.",
        "content": "<p>The life which I now live in the flesh. Gal. 2. 20.</p><p>I expect to pass through this world but once—therefore, if there be any kindness I can show or any good thing I can do to any fellow human being, let me do it now; let me not defer or neglect it, for I shall not pass this way again.— Marcus Aurelius.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Galatians 2:20"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 256,
        "title": "Teach us to number our days so that we may gain wisdom. Psa. 90. 12.",
        "content": "<p>So teach us to number our days, that we may apply our hearts to wisdom. Psa. 90. 12.</p><p>Every day is a little life; and our whole life is but a day repeated: where it is that old Jacob numbers his life by days; and Moses desires to be taught this point of holy arithmetic—to number not his years, but his days. Those, therefore, that dare lose a day, are dangerously prodigal; those that dare misspend it, desperate.— Bishop Hall.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Psalms 90:12"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 257,
        "title": "Christ in you the hope of glory. Col. 1. 27.",
        "content": "<p>Christ in you the hope of glory. Col. 1. 27.</p><p>Religion is not the simple fire-escape that you build in anticipation of a possible danger, upon the outside of your dwelling, and leave there until danger comes. You go to it some morning when a fire breaks out in your house, and the poor old thing that you built up there, and thought that you could use some day, is so rusty and broken, and the weather has so beaten upon it and the sun so turned its hinges, that it will not work. That is the condition of a man who has built himself what seems a creed of faith, a trust in God in anticipation of the day when danger is to overtake him, and has said to himself, I am safe, for I will take refuge in it then. But religion is the house in which we live, it is the table at which we sit, it is the fireside at which we draw near, the room that arches its graceful and familiar presence over us; it is the bed on which we lie and think of the past, and anticipate the future, and gather our refreshment.— Phillips Brooks.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Colossians 1:27"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 258,
        "title": "Wait for the promise of the Father. Acts 1. 4.",
        "content": "<p>Wait for the promise of the Father. Acts 1. 4.</p><p>Wait at a promise till God meets you there. He always returns by way of His promises.— Selected.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Acts 1:4"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 259,
        "title": "This is the victory that overcometh the world, even our faith. 1 John 5. 4.",
        "content": "<p>This is the victory that overcomes the world, even our faith. 1 John 5. 4.</p><p>The world conquers me when it succeeds in hindering me from seeing, loving, holding communion with, and serving my Father, God. I conquer it when I lay my hand upon it and force it to help me to get nearer Him, to get more like Him, to think oftener of Him, to do His will more gladly and more constantly. The one victory over the world is to bend it to serve me in the highest things—the attainment of a clearer vision of the divine nature, the attainment of a deeper love to God Himself, and a more glad consecration and service to Him. That is the victory—when you can make the world a ladder to lift you to God. When the world comes between you and God as an obscuring screen, it has conquered you. When the world comes between you and God as a transparent medium you have conquered it. To win victory is to get it beneath your feet and stand upon it, and reach up by it to God.— Alex. McLaren.</p>",
        "references": [
          "1 John 5:4"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 260,
        "title": "He will command His angels to watch over you in all your ways. Psa. 91. 11.",
        "content": "<p>He shall give His angels charge over you to keep you in all your ways. Psa. 91. 11.</p><p><strong>Source and historical context:</strong> Mark Guy Pearse uses Psalm 91 to honor ordinary duties and service.</p><p><strong>Devotional focus:</strong> Psalm 91 is poetry about taking refuge in God. It should not be presented as an assurance that faithful people cannot be injured at work or while helping others. Jesus himself rejects a misuse of this psalm to justify unnecessary risk (Matthew 4:5–7). Serving God includes appropriate precautions and care for the well-being of others.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> What act of service would become wiser with better preparation or safety?</p><p><strong>Practice:</strong> Make one practical safety check before starting an everyday task.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> God our refuge, help us serve faithfully with wisdom and protection for others.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Psalms 91:11"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 261,
        "title": "At Jesus' feet. Luke 10. 39.",
        "content": "<p>At Jesus' feet. Luke 10. 39.</p><p>At Jesus' feet—that is our place of privilege and of blessing, and here it is that we are to be educated and fitted for the practical duties of life. Here we are to renew our strength while we wait on Him, and to learn how to mount on wings as eagles; and here we are to become possessed of that true knowledge which is power. Here we are to learn how real work is to be done, and to be armed with the true motive power to do it. Here we are to find solace amidst both the trials of work—and they are not few—and the trials of life in general; and here we are to anticipate something of the blessedness of heaven amidst the days of earth; for to sit at His feet is indeed to be in heavenly places, and to gaze upon His glory is to do what we shall never tire of doing yonder.— W. Hay Aitken.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Luke 10:39"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 262,
        "title": "God is love; and he that dwelleth in love dwelleth in God, and God in him. 1 John 4. 16.",
        "content": "<p>God is love; and he that dwells in love dwells in God, and God in him. 1 John 4. 16.</p><p>God is love; and it is good, as it is true, to think that every sun-ray that touches the earth has the sun at the other end of it; so every bit of love upon God's earth has God at the other end of it.— Mark Guy Pearse.</p><p>October 3rd.</p>",
        "references": [
          "1 John 4:16"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 263,
        "title": "They took knowledge of them that they had been with Jesus. Acts 4. 13.",
        "content": "<p>They took knowledge of them that they had been with Jesus. Acts 4. 13.</p><p>A Christian should be a striking likeness of Jesus Christ. You have read lives of Christ, beautifully and eloquently written, but the best life of Christ is His living biography, written out in the words and actions of His people. If we were what we profess to be, and what we should be, we would be pictures of Christ; yes, such striking likenesses of Him that the world would not have to hold us up by the hour together, and say, \"Well, it seems somewhat of a likeness\": but they would, when they once beheld us, exclaim, \"He has been with Jesus; he has been taught of Him; he is like Him; he has caught the very idea of the holy Man of Nazareth, and he works it out in his life and every day actions.\"— Spurgeon.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Acts 4:13"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 264,
        "title": "Be not afraid, only believe. Mark 5. 36.",
        "content": "<p>Be not afraid, only believe. Mark 5. 36.</p><p><strong>Source and historical context:</strong> The writer applies Jesus’ words to Jairus to storms in a believer’s life.</p><p><strong>Devotional focus:</strong> In Mark 5, Jesus comforts a frightened father and restores his daughter; the passage does not explain why every family suffers. It would be harmful to tell someone that God arranged illness or danger to increase faith. Courage includes accepting medical care, asking questions, and taking protective action. Faith can coexist with fear without needing to justify tragedy.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> Where can I offer presence instead of an explanation for someone’s distress?</p><p><strong>Practice:</strong> Help someone find practical assistance or accompany them through a hard conversation.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Merciful Jesus, stay close to the afraid and guide us to care for one another.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Mark 5:36"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 265,
        "title": "Happy is the man whom God correcteth. Job 5. 17.",
        "content": "<p>Happy is the man whom God corrects. Job 5. 17.</p><p><strong>Source and historical context:</strong> George Bowen repeats the claim that painful circumstances are always benevolent correction.</p><p><strong>Devotional focus:</strong> Job 5:17 is spoken by Eliphaz, one of Job’s friends; at the end of the book God rebukes these friends for failing to speak rightly about him (Job 42:7). The verse must not be read as proof that sickness or disaster is a punishment designed for improvement. The book of Job challenges easy explanations of suffering. Faithful friends listen, bring practical help, and refrain from making spiritual accusations.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> Have I repeated an explanation of suffering without checking who said it in Scripture?</p><p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read Job 5:17 in context with Job 42:7 and offer compassionate presence to someone hurting.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> God of Job, forgive our easy answers and make us truthful, patient companions.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Job 5:17"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 266,
        "title": "When they saw the star they rejoiced with exceeding great joy. Matt. 2. 10.",
        "content": "<p>When they saw the star they rejoiced with exceeding great joy. Matt. 2. 10.</p><p><strong>Source and historical context:</strong> Thomas Champness urges believers to rejoice at the Magi’s joy.</p><p><strong>Devotional focus:</strong> Matthew tells of the Magi rejoicing when they see the star. It does not require cheerful expressions from people who are depressed, bereaved, or frightened. Joy and tears coexist in the Christian story. The Gospels portray Jesus himself grieving and weeping. Christian hospitality can make room for those who are not able to smile today.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> Whose sorrow have I unintentionally judged because it was visible?</p><p><strong>Practice:</strong> Welcome someone as they are, with no pressure to appear cheerful.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Jesus who wept, teach me a joy spacious enough for sorrow.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Matthew 2:10"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 267,
        "title": "When I sit in darkness, the Lord will be my light. Micah 7. 8.",
        "content": "<p>When I sit in darkness the Lord shall be a light to me. Micah 7. 8.</p><p><strong>Source and historical context:</strong> A. J. Gordon reflects on finding light while trusting God in darkness.</p><p><strong>Devotional focus:</strong> Micah 7 speaks of hope amid communal wrongdoing and hardship; it does not tell readers to abandon thought or evidence. Christian faith can involve study, questions, careful reasoning, and humility. Mystery remains, but seeking understanding need not be a betrayal of trust. God invites truthfulness rather than the suppression of important questions.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> What question have I been afraid to examine thoughtfully?</p><p><strong>Practice:</strong> Explore one question with Scripture and a trustworthy, knowledgeable conversation partner.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> God of truth, make me open to learning, honest about uncertainty, and faithful in love.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Micah 7:8"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 268,
        "title": "I (Daniel) was left alone, and saw this great vision. Dan. 10. 8.",
        "content": "<p>I (Daniel) was left alone, and saw this great vision. Dan. 10. 8.</p><p>Solitude is the antechamber of God; only one step more and you can be in His immediate presence.— Landor.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Daniel 10:8"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 269,
        "title": "Come and dine. John 21. 12.",
        "content": "<p>Come and dine. John 21. 12.</p><p>This morning the voice of the Beloved of our soul is heard giving us His invitation.</p><p>\"Children,\" He asks, \"have you any food?\"</p><p>We answer, \"No; of ourselves we have nothing but hunger and starvation. O God, we cannot feed ourselves!\"</p><p>Then it is that His own sweet voice replies, \"Come and dine!\"— W. Hay Aitken.</p>",
        "references": [
          "John 21:12"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 270,
        "title": "O Lord God, you know! Ezek. 37. 3.",
        "content": "<p>O Lord God, You know! Ezek. 37. 3.</p><p>Here is the response of faith. \"You know!\"—what a pillow for the heart to repose upon! \"You know!\"—what few but comprehensive words to sum up and express the heart's difficulties and perplexities and trials. \"You know!\"—what an inexpressibly sweet resting-place in the midst of life's tumultuous heavings; in the midst of a sea that knows no calm; in the midst of a scene in which tossings to and fro are the hourly history! What an answer they contain for every heart that can find no words to express its big emotions; for a heart whose sorrows are too deep for language to find its way to God! Oh, that they were ever uppermost in the soul, as the response to every difficulty in our path! They are God's answer to everything we cannot fathom; God's answer for our hearts to rest upon, and our lips to utter, when every way is hedged up so that we cannot pass. \"O Lord God, you know!\" Rest here, believer. Lean your soul on these words. Repose calmly on the bosom of your God, and carry them with you into every scene of life. \"O Lord God, you know.\"— F. Whitfield.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Ezekiel 37:3"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 271,
        "title": "There was a ladder set up on the earth, with its top reaching heaven. Gen. 28. 12.",
        "content": "<p>See, a ladder set up on the earth, and the top of it reached to heaven. Gen. 28. 12.</p><p>Think of that mystic ladder, which descends from the throne of God to the spot, however lowly, where you may be. It may be a moorland waste; a humble cottage; a ship's cabin; a settler's hut; a bed of pain; but Jesus Christ finds you out, and comes just where you are. The one pole of this ladder is the gold of His deity; the other is the silver of His manhood; the rungs are the series of events from the cradle of Bethlehem to the right hand of power, where He sits. That ladder sways beneath a weight of blessing for you. Oh, that you would send away your burdens of sin, and care, and fear, by the hands of the ascending angels of prayer and faith!—so as to be able to receive into your heart the trooping angels of peace, and joy, and love, and glory.— F. B. Meyer.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Genesis 28:12"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 272,
        "title": "Surely God is in this place, and I knew it not. Gen. 28. 16.",
        "content": "<p><strong>Read:</strong> Genesis 28:16; Deuteronomy 4:1; Galatians 2:20</p><p><strong>Source and historical context:</strong> A compiled anthology day joins reflections on Jacob, Moses, and Paul.</p><p><strong>Devotional focus:</strong> Deuteronomy 4 calls Israel to listen and live faithfully, while Scripture also records debate, lament, poetry, and searching questions. Careful study and discussion can be acts of obedience rather than obstacles to it. Christian reading should consider speaker, audience, genre, and the welfare of those affected by our interpretation. Faithful action grows stronger when it is informed by humble understanding.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> Which Bible passage would benefit from closer context and thoughtful conversation?</p><p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read Deuteronomy 4:1–9 with a trusted study partner and ask both what it means and how to practice it.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> God of wisdom, guide our questions and our actions toward truth and love.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Genesis 28:16",
          "Deuteronomy 4:1",
          "Galatians 2:20"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 273,
        "title": "Let us who are of the day be sober, putting on the breastplate of faith and love, and for a helmet, the hope of salvation. 1 Thess. 5. 8.",
        "content": "<p>Let us who are of the day be sober, putting on the breastplate of faith and love, and for a helmet, the hope of salvation. 1 Thess. 5. 8.</p><p>Faith, love, hope —these three form the defensive armor that guards the soul; and these three make self-control possible. Like a diver in his dress who is let down to the bottom of the wild, far-weltering ocean, a man whose heart is girt by faith and charity, and whose head is covered with the helmet of hope, may be dropped down into the wildest sea of temptation and of worldliness, and yet will walk dry and unharmed through the midst of its depths, and breathe air that comes from a world above the restless surges. Faith will bring you into communication with all the power of God. Love will lead you into a region where all the temptations round you will be touched as by Ithuriel's spear, and will show their own foulness. And Hope will turn away your eyes from looking at the tempting splendor around, and fix them upon the glories that are above. And so the reins will come into your hands in an altogether new manner, and you will be able to be king over your own nature in a fashion that you did not dream of before, if only you will trust in Christ and love Him, and fix your desires on the things above. Then you will be able to govern yourself, when you let Christ govern you.— Alex. McLaren.</p>",
        "references": [
          "1 Thessalonians 5:8"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 274,
        "title": "The word of our God shall stand forever. Isa. 40. 8.",
        "content": "<p>The word of our God shall stand forever. Isa. 40. 8.</p><p>The Word of God is the water of life; the more you lave it forth, the fresher it runs. It is the fire of God's glory; the more you blow it, the clearer it burns. It is the corn of the Lord's field; the better you grind it, the more it yields. It is the bread of heaven; the more it is broken and given forth, the more it remains. It is the sword of the Spirit; the more it is scoured, the brighter it shines.— Bishop Jewel.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Isaiah 40:8"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 275,
        "title": "I spoke to you in your prosperity. Jer. 22. 21.",
        "content": "<p>I spoke to you in your prosperity. Jer. 22. 21.</p><p><strong>Source and historical context:</strong> Ludlow pictures prosperity as blinding glare and affliction as God’s corrective hand.</p><p><strong>Devotional focus:</strong> Jeremiah 22 is a prophetic warning to a ruler about ignoring justice and responsibility. It is not a claim that every person’s sickness, grief, or poverty is a blessing sent to correct them. Scripture calls leaders to protect the poor and do what is right. In hardship we may find support and new perspectives, but neither makes the harm good or removes the need for relief.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> What does the passage teach about justice rather than guessing why hardship occurs?</p><p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read Jeremiah 22:13–17 and take one step toward fair treatment of a vulnerable neighbor.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> God of justice, teach us humility in prosperity and practical care in suffering.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Jeremiah 22:21"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 276,
        "title": "Surely He will deliver you from the fowler's snare. Psa. 91. 3.",
        "content": "<p>Surely He shall deliver you from the snare of the fowler. Psa. 91. 3.</p><p>He shall deliver you from the snare of the fowler. That is, from the little things, the hidden traps and nets that are set for us. Great sins frighten where little snares entangle. It is easier to escape the huntsman's arrow than the crafty lure.</p><p>And where are they not set? Riches and poverty, sickness and strength, prosperity and adversity, friendship and loneliness, the work and the want of it—each has its snare, in which not only are the unwary caught, but the wise and the watchful sometimes fall a prey. Little things, mere threads, hardly worth guarding against—yet they are strong enough to hold us and hinder us, and may be the beginning of our destruction.— Mark Guy Pearse.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Psalms 91:3"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 277,
        "title": "The Lord set a mark upon Cain. Gen. 4. 15.",
        "content": "<p>The Lord set a mark upon Cain. Gen. 4. 15.</p><p>We speak of the mark of Cain as if it was the mark of a curse. In reality it was the mark of God's mercy, a defence against his enemies.— D. J. Burrell.</p><p>Who is among you that fears the Lord . . . that walks in darkness, and has no light? Let him trust in the name of the Lord, and stay upon his God. Isa. 50. 10.</p><p>\"In fierce storms,\" said an old seaman, \"we can do but one thing, there is only one way; we must put the ship in a certain position and keep her there.\"</p><p>This, Christian, is what you must do. Sometimes, like Paul, you can see neither sun nor stars, and no small tempest lies on you; and then you can do but one thing; there is only one way. Reason cannot help you. Past experiences give you no light. Even prayer fetches no consolation. Only a single course is left. You must put your soul in one position and keep it there. You must stay upon the Lord; and, come what may—winds, waves, cross seas, thunder, lightning, frowning rocks, roaring breakers—no matter what, you must lash yourself to the helm, and hold fast your confidence in God's faithfulness, His covenant engagement, His everlasting love in Christ Jesus.— Richard Fuller.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Genesis 4:15",
          "Isaiah 50:10"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 278,
        "title": "Be faithful until death, and I will give you a crown of life. Rev. 2. 10.",
        "content": "<p>Be you faithful to death, and I will give you a crown of life. Rev. 2. 10.</p><p>There is a heaven at the end of every faithful Christian's journey.— Cuyler.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Revelation 2:10"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 279,
        "title": "Flee into Egypt. Matt. 2. 13.",
        "content": "<p>Flee into Egypt. Matt. 2. 13.</p><p>Why? Because there is a cruel king who will seek the young child's life.</p><p>Is Christ born in you? Is your life like that manger—precious as a casket, because of what it holds? Then have a care; for, craftier and more unscrupulous than Herod, the destroyer of souls will seek to destroy you.</p><p>There is a day coming when they shall say, \"They are dead which sought the young child's life.\" Grace shall survive the foe, and we shall yet return to enjoy the comforts of life, with no Herod to threaten us. After all, it is sin which is short-lived, for goodness shall flourish when the evil one is chained up forever.— Thos. Champness.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Matthew 2:13"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 280,
        "title": "Your servant will do as my lord the king has said. 1 Kings 2. 38.",
        "content": "<p>As my lord the king has said, so will your servant do. 1 Kings 2. 38.</p><p>There is something infinitely better than doing a great thing for God, and the infinitely better thing is to be where God wants us to be, to do what God wants us to do, and to have no will apart from His.— G. Campbell Morgan.</p>",
        "references": [
          "1 Kings 2:38"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 281,
        "title": "Let your light so shine before men, that they may see your good works, and glorify your Father which is in heaven. Matt. 5. 16.",
        "content": "<p>Let your light so shine before men, that they may see your good works, and glorify your Father which is in heaven. Matt. 5. 16.</p><p>They say the world has an eagle eye for anything inconsistent, an eye sharp to discover the vagaries and inconsistencies in the defaulty and the unworthy. It has an eagle eye; but the eagle winks before the sun, and the burning iris of its eye shrinks abashed before the unsullied purity of noon. Let your light so shine before men, that others, awed and charmed by the consistency of your godly life, may come to enquire, and to say you have been with Jesus.— Punshon.</p><p>The eleven disciples went . . . into a mountain where Jesus had appointed them . . . Jesus came and spoke to them saying . . . Go you and teach all nations. Matt. 28. 16, 18, 19.</p><p>The considerable actions in the world have usually very small beginnings. Of a few letters, how many thousand words are made! Of ten figures, how many thousand numbers! A point is the beginning of all geometry. A little stone flung into a pond makes a little circle, then a greater, till it enlarges itself to both the sides. So from small beginnings God does cause an efflux through the whole world.— Charnock.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Matthew 5:16",
          "Matthew 28:16",
          "Matthew 28:18-19"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 282,
        "title": "Listen, I bring you good news of great joy for all people. Luke 2. 10.",
        "content": "<p>See, I bring you good tidings of great joy, which shall be to all people. Luke 2. 10.</p><p>It is true that these good tidings of great joy were to be \"for all people,\" but not first . The message falls on our own ears, and is first for our own souls.</p><p>Oh, ponder this well! Take all God's truths home first to your own heart. Ask in earnest prayer that the Spirit may write them with the pen of heaven on your own conscience. Then will you be a vessel fitted for the Master's use, and carry His message with spiritual power to the souls of others.— F. Whitfield.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Luke 2:10"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 283,
        "title": "The Lord disciplines those He loves. Heb. 12. 6.",
        "content": "<p>Whom the Lord loves He chastens. Heb. 12. 6.</p><p><strong>Source and historical context:</strong> F. B. Meyer develops an extended image of sorrow as God’s classroom.</p><p><strong>Devotional focus:</strong> Hebrews 12 uses family imagery for the formation of believers, but this cannot justify abuse or the withholding of appropriate care. We should not declare that God chose every hardship or that a sufferer must submit without question. Learning amid difficulty is possible; so are lament, resistance to injustice, and the search for relief. God’s love should not be made to sound like indifference to pain.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> When have I expected someone to accept suffering instead of seeking safety?</p><p><strong>Practice:</strong> Offer a practical resource or a patient listening ear to someone bearing sorrow.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Loving Father, draw near in trouble and keep our response compassionate and wise.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Hebrews 12:6"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 284,
        "title": "Whatever you ask the Father in my name, He will give you. John 16. 23.",
        "content": "<p>Whatever you shall ask the Father in my name, He will give it you. John 16. 23.</p><p>Prayer must be based upon promise, but, thank God, His promises are always broader than our prayers! No fear of building inverted pyramids here, for Jesus Christ is the foundation.— Frances Ridley Havergal.</p><p>He rises from supper, and laid aside His garments; and took a towel, and girded Himself. After that He pours water into a basin, and began to wash the disciples' feet, and to wipe them with the towel with which He was girded. John 13. 4, 5.</p><p>Acts are common and mean because they are ordinarily expressive of the common and mean thoughts of men. Let us not accuse the acts that make up our daily life of meanness, but our ignoble souls that reveal themselves so unworthily through those acts. The same act may successively mount up through every intermediate stage from the depth of unworthiness to a transcendent height of excellence, according to the soul that is manifested by it. One of the glorious ends of our Lord's incarnation was that He might propitiate us with the details of life, so that we should not disdain these as insignificant, but rather disdain ourselves for our inability to make these details interpreters of a noble nature. Oh, let us then look with affectionateness and gratitude upon the daily details of life, seeing the sanctifying imprint of the hand of Jesus upon them all!— George Bowen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "John 16:23",
          "John 13:4-5"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 285,
        "title": "He placed . . . cherubims, and a flaming sword . . . to keep the way of the tree of life. Gen. 3. 24.",
        "content": "<p>He placed . . . cherubims, and a flaming sword . . . to keep the way of the tree of life. Gen. 3. 24.</p><p>Editorial reflection: Genesis pictures the way to the tree of life closed after human rebellion. The final vision of Scripture holds out life with God again. Let that promised future steady you today: Christ's work gives hope stronger than loss and calls you to walk in his light.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Genesis 3:24"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 286,
        "title": "Blessed are they that do His commandments, that they may have right to the tree of life. Rev. 22. 14.",
        "content": "<p>Blessed are they that do His commandments, that they may have right to the tree of life. Rev. 22. 14.</p><p>How remarkable and how beautiful it is that the last page of the Revelation should come bending round to touch the first page of Genesis. The history of man began with angels with frowning faces and flaming swords barring the way to the Tree of Life. It ends with the guard of cherubim withdrawn; or rather, perhaps, sheathing their swords and becoming guides to the no longer forbidden fruit, instead of being its guards. That is the Bible's grand symbolical way of saying that all between—the sin, the misery, the death—is a parenthesis. God's purpose is not going to be thwarted. The end of His majestic march through history is to be men's access to the Tree of Life, from which, for the dreary ages—that are but as a moment in the great eternities—they were barred out by their sin,— Alex. McLaren.</p><p>That the God of our Lord Jesus Christ, the Father of glory, may give to you the spirit of wisdom and revelation in the knowledge of Him: the eyes of your understanding being enlightened. Eph. 1. 17, 18.</p><p>We were coming down a mountain in Switzerland one evening, when a black thunder-storm blotted out the day, and all things were suddenly plunged into darkness. We could only dimly see the narrow, dusty footpaths, and the gloomy sides that were swallowed up in deeper gloom. What, then, of the majesty all about us, heights, and depths, and wonders? All was darkness. Then came the lightning—not flashes, but the blazing of the whole sky, incessant, and on every side. What recesses of glory we gazed into! What marvels of splendor shone out of the darkness!</p><p>Think how with us, in us, is One who comes to make the common, dusty ways of life resplendent, illuminating our dull thoughts by the light of the glory of God; clearing the vision of the soul, and then revealing the greatness of the salvation that is ours in Christ.— Mark Guy Pearse.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Revelation 22:14",
          "Ephesians 1:17-18"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 287,
        "title": "Jesus was left alone, and the woman standing in the midst. John 8. 9.",
        "content": "<p>Jesus was left alone, and the woman standing in the midst. John 8. 9.</p><p>Alone with Jesus! What a sweet and holy spot! What a blessed refuge to which the soul may betake itself from the charges of Satan, the accusations of the world, and the sorrows of life! Sweet spot for the heart to unfold itself, to tell its hidden tale in the ear of Infinite love, tenderness, and compassion!</p><p>Alone with Jesus! How different a front would Christianity present to the world if the Lord's people were oftener there! What humility, and gentleness, and love, would characterize all their dealings! What holiness stamped on the very brow, that all might read! What few judgments passed on others, how many more on ourselves! What calmness and resignation and joyful submission to all the Lord's dealings!</p><p>Be much \"alone with Jesus!\" Then will the passage to glory be one of sunshine, whether it be through the portals of the grave or through the clouds of heaven.— F. Whitfield.</p>",
        "references": [
          "John 8:9"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 288,
        "title": "You will show me the path of life; in your presence is fullness of joy; at your right hand are pleasures forever. Psa. 16. 11.",
        "content": "<p>You will show me the path of life: in Your presence is fullness of joy; at Your right hand there are pleasures forever. Psa. 16. 11.</p><p>The man who walks along the path of life lives in the presence of the joy-giving God. Just in so far as he is true to that path of life, and wanders neither to the right hand nor to the left, his joy becomes deeper, no! he becomes partaker of that very fullness of joy in which God Himself lives, and moves, and has His being. And while such is his experience in the midst of all the trials of life, he has also the privilege of looking forward to grander things yet in store for him, when that higher world shall be reached, and the shadows of time have passed away forever. \"At Your right hand,\" exclaims the psalmist, \"there are pleasures forever.\"— W. Hay Aitken.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Psalms 16:11"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 289,
        "title": "Be clothed with humility. 1 Pet. 5. 5.",
        "content": "<p>Be clothed with humility. 1 Pet. 5. 5.</p><p>Is it not one of the difficulties of church work that we have more officers than men? We need more of the rank and file, who are willing to march anywhere, and to do the lowliest of tasks. We shall succeed in doing greater things when we are all of us willing to be subject. It is the bayonet rather than the gold lace which is wanted when the enemy is to be subdued.— Thomas Champness.</p>",
        "references": [
          "1 Peter 5:5"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 290,
        "title": "Jacob looked up and saw Esau coming with four hundred men. Gen. 33. 1.",
        "content": "<p>Jacob lifted up his eyes, and looked, and see, Esau came, and with him four hundred men. Gen. 33. 1.</p><p>Do not lift up your eyes and look for Esaus. Those who look for troubles will not be long without finding trouble to look at. Lift them higher—to Him from whom our help comes. Then you will be able to meet your troubles with an unperturbed spirit. Those who have seen the face of God need not fear the face of man that shall die. To have power with God is to have power over all the evils that threaten us.— F. B. Meyer.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Genesis 33:1"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 291,
        "title": "Let us cleanse ourselves from all filthiness of the flesh and spirit, perfecting holiness in the fear of God. 2 Cor. 7. 1.",
        "content": "<p>Let us cleanse ourselves from all filthiness of the flesh and spirit, perfecting holiness in the fear of God. 2 Cor. 7. 1.</p><p>The Tree of Life, according to some of the old rabbinical legends, lifted its branches, by an indwelling motion, high above impure hands that were stretched to touch them; and until our hands are cleansed through faith in Jesus Christ, its richest fruit hangs unreachable, golden above our heads. The fullness of the life of heaven is only granted to those who, drawing near Jesus Christ by faith on earth, have by it cleansed themselves from all filthiness of the flesh and spirit.— Alex. McLaren.</p>",
        "references": [
          "2 Corinthians 7:1"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 292,
        "title": "The pillar of the cloud went from before their face, and stood behind them. Ex. 14. 19.",
        "content": "<p>The pillar of the cloud went from before their face, and stood behind them. Ex. 14. 19.</p><p>It is not always guidance that we most need. Many of our dangers come upon us from behind. They are stealthy, insidious, assaulting us when we are unaware of their nearness. The tempter is cunning and shrewd. He does not meet us full front. It is a comfort to know that Christ comes behind us when it is there we need the protection.— J. R. Miller.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Exodus 14:19"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 293,
        "title": "Iniquities overwhelm me; you will forgive our transgressions. Psa. 65. 3.",
        "content": "<p>Iniquities prevail against me: as for our transgressions, You shall purge them away. Psa. 65. 3.</p><p>There is much earnest religion that lives in the dreary compass of these first four words, \"Iniquities prevail against me,\" and never gets a glimpse beyond it. But do not put a full stop there. Fetch in One who can help. \"As for our transgressions, You will purge them away.\" The moment we bring the Lord in, that moment defeat is turned to triumphant deliverance!</p><p>Write that up in golden letters— You ! And do not find in this word only a trembling hope, or a wondering wish. Listen to its full assurance— You will !</p><p>There is but one result that can warrant the agony of Calvary; there is but one result that can satisfy either our blessed Savior or ourselves; and that is our being conquerors over sin.— Mark Guy Pearse.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Psalms 65:3"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 294,
        "title": "Speaking the truth in love. Eph. 4. 15.",
        "content": "<p>Speaking the truth in love. Eph. 4. 15.</p><p>The best way of eradicating error is to publish and practice truth.— W. Arnot.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Ephesians 4:15"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 295,
        "title": "So he arose, and went to Zarephath. 1 Kings 17. 10.",
        "content": "<p>So he arose, and went to Zarephath. 1 Kings 17. 10.</p><p>Let it be equally said of you to whatever duty the Lord may call you away, \"He arose and went.\" Be the way ever so laborious or dangerous, still arise, like Elijah, and go. Go cheerfully, in faith, keeping your heart quietly dependent on the Lord, and in the end you will surely see and sing of His goodness. Though tossed on a sea of troubles you may anchor on the firm foundation of God, which stands sure. You have for your security His exceeding great and precious promises, and may say with the psalmist, \"Why are you cast down, O my soul? and why are you disquieted within me? Hope you in God, for I shall yet praise Him who is the health of my countenance, and my God.\"— F. W. Krummacher.</p>",
        "references": [
          "1 Kings 17:10"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 296,
        "title": "A daily rate for every day. 2 Kings 25. 30.",
        "content": "<p>A daily rate for every day. 2 Kings 25. 30.</p><p>The acts of breathing which I performed yesterday will not keep me alive today; I must continue to breathe afresh every moment, or animal life ceases. In like manner yesterday's grace and spiritual strength must be renewed, and the Holy Spirit must continue to breathe on my soul from moment to moment in order to my enjoying the consolations, and to my working the works of God.— Toplady.</p><p>And when the vessel that he made of the clay was marred in the hand of the potter, he made it again another vessel, as seemed good to the potter to make it. Jer. 18. 4. (R. V.)</p><p>God's fairest, highest place of service in the land that lies beyond will be filled by the men and women who have been broken upon the wheel on earth.— G. Campbell Morgan.</p>",
        "references": [
          "2 Kings 25:30",
          "Jeremiah 18:4"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 297,
        "title": "Examine yourselves. 2 Cor. 13. 5.",
        "content": "<p>Examine yourselves. 2 Cor. 13. 5.</p><p>If your state be good, searching into it will give you that comfort of it. If your state be bad, searching into it cannot make it worse; no, it is the only way to make it better, for conversion begins with conviction.— Bishop Hopkins.</p>",
        "references": [
          "2 Corinthians 13:5"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 298,
        "title": "Choose today whom you will serve. Josh. 24. 15.",
        "content": "<p>Choose you this day whom you will serve. Josh. 24. 15.</p><p>Choice and service —these were demanded of the Israelites; these are demanded of you, these only. Choice and service—in these are the whole of life.— Mark Hopkins.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Joshua 24:15"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 299,
        "title": "Lord, you have been our dwelling place through all generations. Psa. 90. 1.",
        "content": "<p>Lord, you have been our dwelling place in all generations. Psa. 90. 1.</p><p>You cannot detain the eagle in the forest. You may gather around him a chorus of the choicest birds; you may give him a perch on the goodliest pine; you may charge winged messengers to bring him choicest dainties; but he will spurn them all. Spreading his lordly wings, and with his eye on the Alpine cliff, he will soar away to his own ancestral halls amid the munitions of rocks and the wild music of tempest and waterfall.</p><p>The soul of man, in its eagle soarings, will rest with nothing short of the Rock of Ages. Its ancestral halls are the halls of heaven. Its munitions of rocks are the attributes of God. The sweep of its majestic flight is Eternity! \"Lord, You have been our dwelling place in all generations!\"— Macduff.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Psalms 90:1"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 300,
        "title": "He has said. Heb. 13. 5.",
        "content": "<p>He has said. Heb. 13. 5.</p><p>If we can only grasp these words of faith, we have an all-conquering weapon in our hand. What doubt is there that will not be slain by this two-edged sword? What fear is there which shall not fall smitten with a deadly wound before this arrow from the bow of God's covenant? \"He has said!\" Yes; whether for delight in our quietude, or for strength in our conflict, \"He has said!\" must be our daily resort.</p><p>Since \"He has said\" is the source of all wisdom, and the fountain of all comfort, let it dwell in you richly, as \"a well of water, springing up to everlasting life.\" So shall you grow healthy, strong, and happy, in the divine life.— Spurgeon.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Hebrews 13:5"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 301,
        "title": "Not I, but Christ liveth in me. Gal. 2. 20.",
        "content": "<p>Not I, but Christ lives in me. Gal. 2. 20.</p><p><strong>Source and historical context:</strong> Phillips Brooks emphasizes practical Christian obedience and closeness to Jesus.</p><p><strong>Devotional focus:</strong> Galatians 2:20 speaks of life shaped by faith in Christ, not instantaneous certainty about every complex decision. Difficult ethical choices often demand careful information, wise counsel, and humility about consequences. A slow decision can be conscientious rather than faithless. Following Christ includes asking whose voice is missing and whose well-being may be affected.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> What decision deserves patience instead of pretending the right answer is obvious?</p><p><strong>Practice:</strong> Seek advice and relevant facts before making one important choice.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Wise Lord, teach me to act faithfully while remaining willing to learn and correct course.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Galatians 2:20"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 302,
        "title": "Who is my neighbor? Luke 10. 29.",
        "content": "<p>Who is my neighbor? Luke 10. 29.</p><p>\"Who is your neighbor?\" It is the sufferer, wherever, whoever, whatever he be. Wherever you hear the cry of distress, wherever you see anyone brought across your path by the chances and changes of life (that is, by the Providence of God), whom it is in your power to help—he, stranger or enemy though he be— he is your neighbor.— A. P. Stanley.</p><p>He which establishes us . . . in Christ, and has anointed us, is God; who has also sealed us, and given the earnest of the Spirit in our hearts. 2 Cor. 1. 21, 22.</p><p>When a Christian is \"sealed\" by the Holy Spirit, \"sealed\" as the property of his Master, there will be no need to ask, \"Whose image and superscription is this\" upon the \"sealed\" one? The King's, of course. Anyone can see the image.</p><p>Of what use is a \"seal\" if it cannot be seen?</p><p>Is the King's image visibly, permanently, stamped upon us? It is on every Spirit-filled, \"sealed\" believer.— John McNeil.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Luke 10:29",
          "2 Corinthians 1:21-22"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 303,
        "title": "They shall rejoice, and shall see the plummet in the hand of Zerubbabel. Zech. 4. 10.",
        "content": "<p>They shall rejoice, and shall see the plummet in the hand of Zerubbabel. Zech. 4. 10.</p><p>It is joy to the Christian to know that the plummet is now in the hands of our great Zerubbabel, and that when He comes forth, the world's misrule shall be over. The false standards and false estimates of men shall be swept away. The standards of \"expediency,\" of \"conscience,\" of \"every man thinking as he likes, if he is only sincere \"—these, and all similar refuges of lies shall be like a spider's web. The measure of all things will be Christ, and Christ the Measurer of all things.</p><p>How everything will be reversed! What a turning upside down of all that now exists!</p><p>Blessed day, and longed for—the world's great jubilee, the earth's long-looked-for Sabbath, groaning creation's joy, and nature's calm repose! Who would not cry, \"Come, Lord Jesus, and end this troubled dream! Shatter the shadows of the long, dark night of sin and sorrow, sighing and tears, despair and death!\"— F. Whitfield.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Zechariah 4:10"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 304,
        "title": "In the world you will have trouble; but take heart, for I have overcome the world. John 16. 33.",
        "content": "<p>In the world you shall have tribulation: but be of good cheer; I have overcome the world. John 16. 33.</p><p><strong>Source and historical context:</strong> J. R. Miller interprets tribulation as the instrument that purifies believers.</p><p><strong>Devotional focus:</strong> In John 16, Jesus warns his disciples that trouble will come and offers peace in him. The passage does not say God must injure people to make them good. Harm can damage lives and deserves prevention and treatment. Spiritual formation also grows through love, community, study, gratitude, and rest. Hope in Christ encourages care, not an obligation to suffer.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> Where have I assumed that pain is the only route to maturity?</p><p><strong>Practice:</strong> Choose one healthy practice of growth that does not involve harm to yourself or others.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Prince of Peace, strengthen us in trouble and teach us to nurture life gently.</p>",
        "references": [
          "John 16:33"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 305,
        "title": "I . . . heard behind me a great voice, as of a trumpet, saying . . . Write. Rev. 1. 10, 11.",
        "content": "<p>I . . . heard behind me a great voice, as of a trumpet, saying . . . Write. Rev. 1. 10, 11.</p><p>It is very sweet to note that a voice from heaven said to John, \"Write.\" Does not that voice come to us? Are there not those who would taste the joys of heaven if we wrote them words of forgiveness and affection? Are there not others who would dry their tears if we would remind them of past joys, when we were poor as they are now? No, could not some, who read these plain words, place inside the envelope something bearing their signature which would make the widow's heart dance for joy?</p><p>What is our pen doing? Is it adding joy to other men's lives? If so, then angels may tune their harps when we sit at our desk. They are sent to minister to the heirs of salvation, and would be glad to look upon our pen as writing music for them to sing, because what we write makes their client's joy to be full.— Thomas Champness.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Revelation 1:10-11"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 306,
        "title": "The Lord disciplines those He loves. Heb. 12. 6.",
        "content": "<p>Whom the Lord loves He chastens. Heb. 12. 6.</p><p>We should ever bear in mind that the discipline of our heavenly Father's hand is to be interpreted in the light of our Father's countenance; and the deep mysteries of His moral government to be contemplated through the medium or His tender love.— Selected.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Hebrews 12:6"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 307,
        "title": "Faithful is He that calleth you, who also will do it. 1 Thess. 5. 24.",
        "content": "<p>Faithful is He that calls you, who also will do it. 1 Thess. 5. 24.</p><p>Earthly faithfulness is possible only by the reception of heavenly gifts. As surely as every leaf that grows is mainly water that the plant has got from the clouds, and carbon that it has got out of the atmosphere, so surely will all our good be mainly drawn from heaven and heaven's gifts. As certainly as every lump of coal that you put upon your fire contains in itself sunbeams that have been locked up for all these millenniums that have passed since it waved green in the forest, so certainly does every good deed embody in itself gifts from above. And no man is pure except by impartation; and every good thing and every perfect thing comes from the Father of lights.— Alex. McLaren.</p>",
        "references": [
          "1 Thessalonians 5:24"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 308,
        "title": "Singing with grace in your hearts to the Lord. Col. 3. 16.",
        "content": "<p>Singing with grace in your hearts to the Lord. Col. 3. 16.</p><p>Remember your life is to be a singing life. This world is God's grand cathedral for you. You are to be one of God's choristers, and there is to be a continual eucharistic sacrifice of praise and thanksgiving going up from your heart, with which God shall be continually well pleased. And there should be not only the offering of the lips, but the surrender of the life with joy. Yes, with joy , and not with constraint . Every faculty of our nature should be presented to Him in gladsome service, for the Lord Jehovah is my song as well as my strength.— W. Hay Aitken.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Colossians 3:16"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 309,
        "title": "Call to remembrance the former days. Heb. 10. 32.",
        "content": "<p>Call to remembrance the former days. Heb. 10. 32.</p><p>The former days —times of trial, conflict, discouragement, temptation. Did we oftener call these to remembrance, with how much more delight would we make the covert of God's faithfulness our refuge, exclaiming with the psalmist, \"Because You have been my help, therefore in the shadow of Your wings will I rejoice.\"— R. Fuller.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Hebrews 10:32"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 310,
        "title": "The Lord . . . your dwelling place. Psa. 91. 9.",
        "content": "<p>The Lord . . . your habitation. Psa. 91. 9.</p><p>We go home without arrangement. We plan our visits, and then go home because they are over. Duty, want, a host of things, lead us forth elsewhere; but the heart takes us home. Blessed, most blessed is he whose thoughts pass up to God, not because they are driven like a fisherman's craft swept by the fierceness of the storm, not because they are forced by want or fear, not because they are led by the hand of duty, but because God is in his habitation and his home. Loosed from other things, the thoughts go home for rest.</p><p>In God the blessed man finds the love that welcomes. There is the sunny place. There care is loosed and toil forgotten. There is the joyous freedom, the happy calm, the rest, and renewing of our strength—at home with God.— Mark Guy Pearse.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Psalms 91:9"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 311,
        "title": "These have turned the world upside down. Acts 17. 6.",
        "content": "<p>These have turned the world upside down. Acts 17. 6.</p><p>Editorial reflection: The early Christians were accused of unsettling the world. Their witness was sustained by loyalty to Jesus, not by chasing influence for its own sake. Ask where a steady, truthful, and compassionate life might challenge an unjust habit around you.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Acts 17:6"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 312,
        "title": "None of these things move me. Acts 20. 24.",
        "content": "<p>None of these things move me. Acts 20. 24.</p><p>The men that move the world are the ones who do not let the world move them.— Selected.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Acts 20:24"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 313,
        "title": "Jacob Wrestles and Receives a Blessing",
        "content": "<p><strong>Read:</strong> Genesis 32:24–32 (Jacob’s struggle and blessing).</p><p><strong>Source and historical context:</strong> F. B. Meyer interprets Jacob’s injured hip as God’s necessary correction.</p><p><strong>Devotional focus:</strong> Genesis 32 describes a mysterious night struggle, blessing, and lasting limp. The touching of Jacob’s hip occurs earlier in the passage than the original epigraph’s verse 32, which explains a dietary custom. Do not tell people that a disability or injury proves divine punishment or resistance to blessing. The story can invite honest wrestling with fear, vulnerability, and the hope that God remains present.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> How can I honor the experience of someone living with injury rather than supplying an explanation?</p><p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read Genesis 32:24–32 and check the wording of the epigraph against the passage.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> God who meets Jacob, stay close in our vulnerability and teach us reverent compassion.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Genesis 32:25-32"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 314,
        "title": "With God all things are possible. Mark 10. 27.",
        "content": "<p>With God all things are possible. Mark 10. 27.</p><p>Unbelief says, \"How can such and such things be?\" It is full of \"hows\"; but faith has one great answer to the ten thousand \"hows,\" and that answer is— God !— C. H. M.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Mark 10:27"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 315,
        "title": "You are the temple of the living God; as God has said, I will dwell among them and walk among them. 2 Cor. 6. 16.",
        "content": "<p>You are the temple of the living God; as God has said, I will dwell in them, and walk in them. 2 Cor. 6. 16.</p><p>These temples were reared for Him. Let Him fill them so completely that, like the oriental temple of glass in the ancient legend, the temple shall not be seen, but only the glorious sunlight, which not only shines into it, but through it, and the transparent walls are all unseen.— A. B. Simpson.</p>",
        "references": [
          "2 Corinthians 6:16"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 316,
        "title": "Without Christ. Eph. 2. 12.",
        "content": "<p>Without Christ. Eph. 2. 12.</p><p>Without a hope to cheer, a Pilot to steer, a Friend to counsel, grace to sustain, heaven to welcome us, and God to console!— Selected.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Ephesians 2:12"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 317,
        "title": "When I am weak, then am I strong. 2 Cor. 12. 10.",
        "content": "<p>When I am weak, then am I strong. 2 Cor. 12. 10.</p><p>This is God's way. We advance by going backwards, we become strong by becoming weak, we become wise by being fools.— F. Whitfield.</p>",
        "references": [
          "2 Corinthians 12:10"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 318,
        "title": "Holy men of God spoke as they were moved by the Holy Spirit. 2 Pet. 1. 21.",
        "content": "<p>Holy men of God spoke as they were moved by the Holy Spirit. 2 Pet. 1. 21.</p><p><strong>Source and historical context:</strong> Spurgeon celebrates Scripture’s authority with a highly specific account of inspiration.</p><p><strong>Devotional focus:</strong> 2 Peter 1:21 speaks of people moved by the Holy Spirit, but Christian traditions differ about the mechanics of biblical inspiration and interpretation. Scripture contains distinct literary forms and recognizable human voices. Reverence for the Bible does not require claiming that every sentence was mechanically dictated. Careful reading attends to genre, context, and the loving purposes of the text.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> What difference would literary context make to my understanding of one biblical passage?</p><p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read 2 Peter 1:19–21 and compare two different biblical genres, such as a psalm and a letter.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Spirit of truth, guide my reading with reverence, care, and humility.</p>",
        "references": [
          "2 Peter 1:21"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 319,
        "title": "They all forsook Him, and fled. Mark 14. 50.",
        "content": "<p>They all forsook Him, and fled. Mark 14. 50.</p><p>Separation never comes from His side.— J. Hudson Taylor.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Mark 14:50"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 320,
        "title": "Belshazzar the king made a great feast. Dan. 5. 1.",
        "content": "<p>Belshazzar the king made a great feast. Dan. 5. 1.</p><p>There was one Guest not invited, but He came, and the work of His finger glowed upon the wall.— Selected.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Daniel 5:1"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 321,
        "title": "He that watereth shall be watered also himself. Prov. 11. 25.",
        "content": "<p>He that waters shall be watered also himself. Prov. 11. 25.</p><p>The effective life and the receptive life are one. No sweep of arm that does some work for God but harvests also some more of the truth of God, and sweeps it into the treasury of life.— Phillips Brooks.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Proverbs 11:25"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 322,
        "title": "They came to Him, bringing a man who was paralyzed. Mark 2. 3.",
        "content": "<p>And they came to Him, bringing one sick of the palsy. Mark 2. 3.</p><p><strong>Source and historical context:</strong> An unattributed one-sentence selection reflects on the man carried to Jesus in Mark 2.</p><p><strong>Devotional focus:</strong> Mark 2 celebrates the friends who bring a paralyzed man to Jesus and Jesus’ response to him. The story does not require us to call the man’s disability a blessing or presume he could not have known Christ otherwise. People with disabilities are fully worthy of community and care. We can follow the friends’ example by removing barriers and listening to what a person actually needs.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> What practical barrier is preventing someone from participating fully?</p><p><strong>Practice:</strong> Ask how to improve accessibility or offer assistance without assuming what another person wants.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Jesus, teach us to welcome each person with dignity and practical love.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Mark 2:3"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 323,
        "title": "Bless the Lord, O my soul, and do not forget His benefits . . . He crowns you with loving kindness and tender mercies. Ps. 103. 2, 4.",
        "content": "<p><strong>Read:</strong> Psalms 103:2; Psalms 103:4</p><p><strong>Source and historical context:</strong> A historic reflection on Psalm 103 praises divine care in times of trial.</p><p><strong>Devotional focus:</strong> Psalm 103 recalls God’s compassion, forgiveness, and care for frail human beings. We may be sustained during hardship, but that does not mean people should prefer danger, illness, or deprivation to safety and healing. Both gratitude and honest requests for relief belong in prayer. The community can mirror divine compassion through accessible care and practical help.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> Am I valuing an interpretation of suffering more than a person’s need for relief?</p><p><strong>Practice:</strong> Choose one concrete action to make a difficult situation safer or less burdensome.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Merciful God, teach me gratitude that never withholds compassion.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Psalms 103:2",
          "Psalms 103:4"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 324,
        "title": "Then you will discern between the righteous and the wicked. Mal. 3. 18.",
        "content": "<p>Then shall you discern between the righteous and the wicked. Mal. 3. 18.</p><p>Said Anne of Austria to Cardinal Richelieu: \"God does not pay at the end of every week, but He pays at last!\"— Selected.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Malachi 3:18"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 325,
        "title": "What is your life? It is even a vapor, that appeared for a little time, and then vanisheth away. James 4. 14.",
        "content": "<p>What is your life? It is even a vapor, that appeared for a little time, and then vanishes away. James 4. 14.</p><p>Editorial reflection: James reminds us that life is brief and uncertain. That truth need not lead to fear; it can bring our attention back to the people and work God has placed before us. Give this day to Christ, and do one good thing that should not wait for tomorrow.</p>",
        "references": [
          "James 4:14"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 326,
        "title": "He (Jesus) . . . looked up to heaven. Mark 6. 41.",
        "content": "<p>He (Jesus) . . . looked up to heaven. Mark 6. 41.</p><p>In working for God, first look to heaven. It is a grand plan. Over and over again our Lord Jesus Christ looked to heaven and said, \"Father.\" Let us imitate Him; although standing on the earth, let us have our conversation in heaven. Before you go out, if you would feed the world, if you would be a blessing in the midst of spiritual dearth and famine, lift up your head to heaven. Then your very face will shine, your very garments will smell of myrrh and aloes and cassia out of the ivory palaces where you have been with your God and Savior. There will be stamped upon you the dignity and power of the service of the Most High God.— McNeil.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Mark 6:41"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 327,
        "title": "The disciples were called Christians first in Antioch. Acts 11. 26.",
        "content": "<p>The disciples were called Christians first in Antioch. Acts 11. 26.</p><p>This name suggests that the clear impression made by our character, as well as by our words, should be that we belong to Jesus Christ. He should manifestly be the center and the guide, the impulse and the pattern, the strength and reward, of our lives. We are Christians. That should be plain for all folks to see, whether we speak or be silent.</p><p>Is it so with you?— Alex. McLaren.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Acts 11:26"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 328,
        "title": "Having therefore these promises. 2 Cor. 7. 1.",
        "content": "<p>Having therefore these promises. 2 Cor. 7. 1.</p><p>The forests in summer days are full of birds' nests. They are hidden among the leaves. The little birds know where they are; and when a storm arises, or when night draws on, they fly, each to his own nest. So the promises of God are hidden in the Bible, like nests in the great forests; and there we should fly in any danger or alarm, hiding there in our soul's nest until the storm be overpast. There are no castles in this world so impregnable as the words of Christ.— J. R. Miller.</p>",
        "references": [
          "2 Corinthians 7:1"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 329,
        "title": "Faith, hope, and love remain, these three; but the greatest of these is love. 1 Cor. 13. 13. (R. V.)",
        "content": "<p>Now abides faith, hope, love, these three; but the greatest of these is love. 1 Cor. 13. 13. (R. V.)</p><p>Love is the greatest thing that God can give us: for Himself is Love; and it is the greatest thing we can give to God: for it will give ourselves, and carry with it all that is ours.— Jeremy Taylor.</p>",
        "references": [
          "1 Corinthians 13:13"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 330,
        "title": "He (Thomas) . . . said, Except I shall see . . . I will not believe. . . . Jesus . . . said . . . Be not faithless, but believing. John 20. 25, 27.",
        "content": "<p>He (Thomas) . . . said, Except I shall see . . . I will not believe. . . . Jesus . . . said . . . Be not faithless, but believing. John 20. 25, 27.</p><p><strong>Source and historical context:</strong> The unattributed selection condemns Thomas’s hesitation.</p><p><strong>Devotional focus:</strong> In John 20, Thomas asks for evidence; Jesus meets him and invites him to see. The passage encourages faith without teaching that every question is a betrayal. Honest inquiry can belong to mature discipleship. People who have been hurt by religious authorities may need time, evidence, safety, and patient friendship. A trustworthy Christian community welcomes serious questions rather than punishing them.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> What sincere question have I been reluctant to voice?</p><p><strong>Practice:</strong> Ask your question respectfully of someone who can respond without pressure or shame.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Risen Jesus, meet us with patience and lead us toward truth.</p>",
        "references": [
          "John 20:25",
          "John 20:27"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 331,
        "title": "Lot . . . pitched his tent toward Sodom. Gen. 13. 12.",
        "content": "<p>Lot . . . pitched his tent toward Sodom. Gen. 13. 12.</p><p>And soon Lot moved into Sodom; and before long Sodom moved into him.— Theodore Cuyler.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Genesis 13:12"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 332,
        "title": "Cleanse me from hidden faults. Psa. 19. 12.",
        "content": "<p>Cleanse me from hidden faults. Psa. 19. 12.</p><p>The world needs men who are free from secret faults. Most men are free from gross, public faults.— Selected.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Psalms 19:12"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 333,
        "title": "A hearer of the word . . . a doer of the work. Jas. 1. 23, 25.",
        "content": "<p>A hearer of the word . . . a doer of the work. Jas. 1. 23, 25.</p><p>Religion may be learned on Sunday, but it is lived in the week-day's work. The torch of religion may be lit in the church, but it does its burning in the shop and on the street. Religion seeks its life in prayer, but it lives its life in deeds. It is planted in the closet, but it does its growing out in the world. It plumes itself for flight in songs of praise, but its actual flights are in works of love. It resolves and meditates on faithfulness as it reads its Christian lesson in the Book of Truth, but \"faithful is that faithful does.\" It puts its armor on in all the aids and helps of the sanctuary as its dressing-room, but it combats for the right, the noble, and the good in all the activities of practical existence, and its battle ground is the whole broad field of life.— John Doughty.</p>",
        "references": [
          "James 1:23",
          "James 1:25"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 334,
        "title": "You do not know what tomorrow will bring. James 4. 14.",
        "content": "<p>You know not what shall be on the morrow. James 4. 14.</p><p>\"Tomorrow\" is the devil's great ally—the very Goliath in whom he trusts for victory. \"Now\" is the stripling sent forth against him. . . . The world will freely agree to be Christians tomorrow if Christ will permit them to be worldly today.— William Arnot.</p>",
        "references": [
          "James 4:14"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 335,
        "title": "The sea wrought, and was tempestuous. Jonah 1. 11.",
        "content": "<p>The sea wrought, and was tempestuous. Jonah 1. 11.</p><p><strong>Source and historical context:</strong> An unattributed saying equates inner wrongdoing with a literal sea storm.</p><p><strong>Devotional focus:</strong> Jonah is a particular story of prophetic resistance and a storm at sea. It is not a formula for attributing accidents, illness, or other people’s trouble to someone’s secret sin. Such accusations cause real harm. The book also tells of God’s mercy to Jonah and to Nineveh. We can examine our choices honestly without blaming victims for what they suffer.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> Have I treated another person’s hardship as proof that someone must have sinned?</p><p><strong>Practice:</strong> Reject an unverified spiritual accusation and offer practical help instead.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Merciful God, make us accountable for our actions and gentle toward people in distress.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Jonah 1:11"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 336,
        "title": "Be not doubtful, but followers of them also, through faith and patience, inherit the promises. Heb. 6. 12.",
        "content": "<p>Be not doubtful, but followers of them also, through faith and patience, inherit the promises. Heb. 6. 12.</p><p>God makes a promise. Faith believes it. Hope anticipates it. Patience quietly awaits it.— Selected.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Hebrews 6:12"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 337,
        "title": "Go and sit down in the lowest room. Luke 14. 10.",
        "content": "<p>Go and sit down in the lowest room. Luke 14. 10.</p><p>He who is willing to take the lowest place will always find sitting room; there is no great crush for the worst places. There is nothing like the jostling at the back there is at the front; so if we would be comfortable, we shall do well to keep behind.— Thomas Champness.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Luke 14:10"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 338,
        "title": "Continue in prayer. Col. 4. 2.",
        "content": "<p>Continue in prayer. Col. 4. 2.</p><p>Our prayers often resemble the mischievous tricks of town children, who knock at their neighbor's houses and then run away; we often knock at heaven's door and then run off into the spirit of the world; instead of waiting for entrance and answer, we act as if we were afraid of having our prayers answered.— Williams.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Colossians 4:2"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 339,
        "title": "A multitude of the heavenly host praising God, and saying, Glory to God in the highest. Luke 2. 13, 14.",
        "content": "<p>A multitude of the heavenly host praising God, and saying, Glory to God in the highest. Luke 2. 13, 14.</p><p>Angels had been present on many august occasions, and they had joined in many a solemn chorus to the praise of their Almighty Creator. They were present at the creation: \"The morning stars sang together, and all the sons of God shouted for joy.\" They had seen many a planet fashioned between the palms of Jehovah, and wheeled by His eternal hands through the infinitude of space. They had sung solemn songs over many a world which the Great One had created. We doubt not, they had often chanted, \"Blessing and honor, and glory, and majesty, and power, and dominion, and might, be to Him that sits on the throne,\" manifesting Himself in the work of creation. I doubt not, too, that their songs had gathered force through ages. As when first created, their first breath was song, so when they saw God create new worlds, then their song received another note; they rose a little higher in the gamut of adoration. But this time, when they saw God stoop from His throne and become a babe hanging upon a woman's breast, they lifted their notes higher still; and reaching to the uttermost stretch of angelic music, they gained the highest notes of the divine scale of praise and they sang, \"Glory to God in the highest ,\" for higher in goodness they felt God could not go. Thus their highest praise they gave to Him in the highest act of His Godhead.— Spurgeon.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Luke 2:13-14"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 340,
        "title": "God forbid that I should glory save in the cross of our Lord Jesus Christ. Gal. 6. 14.",
        "content": "<p>God forbid that I should glory save in the cross of our Lord Jesus Christ. Gal. 6. 14.</p><p>The cross is the great center of God's moral universe! To this center God ever pointed, and the eye of faith ever looked forward, until the Savior came. And now we must ever turn to that cross as the center of all our blessing, and the basis of all our worship, both on earth and in heaven—in time and throughout all eternity.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Galatians 6:14"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 341,
        "title": "He ever liveth. Heb. 7. 25.",
        "content": "<p>He ever lives. Heb. 7. 25.</p><p>It is our hope for ourselves, and for His truth, and for mankind. Men come and go. Leaders, teachers, thinkers, speak and work for a season, and then fall silent and impotent. He abides. They die, but He lives. They are lights kindled, and therefore, sooner or later quenched, but He is the true Light from which they draw all their brightness, and He shines forever.— Alex. McLaren.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Hebrews 7:25"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 342,
        "title": "The friendship of the world is enmity with God. James 4. 4.",
        "content": "<p>The friendship of the world is enmity with God. James 4. 4.</p><p>It is like the ivy with the oak. The ivy may give the oak a grand, beautiful appearance, but all the while it is feeding on its vitals. Are we compromising with the enemies of God? Are we being embraced by the world by its honors, its pleasures, its applause? This may add to us in the world's estimation, but our strength becomes lost.— Denham Smith.</p>",
        "references": [
          "James 4:4"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 343,
        "title": "Hannah . . . prayed to the Lord and wept bitterly . . . she spoke in her heart. 1 Sam. 1. 10, 13.",
        "content": "<p>She (Hannah) . . . prayed to the Lord, and wept sore . . . she spoke in her heart. 1 Sam. 1. 10, 13.</p><p>For real business at the mercy-seat give me a home-made prayer, a prayer that comes out of the depths of my heart, not because I invented it, but because God the Holy Spirit, put it there, and gave it such living force that I could not help letting it out. Though your words are broken, and your sentences disconnected, if your desires are earnest, if they are like coals of juniper, burning with a vehement flame, God will not mind how they find expression. If you have no words, perhaps you will pray better without them than with them. There are prayers that break the backs of words; they are too heavy for any human language to carry.— Spurgeon.</p>",
        "references": [
          "1 Samuel 1:10",
          "1 Samuel 1:13"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 344,
        "title": "Noah found grace in the eyes of the Lord. Gen. 6. 8.",
        "content": "<p>Noah found grace in the eyes of the Lord. Gen. 6. 8.</p><p>Editorial reflection: Noah received grace before the long work of building and waiting was complete. God's favor is a gift, not a reward for having every answer. When the next step feels slow or lonely, ask for grace to obey faithfully today and trust God with what follows.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Genesis 6:8"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 345,
        "title": "Mercy Takes Hold",
        "content": "<p>Noah found grace in the same way that Paul obtained mercy (1 Tim. 1: 16), namely, by mercy's taking hold of him.— Selected.</p>",
        "references": [
          "1 Timothy 1:16"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 346,
        "title": "Which hope we have as an anchor to the soul. Heb. 6. 19.",
        "content": "<p>Which hope we have as an anchor to the soul. Heb. 6. 19.</p><p>Anchor to the throne of God, and then shorten the rope!— Selected.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Hebrews 6:19"
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "wave": 43,
    "plan_id": "90000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000102",
    "title": "Morning and Evening: 366 Scripture-Guided Devotions",
    "description": "A 366-day morning-and-evening Scripture plan adapted from the verse calendar of Charles H. Spurgeon's Morning and Evening. Every one of the 732 positions retains its historical Scripture heading and now provides a clearly labeled, newly written contemporary reflection, practice, and prayer. The adaptation has been screened for Nicene compatibility, non-coercive pastoral language, modern English, disability and cultural dignity, and consistent two-reference metadata.",
    "source_url": "https://ccel.org/ccel/spurgeon/morneve/morneve.d0101am.html",
    "total_days": 366,
    "days": [
      {
        "day_number": 1,
        "title": "January 1",
        "content": "<p>Morning</p>\n<p><em>“They did eat of the fruit of the land of Canaan that year.”</em></p>\n<p>Joshua 5:12</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial morning reflection:</strong> Faith is not denial of evidence, pain, or honest questions. It is a growing reliance on God's character that can seek wisdom, receive help, and take the next faithful step without pretending certainty we do not have.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more before the day gathers pace. Name one concrete way its truth can shape your attention, speech, or action today.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Trustworthy God, hold me amid uncertainty and guide my next step in wisdom, courage, and love. Amen.</p>\n<p>Evening</p>\n<p><em>“We will be glad and rejoice in you.”</em></p>\n<p>Song of Solomon 1:4</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial evening reflection:</strong> Worship turns attention toward the triune God and reorders desire around truth, beauty, justice, and love. Praise need not hide grief; thanksgiving and lament can both be faithful speech before God.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more and review the day without denial or self-contempt. Give thanks, name what needs repair, and entrust tomorrow to God.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Father, Son, and Holy Spirit, receive my worship and make my whole life an offering of gratitude, truth, and love. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
