      {
        "day_number": 85,
        "title": "March 25",
        "content": "<p>Morning</p>\n<p><em>“Do you betray the Son of Man with a kiss?”</em></p>\n<p>Luke 22:48</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial morning reflection:</strong> Judas's kiss concealed betrayal beneath a sign of friendship. Spurgeon's original application adds gendered stereotypes, condemning labels for theological opponents, and threats of final punishment; those elements are omitted. Jesus' question instead invites honest self-examination. Let public worship and private conduct tell the same truth. Where you have broken trust, confess it, protect anyone who has been harmed, and take concrete steps toward repair.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more before the day gathers pace. Name one concrete way its truth can shape your attention, speech, or action today.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Lord Jesus Christ, true God and true human, draw me into your life and teach me to follow you in love. Amen.</p>\n<p>Evening</p>\n<p><em>“The Son of man.”</em></p>\n<p>John 3:13</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial evening reflection:</strong> Jesus often calls himself the Son of Man, a title that expresses both his solidarity with humanity and the authority described in Daniel 7. Christians confess the one Lord Jesus Christ as the eternal Son, fully divine and fully human, distinct from the Father yet one with the Father in divine being. Because the Son truly shared our human life, we may bring him grief, temptation, and need without fear. Receive his compassion and practice the same humility toward others.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more and review the day without denial or self-contempt. Give thanks, name what needs repair, and entrust tomorrow to God.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Lord Jesus Christ, true God and true human, draw me into your life and teach me to follow you in love. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Luke 22:48",
          "John 3:13"
        ]
      },
