        "day_number": 101,
        "title": "April 10",
        "content": "<p>Morning</p>\n<p><em>“The place which is called Calvary.”</em></p>\n<p>Luke 23:33</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial morning reflection:</strong> The cross reveals Christ's self-giving love and God's refusal to abandon a wounded world. Receive forgiveness as grace, name harm truthfully, and let reconciliation include accountability and repair.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more before the day gathers pace. Name one concrete way its truth can shape your attention, speech, or action today.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Crucified and risen Lord, forgive me, free me from sin, and make your self-giving love visible in my life. Amen.</p>\n<p>Evening</p>\n<p><em>“For there stood by me this night the angel of God.”</em></p>\n<p>Acts 27:23</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial evening reflection:</strong> During the storm in Acts 27, Paul tells the whole ship's company that God's messenger has reassured him. Spurgeon's original reflection heightens Paul's courage by contrasting a Jewish prisoner with Roman soldiers and sailors, and it promises angelic visits in distress; the ethnic comparison and guarantee are omitted. God may sustain people through Scripture, prayer, skilled helpers, neighbors, and unexpected courage. In crisis, receive sound help and speak hope that serves everyone at risk.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more and review the day without denial or self-contempt. Give thanks, name what needs repair, and entrust tomorrow to God.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Holy Spirit, giver of life, guide me into truth and grow in me the fruit of Christlike love. Amen.</p>",
