        "content": "<p>Morning</p>\n<p><em>“They gathered manna every morning.”</em></p>\n<p>Exodus 16:21</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial morning reflection:</strong> Faith is not denial of evidence, pain, or honest questions. It is a growing reliance on God's character that can seek wisdom, receive help, and take the next faithful step without pretending certainty we do not have.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more before the day gathers pace. Name one concrete way its truth can shape your attention, speech, or action today.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Trustworthy God, hold me amid uncertainty and guide my next step in wisdom, courage, and love. Amen.</p>\n<p>Evening</p>\n<p><em>“You will arise, and have mercy upon Zion: for the time to favour her, yes, the set time is come. For your servants take pleasure in her stones, and favour the dust of it.”</em></p>\n<p>Psalm 102:13,14</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial evening reflection:</strong> God's mercy is gift, not payment for achievement or respectability. Receive grace without self-contempt, extend it without favoritism, and remember that forgiveness never makes another person's suffering unimportant.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more and review the day without denial or self-contempt. Give thanks, name what needs repair, and entrust tomorrow to God.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Merciful God, let your grace free me from shame and pride, and make me generous in forgiveness, truth, and repair. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Exodus 16:21",
          "Psalm 102:13,14"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 199,
        "title": "July 17",
        "content": "<p>Morning</p>\n<p><em>“Knowing, brethren beloved, your election of God.”</em></p>\n<p>1 Thessalonians 1:4</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial morning reflection:</strong> Love of God takes shape in patient, truthful care for neighbors, family, strangers, and the church. Healthy love respects conscience and boundaries; it does not excuse harm or demand silence from those who suffer.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more before the day gathers pace. Name one concrete way its truth can shape your attention, speech, or action today.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> God of communion, teach me to love with truth, patience, courage, and respect for every person's dignity. Amen.</p>\n<p>Evening</p>\n<p><em>“Let not one of them escape.”</em></p>\n<p>1 Kings 18:40</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial evening reflection:</strong> Elijah's confrontation with the prophets of Baal takes place within an ancient narrative that includes lethal religious violence (1 Kings 18). It is not an example for Christians to reenact against people or groups. The devotional application is about turning away from habits that damage us and others, not attacking human beings. In Colossians 3:5–14, Paul pairs the putting away of destructive conduct with compassion, humility, and love. Name one harmful pattern you need help to change, and seek support without shame or violence.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more and review the day without denial or self-contempt. Give thanks, name what needs repair, and entrust tomorrow to God.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Trustworthy God, hold me amid uncertainty and guide my next step in wisdom, courage, and love. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "1 Thessalonians 1:4",
          "1 Kings 18:40"
        ]
      },
